| 1 | 9 | Telling it out of order. *How does a writer move a story through time, and how do we tell one event two ways?* | 24 | g9-la-narrate; g9-si-narrate | | ELA grade 9 unit 1 (days 1 to 22), Traps and bargains: its four suspense stories and their structure across time (e9-rl-structure-time), class discussion (e9-sl-discussion); ELA grade 9 unit 2 (days 23 to 50), the Odyssey, opens its narrative writing (e9-w-narrative) on day 23. ELD days 1 to 24, weeks 1 to 5. Narrate | LA Narrate in both modes (WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Narrate.Interpretive and WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Narrate.Expressive, carried; owned in grade 12): students take apart one passage of "The Cask of Amontillado" (1846), told by a narrator looking back on what he did, and mark the tense shifts that move between the telling and the past, then build a narrative scene with one time shift, with the teacher first and then alone. SI Narrate (WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Narrate, carried; owned in grade 4): recount one event (orientation day, a team meeting) for a peer and then for an adult, and say what changed. Retrieval on day 2: grade 8's newsletter recount and its story map (g8-la-narrate, g8-si-narrate). Forms: text, a scene with one time shift, and one recount told twice; sentence, past and past perfect against the narrator's present ("Even now, ___. Back then, ___."), reflective "looking back, I"; word, time shift, back then, even now, during, the group discussed. Model: the story map at the 9 to 12 stage, two time lines, introduced. The "point, build, ask" routine is taught in week 1. Entry points as the rows (story map, audio of the passage, word bank and cloze; tense-shift frames; story map only, shaping the shift and naming its effect). Change of audience: the event told to a peer, then to an adult. Talk about language: what "had" does to time. End-of-unit assessment (two days): read and hear a new short passage with a flashback (Curio Class's own, invented for practice) and mark where time shifts; recount a new event in a small seminar for a named adult; write a scene with one shift; then a respond day. If later: ELA's narrative writing lessons start day 23, so ELD's scene uses its own frame and model, and the Odyssey (whose books 9 to 12 are one long flashback) is not used. Out: the full narrative and the reading lessons (ELA). Tools: audio of the passage (paper path: the teacher reads it twice). Curio Class writes the frames, the model scene and the new passage | First because ELA grade 9 opens with stories built on what the reader learns when, and recounting a first school week to two listeners is the year's first talk. The routine and the 9 to 12 story map start here | Candidate: California 1.26, all four domains in the unit; PRIME Match.LE, one interpretive item on the new passage | The host stories hold murder and a son's death ("The Monkey's Paw"): the family note previews loss and how to ask for another story, and ELD uses the Poe passage for its structure, not its crime. Own-story prompts follow APPROACH "Sensitive ground": an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed. Informal English and the home language are not wrong; the task is choosing for the listener. Agent reads: sensitivity; spanish (family note) |
| 2 | 9 | Code and energy. *How does a cell's structure explain what it does?* | 18 | g9-sc-explain; g9-si-explain | | Biology unit 3 (days 26 to 43), Where the mass goes: photosynthesis stores energy (bio-photosynthesis-energy), alongside; Biology unit 2 (days 14 to 25), DNA codes for proteins (bio-dna-to-protein), retrieved; ELA grade 9 unit 1 (days 1 to 22) discussion turns (e9-sl-discussion), retrieved. ELD days 25 to 42, weeks 5 to 9. Explain | SC Explain in both modes (WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Explain.Interpretive and WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Explain.Expressive, carried; owned in grade 12): students read a short labeled explanation from the biology pages and trace how a structure leads to a function (the order of DNA bases to a protein; chloroplasts to stored sugar), then explain one mechanism on a cause chain, citing the class's own seedling data and naming one limit. SI Explain (WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Explain, carried; owned in grade 4): explain one's own reasoning in seminar ("My reasoning was that ___, which suggests ___") and ask a partner what led to theirs. Retrieval on day 2: grade 8's cause chain (g8-sc-explain, g8-si-explain), and the DNA model of Biology unit 2. Forms: text, an explanation that links structure to function in steps; sentence, cause verbs (codes for, results in) and hedging (may, suggests), dense noun groups ("the sequence of bases in the gene"); word, structure, function, codes for. Model: the cause chain at the 9 to 12 stage, which cause counts most, introduced. Change of audience: the mechanism explained to a classmate, then written as a caption for a hallway poster. End-of-unit assessment (two days): read a new labeled diagram of a cell process not studied (Curio Class's own) and order its steps; explain one step in a seminar; write the explanation; then a respond day. Out: the investigation and the biology itself (Biology). Curio Class writes the labeled diagrams at three levels and the new diagram | Alongside biology's photosynthesis unit, with DNA just taught and retrieved. Explain comes second because a reasoning turn in seminar is the talk move every later unit uses | Candidate: PRIME Depth.ELD, a cause verb taught as a feature with a job; bar item 4, asking a partner to explain | Home language: proteA-na and protein, estructura and structure are cognates. No task asks about a student's own body or health. Agent reads: sensitivity |
