si,K,k-si-narrate,~WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Narrate,,g1-si-narrate,,story map,first; then; at the end; yesterday; today; we went,ela:ek-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:ek-describe-aloud (telling and describing aloud),"Students tell a partner about a class event (the morning walk, a class visitor) in two pictured steps, then listen and say back one thing the partner did. Entry points (all tell a partner about one class event in two steps and say back one thing the partner told): levels 1 and 2 with two photo cards of the event to point to, a picture word bank and the cloze ""First we ___. Then we ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with the photo cards and ""First ___. Then ___. It was ___."", taken off before the end-of-unit telling; 5 and 6 with the photo cards only, adding a feeling word, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a two-step telling that opens with ""Today we""; past tense of common verbs (went, saw, made) joined with ""and then""; the time words first, then, yesterday and today. Model: story map at the K to 1 stage, three pictures in a row; in kindergarten students fill two. Home language: a partner who shares the student's home language may rehearse the telling with them in it first, then both tell it in English; no student translates for another. Carried, owned in grade 3. In kindergarten a telling is two pictured steps about something the whole class saw, so no one needs home or family details; by grade 3, the owner year, the photo cards are gone for levels 3 and 4. Builds on nothing earlier. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."
si,K,k-si-inform,~WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Inform,,g1-si-inform,,text map,this is; it has; we use it to; color; big; small,ela:ek-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:ek-describe-aloud (telling and describing aloud),"Students name and describe a classroom object or a center routine to a partner (what it is, its color, what we use it for) and follow a one-step direction. Entry points (all describe one classroom object so a partner can find it, then follow one direction back): levels 1 and 2 with the real object to hold, a picture word bank of colors and sizes and the cloze ""This is a ___. It is ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""This is ___. It has ___. We use it to ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the object hidden in a bag so the words must carry it, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a describing turn that names the thing first and then tells about it; ""This is / it has / we use it to""; color and size words and picture labels. Model: text map at the K to 1 stage, the topic as a picture with two facts beside it. Home language: students may name the object in the home language first and then in English; the teacher points out a cognate where one exists (color is spelled the same in Spanish and English). Carried, owned in grade 3. In kindergarten describing stays with things on the table, which every student can see and point to; by grade 3 the object is not in the room. Builds on nothing earlier."
si,K,k-si-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Explain,,g1-si-explain,,cause chain,how; why; because; so; first; wash,ela:ek-discussion (class discussion rules and turns),"Students say how to do a class job (washing hands, cleaning up blocks) and why we do it, acting out the steps with a partner. Entry points (all act out a class job with a partner and say how to do it and why): levels 1 and 2 with photo step cards to act out, a picture word bank and the cloze ""First ___. Then ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with the step cards and ""First ___, then ___, because ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the step cards only, joining two reasons with ""and"" and ""so"", the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a how-to in two or three steps with the reason last; ""When we ___, ___"" and ""because""; how, why and so. Model: cause chain at the K to 1 stage, two linked boxes (what we do, what happens). Home language: partners may act out and talk through the steps in the home language first; the actions carry the meaning, so no one has to translate. Carried, owned in grade 3. In kindergarten each job has two or three steps the class does every day, so the language rides on a routine students already know; by grade 3 the steps are a problem the group solved, not a routine. Builds on nothing earlier."
si,K,k-si-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Argue,,g1-si-argue; g1-ss-argue,,claim chart,I think; I like; because; agree; disagree; vote,"ela:ek-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:ek-opinion (opinion with a reason, no ELD-LA Argue id before 4-5)","Students say which book, game or picture they like and why, vote, and hear that others chose differently. Entry points (all choose between two books or games, give a reason, vote and hear a different choice): levels 1 and 2 with both choices pictured on the vote chart, a picture word bank of reasons (fun, funny, pretty) and the cloze ""I like ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""I think ___ because ___."" and ""I agree"" or ""I disagree"" cards, off before the end-of-unit vote; 5 and 6 with the vote chart only, giving two reasons and answering a friend who chose the other, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a turn that gives the choice and then the reason; ""I think ___ because ___"" and ""I agree with ___ because""; like, best and favorite. Model: claim chart at the K to 1 stage, a picture vote chart with the choice and one reason. Home language: a student may give the reason in the home language first and the teacher then models it in English to say back; a reason in any language counts as a reason. Carried, owned in grade 3. In kindergarten the choices are two things in the room, and the vote shows that people can choose differently; by grade 3 the group must reach one decision together. Builds on nothing earlier."
la,K,k-la-narrate,WIDA.ELD-LA.K.Narrate.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.K.Narrate.Expressive,,g1-la-narrate,story map,story map,story; character; first; next; at the end; happy; sad,"ela:ek-narrate (narrative writing); ela:ek-retell (reading literature, same text)","Students hear the ELA unit's read-aloud story again and take apart its opening and its ending: who the character is, and the time words that move the story on. They retell it in three parts on a story map with a partner, then tell their own short story with a character, with the teacher first and then with a partner. Reading the story and writing about it stay in ELA. Entry points (all retell the read-aloud in three parts and tell an own story with a character): levels 1 and 2 with three picture cards to order, a picture word bank and the cloze ""First ___. Next ___. At the end ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with the story map and ""First ___. Next ___. At the end ___. The character felt ___."", off before the end-of-unit telling; 5 and 6 with the story map only, adding a feeling word and a ""because"" to one part, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a story in three parts (first, next, at the end); past tense of common verbs (ran, saw, said) and ""and then""; the time words first, next and at the end, and feeling words happy and sad. Model: story map at the K to 1 stage, three pictures in a row. Home language: a student may tell their own story in the home language first, to a partner who shares it or to a family member at home, then tell it in English; a story in any language is a story. Owner year for LA K. The K expectations in both modes: retelling a heard story and telling one in order. Builds on nothing earlier. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."
la,K,k-la-inform,WIDA.ELD-LA.K.Inform.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.K.Inform.Expressive,,g1-la-inform,text map,text map,topic; fact; it has; it can; label,"ela:ek-inform (informative writing); ela:ek-main-topic (reading informational, same text); ela:ek-sentences (forms of English)","Students hear an information book from the ELA unit again and take apart one page: the topic word and how the page tells two facts about it (""It has"", ""It can""). They say the topic and two facts, then draw and label their own all-about page with the teacher first and then alone. The reading and writing lessons stay in ELA. Entry points (all say an information book's topic and two facts and make a labeled all-about page): levels 1 and 2 with the book's pictures to point to, a picture word bank and the cloze ""It is about ___. It has ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with the text map and ""This book is about ___. It has ___. It can ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, telling a fact the picture does not show, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a page with a topic and two facts; ""It is / it has / it can""; labels and the words topic and fact. Model: text map at the K to 1 stage, the topic and its facts. Home language: a student may label the page in the home language beside English if they choose; the teacher names cognates in the book's topic words (animal and animal, planeta and planet). Owner year for LA K. The K expectations in both modes: topic and facts heard and told. Builds on nothing earlier."
ma,K,k-ma-inform,WIDA.ELD-MA.K.Inform.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-MA.K.Inform.Expressive,,g1-ma-inform,text map,text map,more; fewer; same; circle; triangle; side; corner,math:mk-compare-groups (comparing groups); math:mk-analyze-shapes (describing shapes),"Students describe and compare groups and shapes from the math unit (more, fewer, the same; sides and corners) and tell a partner how they sorted a set. Entry points (all describe and compare groups and shapes from the math unit and tell a partner how they sorted a set): levels 1 and 2 with the real objects to sort and point to, a picture word bank and the cloze ""This has ___. It is a ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""This has ___ sides and ___ corners."" and ""___ has more than ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the shapes hidden behind a folder, describing one so a partner can find it, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a describing turn that names the group or shape and then tells about it; ""This has / it is / there are more""; number and shape words, more, fewer and the same. Model: text map at the K to 1 stage, the topic (a shape or group) and two facts. Home language: a student may count and name shapes in the home language first; cA-rculo and circle, triA�ngulo and triangle are cognates. Owner year for MA K. The K expectations in both modes: hearing and giving descriptions of numbers and shapes. Builds on nothing earlier."
sc,K,k-sc-inform,WIDA.ELD-SC.K.Inform.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SC.K.Inform.Expressive,,g1-sc-inform,text map,text map,plant; animal; need; water; food; sunny; rainy; windy,science:sk-living-needs (host investigation); science:sk-weather-watch (host investigation),"Students describe what plants and animals need and what the weather is each day in the science unit, and keep a picture weather chart they talk through. Entry points (all describe what a plant or animal needs and what the weather is today, from the class weather chart): levels 1 and 2 with picture cards to match, the picture weather chart, a word bank and the cloze ""It is ___ today. A plant needs ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Today it is ___. Yesterday it was ___. ___ needs ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the chart only, comparing two days or two living things, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a short report on today's weather or a living thing's needs; ""It is / it needs / they have""; weather words and labels on the chart. Model: text map at the K to 1 stage, the topic and its facts. Home language: a student may name the weather in the home language first; planta and plant, animal and animal are cognates. Owner year for SC K. The K expectations in both modes: hearing and giving descriptions of living things and weather. Builds on nothing earlier."
sc,K,k-sc-explain,WIDA.ELD-SC.K.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SC.K.Explain.Expressive,,g1-sc-explain,cause chain,cause chain,push; pull; fast; slow; harder; warm; because,science:sk-push-pull (host investigation); science:sk-sun-warms (host investigation),"Students say what happens when they push or pull harder and why the sun warms a surface, after testing it in the science unit. Entry points (all say what happens when they push or pull harder and why the sun warms a surface, after testing it): levels 1 and 2 with the test to act out again, photo cards, a word bank and the cloze ""I pushed ___. It went ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""When I push harder, it ___."" and ""The sun makes it ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the photo cards only, saying what happened and why with ""because"", the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a short explanation, what we did and what happened; ""When ___, ___"" and ""because""; push, pull, fast and slow. Model: cause chain at the K to 1 stage, two linked boxes (what we did, what happened). Home language: a student may say what happened in the home language first while acting it out; rA�pido and rapid are cognates, and the teacher pairs rapid with fast. Owner year for SC K. The K expectations in both modes, all parts: hearing and saying the question the class tested or the problem it tried to solve (""What if we push harder?"", ""How can we keep the slide cool?""), using what was seen to say how or why it happened, telling what was seen, linking what we did to what happened, and comparing two ways the class tried (one hand or two; shade from a tree or from a sheet) to say which worked better, all from the host's own records. Builds on nothing earlier."
ss,K,k-ss-inform,WIDA.ELD-SS.K.Inform.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SS.K.Inform.Expressive,,g1-ss-inform; g1-ss-argue,text map,text map,helper; job; works at; helps; goods; community,social-studies:ssk-jobs-helping (host unit); social-studies:ssk-goods-services-money (host unit),"Students describe community helpers and their jobs and goods and services from the social studies unit, and tell a partner what one helper does. Entry points (all describe community helpers and their jobs, and goods and services, from the unit's pictures, and tell a partner what one helper does): levels 1 and 2 with the unit's helper pictures, a picture word bank and the cloze ""This is a ___. A ___ helps ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""A ___ works at ___. A ___ helps people ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the pictures only, telling how two jobs are alike or different, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a describing turn that names the helper and then tells what they do; ""This is / works at / helps""; job and place words and labels. Model: text map at the K to 1 stage, the topic (a helper) and two facts. Home language: a student may name a helper in the home language first; doctor and doctor, policA-a and police are cognates. Owner year for SS K. The K expectations in both modes: hearing and giving descriptions of people and work. Builds on nothing earlier. Teacher note: no task asks what a family member does for work; students describe helpers from the unit's pictures, visitors and class jobs."
si,1,g1-si-narrate,~WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Narrate,k-si-narrate,g2-si-narrate,story map,story map,then; yesterday; next; last; we went; I saw,ela:e1-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e1-describe-present (telling and describing aloud),"Students tell a partner about a class event in three parts with time words and listen to a partner tell theirs, asking one ""what happened next?"" question. Entry points (all tell a class event in three parts and ask a partner ""what happened next?""): levels 1 and 2 with three photo cards, the picture word bank and the cloze ""First ___. Next ___. At the end ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with the cards and ""First ___. Next ___. At the end ___. I felt ___."", off before the end-of-unit telling; 5 and 6 with the cards turned over after one look, adding who was there, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a three-part telling with a beginning, a middle and an end; past tense of common verbs (went, saw, ate, played) and ""and then""; first, next, last and yesterday. Home language: a student may rehearse the telling in the home language with a partner who shares it before telling it in English; the partner is never asked to translate. Carried, owned in grade 3. In grade 1 the telling grows from two steps to three and gains a question back, so the listener has a job too; the two-step cloze of kindergarten is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."
si,1,g1-si-inform,~WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Inform,k-si-inform,g2-si-inform,text map,text map,first; then; get; put; next to; line up,ela:e1-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e1-describe-present (telling and describing aloud),"Students give a partner a two-step direction for a class routine (getting materials, lining up) and follow one back. Entry points (all give a partner a two-step direction for a class routine and follow one back): levels 1 and 2 with photo cards of the two steps, gestures, a picture word bank and the cloze ""First get the ___. Then ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""First ___. Then ___. Put it ___."" and place words on cards, off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with no cards, checking the partner understood (""Show me""), the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a direction in order, the action word first; commands (get, put, line up) and ""This is / it goes""; place words (on, under, next to) and labels. Home language: partners who share a home language may try the direction in it once, then give it in English; gestures and pictures carry the meaning for a student with no partner who shares it. Carried, owned in grade 3. In grade 1 the routine has two steps and a partner checks it back; the kindergarten object is gone, so the words must carry the action."
si,1,g1-si-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Explain,k-si-explain,g2-si-explain; g2-ma-explain; g2-ss-explain,cause chain,cause chain,why; because; so; rule; it helps; turn,ela:e1-discussion (class discussion rules and turns),"Students explain how a class game or center works step by step, and why a rule helps. Entry points (all explain how a class game or center works, step by step, and why one rule helps): levels 1 and 2 with photo step cards, a picture word bank and the cloze ""First ___. Then ___. The rule helps because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with step cards and ""First ___, then ___, so ___."" for the rule, off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cards only, giving two reasons for the rule and answering a partner's ""why?"", the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a how-it-works talk that gives the steps and then the reason for a rule; ""when ___, ___"" and ""because"" joining a rule to what it stops; why, so and rule. Home language: students may talk through the game in the home language with a partner first; the teacher points out cognates in game words where they exist (Spanish regla and English rule share a root but look different, so the picture card carries it). Carried, owned in grade 3. In grade 1 the steps become a game with a rule, and the reason is for the rule, not the step; the kindergarten act-out is gone for levels 3 and 4."
si,1,g1-si-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Argue,k-si-argue,g2-si-argue; g2-ma-argue; g2-sc-argue,claim chart,claim chart,I think; because; I agree with; I disagree; my reason; choose,"ela:e1-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e1-opinion (opinion with a reason, no ELD-LA Argue id before 4-5)","Students give an opinion on a class choice with a reason and say ""I agree"" or ""I disagree"" with a partner. Entry points (all give an opinion on a class choice with a reason and say ""I agree"" or ""I disagree"" with a partner): levels 1 and 2 with the choices pictured on the vote chart, a picture word bank of reasons and the cloze ""I think ___ because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""I agree with ___ because ___."" and ""I disagree because ___."", off before the end-of-unit vote; 5 and 6 with the vote chart only, giving two reasons and answering a partner who disagrees, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a turn that states the choice, gives a reason and answers a partner; ""I think ___ because"" and ""I agree with ___ because""; agree, disagree and because. Home language: a student may think the reason through in the home language and say it in English with the frame; the teacher welcomes a reason said first in the home language. Carried, owned in grade 3. In grade 1 the opinion meets a partner's opinion, so agreeing and disagreeing join the telling; the kindergarten ""I like"" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
la,1,g1-la-narrate,WIDA.ELD-LA.1.Narrate.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.1.Narrate.Expressive,k-la-narrate,g2-la-narrate,story map,story map,character; setting; lesson; then; in the end; learned,"ela:e1-narrative (narrative writing); ela:e1-retell-lesson (reading literature, same text)","Students take apart the ELA unit story's last page: how the writer shows the lesson and closes the story. They retell the story with its character, setting and lesson, then build a two- or three-event story with time words and a closing line, with the teacher first and then alone. The full story writing stays in ELA. Entry points (all retell the ELA story with its lesson and tell and write a short story with a closing): levels 1 and 2 with picture cards to sequence, a picture word bank and the cloze ""The story is about ___. The lesson is ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with the story map and ""First ___. Then ___. In the end ___ learned ___."", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, adding a detail to each event and a closing line of their own, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a story with a beginning, events and a closing that gives the lesson; past tense of common verbs and ""and then""; time words (first, then, in the end) and the words setting and lesson. Model: story map at the K to 1 stage, three pictures, now with a closing box for the lesson. Home language: a student may plan the story in the home language first; many folk tales the class reads have versions in other languages, and a family may share one at home by choice. Owner year for LA 1. Both modes at grade 1: a retold story with its lesson and an own story with an ending. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."
la,1,g1-la-inform,WIDA.ELD-LA.1.Inform.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.1.Inform.Expressive,k-la-inform,g2-la-inform,text map,text map,topic; heading; caption; they can; now you know; closing,"ela:e1-informative (informative writing); ela:e1-main-topic (reading informational, same text); ela:e1-sentences (forms of English)","Students take apart one page of the ELA unit text: how a heading names the topic and a caption tells a fact. They find the main topic and facts, then build a fact page with a closing sentence, with the teacher first and then alone. The reading lessons on text features stay in ELA. Entry points (all find an ELA text's topic and facts from its headings and captions and write a fact page with a closing): levels 1 and 2 with the text's pictures and headings to point to, a picture word bank and the cloze ""This is about ___. It has ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with the text map and ""___ are ___. They can ___. Now you know about ___."", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, adding a fact from a caption and a closing of their own, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a fact page with a topic, facts and a closing sentence; ""This is / it has / they can""; heading, caption, label and the closing ""Now you know"". Model: text map at the K to 1 stage, topic and facts, with a closing line added. Home language: a student may write the caption in the home language beside English if they choose; the teacher names cognates in heading words. Owner year for LA 1. Both modes at grade 1: text features read and a fact page with a closing written."
ma,1,g1-ma-inform,WIDA.ELD-MA.1.Inform.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-MA.1.Inform.Expressive,k-ma-inform,g2-ma-explain; g2-ma-argue,text map,text map,sides; corners; most; least; how many more; chart,math:m1-shape-attributes (shape attributes); math:m1-data-three-categories (reading a data chart),"Students describe shapes by their attributes and tell what a three-category data chart shows (most, least, how many more) from the math unit. Entry points (all describe shapes by their attributes and tell what a three-category data chart shows): levels 1 and 2 with the shapes and the chart to point to, a picture word bank and the cloze ""It has ___ sides. ___ has the most."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""A ___ has ___. ___ has ___ more than ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the chart and shapes out of sight, telling a partner how to sort or compare, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a description of a shape or a chart, the topic first and then the facts; ""has / has more than / has the most""; side, corner, most and least. Model: text map at the K to 1 stage, topic and facts, with a fact from a chart. Home language: a student may count and compare in the home language first; the teacher names cognates in data words (categorA-a and category). Owner year for MA 1. Both modes at grade 1: describing attributes and data heard and told."
sc,1,g1-sc-inform,WIDA.ELD-SC.1.Inform.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SC.1.Inform.Expressive,k-sc-inform,g2-sc-explain,text map,text map,parent; young; signal; protect; alike; pattern; sunrise; sunset,"science:s1-parent-offspring (host investigation); science:s1-like-parents (host investigation, earlier); science:s1-sky-patterns (host investigation)","Students describe a pattern of behavior from the science unit that runs alongside, how young animals signal (crying, cheeping, an open mouth) and how their parents respond (feeding, protecting), and the patterns of the sun and moon; how young animals and plants are like their parents is retrieved from the earlier science unit, which comes first in the host order. Entry points (all describe how a young animal signals and its parent responds, and a pattern of the sun or moon): levels 1 and 2 with matching picture cards (a signal and its response, sky pictures), a word bank and the cloze ""When the baby ___, the parent ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""When the young ___ ___, the parent ___. This helps the young ___."" and a pattern frame (""Every day the sun ___.""), off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cards only, adding how the young is like and not like its parent, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a short report on a pattern of behavior or of the sky; ""When ___, ___"", ""every day"" and, retrieved, ""Both ___ have""; signal, protect, pattern and alike. Model: text map at the K to 1 stage, the topic and its facts, now grouped into alike and different. Home language: a student may name the animals in the home language first; the teacher names cognates in animal names where they exist (elefante and elephant). Owner year for SC 1. Both modes at grade 1: describing patterns and likenesses. Teacher note: the lesson stays with animals and plants and never turns to ""How are you like your parents?""; some students live apart from a parent, in foster care or with an adoptive family."
sc,1,g1-sc-explain,WIDA.ELD-SC.1.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SC.1.Explain.Expressive,k-sc-explain,g2-sc-explain; g2-sc-argue,cause chain,cause chain,sound; vibrate; light; dark; shadow; because,science:s1-sound-vibrates (host investigation); science:s1-see-with-light (host investigation),"Students explain that sound comes from vibration and that we see when there is light, after investigating in the science unit. Entry points (all explain that sound comes from vibration and that we see when there is light, after investigating): levels 1 and 2 with the investigation to show again (a drum, a box with a peephole), a word bank and the cloze ""It vibrates. It makes ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""It makes sound because ___."" and ""We can see it when ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, joining an observation to a reason in their own sentence, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an explanation from what we saw to why; ""when ___, ___"" and ""because""; vibrate, light, dark and shadow. Model: cause chain at the K to 1 stage, two linked boxes. Home language: a student may explain in the home language first while showing the test; vibrar and vibrate are cognates. Owner year for SC 1. Both modes at grade 1, all parts: saying the question the class tested or the problem it tried to solve, putting several observations together to say why, picking the observation that supports a point in an explanation, telling what was seen, linking events to what happened, and comparing two ways the class tried (two ways to make the drum louder; opening the flap or shining a flashlight through a hole) to say which worked better, all from the host's own records."
ss,1,g1-ss-inform,WIDA.ELD-SS.1.Inform.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SS.1.Inform.Expressive,k-ss-inform,g2-ss-explain,text map,text map,mountain; river; lake; leader; the job is to; group,social-studies:ss1-landforms-waters (host unit); social-studies:ss1-group-roles (host unit),"Students describe landforms and bodies of water and the roles in a group from the social studies unit. Entry points (all describe a landform or body of water and the roles in a group, from the unit): levels 1 and 2 with pictures of landforms and role cards, a word bank and the cloze ""This is a ___. It has ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""A ___ is ___. The leader's job is to ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the pictures only, comparing two places or two roles, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a description of a place or a role, the topic first and then the facts; ""This is / it has / the job is to""; landform words and group-role words. Model: text map at the K to 1 stage, topic and facts, grouped into places and roles. Home language: a student may name landforms in the home language first; montaA�a and mountain, lago and lake are cognates. Owner year for SS 1. Both modes at grade 1: describing places and roles."
ss,1,g1-ss-argue,WIDA.ELD-SS.1.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SS.1.Argue.Expressive,k-si-argue; k-ss-inform,g2-ss-argue,text map; claim chart,claim chart,I think; reason; vote; choose; problem; should,social-studies:ss1-claim-reasons (host unit); social-studies:ss1-voting (host unit),"Students give a claim with reasons about a class or community problem and vote, from the social studies unit. Entry points (all give a claim with reasons about a class or community problem and vote): levels 1 and 2 with the problem pictured on the vote chart, a picture word bank and the cloze ""I think we should ___ because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""I think ___ because ___. I vote for ___."", off before the end-of-unit vote; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, giving two reasons and answering a classmate's choice, the frame gone after the first application day; the kindergarten text map of facts is faded here into the claim chart. Forms: a claim with reasons and a vote; ""I think ___ because"" and ""should""; vote, choose and problem. Model: claim chart at the K to 1 stage, a picture vote chart with the choice and one reason. Home language: a student may give the reason in the home language first; votar and vote, problema and problem are cognates. Owner year for SS 1. Both modes at grade 1, all parts: naming the topic of a claim, finding what a short pictured source about the problem says (a class survey chart or a note from the office, Curio Class's own, invented for practice), telling a fact that can be checked from an opinion (""The bench is broken"" or ""Blue is the best color""), choosing the fact that supports the claim and saying how it supports it with ""because"" or ""so"". Builds on kindergarten SI Argue and kindergarten SS Inform, since ELD-SS has no Argue id in K."
si,2,g2-si-narrate,~WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Narrate,g1-si-narrate,g3-si-narrate,story map,story map,after that; finally; we went; my partner said; later; it was,ela:e2-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e2-tell-and-record (telling and describing aloud),"Students recount a class event or a school trip in order with details, then retell a partner's recount to a third student. Entry points (all recount a class event or school trip in order with details, then retell a partner's recount to a third student): levels 1 and 2 with photo cards of the event, the picture word bank and the cloze ""First ___. After that ___. Finally ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""First ___. After that ___. Finally ___. My partner said ___."", off before the end-of-unit telling; 5 and 6 with the cards only, adding a detail and a feeling and choosing what the third student needs to know, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a recount with an opening that says when and where; irregular past verbs (went, saw, ate, took) and ""My partner said that""; then, after that and finally. Home language: partners who share a home language may plan the recount in it first; Spanish cognates for trip words (museo and museum, autobA�s and bus) go on the word cards. Carried, owned in grade 3. In grade 2 students add details and retell a partner's recount without the teacher leading every turn; in grade 3, the owner year, the photo cards are gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."
si,2,g2-si-inform,~WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Inform,g1-si-inform,g3-si-inform,text map,text map,also; another; for example; What do I do next?; they have; it has,ela:e2-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e2-tell-and-record (telling and describing aloud),"Students describe a class topic with two or three facts and follow multi-step directions for a center, asking for help when lost. Entry points (all describe a class topic with two or three facts and follow multi-step directions for a center, asking for help when lost): levels 1 and 2 with picture fact cards, the picture word bank and the cloze ""This is ___. It has ___. Can you help me?"", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""___ have ___. They also ___."" and a help card (""What do I do next?""), off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the fact cards only, adding a ""for example"" and checking a direction back, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a short talk with a topic sentence first and the facts after it; the general present (""Frogs lay eggs"") and the help question ""What do I do next?""; also, another and for example. Home language: students may say a fact in the home language first and then in English; cognates in topic words (animal and animal, planta and plant) go on the fact cards. Carried, owned in grade 3. In grade 2 the facts are about a class topic, not an object in the room, and asking for help is taught as part of informing; in grade 3 the fact cards are gone for levels 3 and 4."
si,2,g2-si-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Explain,g1-si-explain,g3-si-explain,cause chain,cause chain,so; because; it did not work; we tried; next; problem,ela:e2-discussion (class discussion rules and turns),"Students explain why a class plan worked or did not and how they solved a small problem with a partner. Entry points (all explain why a class plan worked or did not, and how they solved a small problem with a partner): levels 1 and 2 with two photo cards (the plan, what happened), the picture word bank and the cloze ""We ___. It did not work, so we ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""First we ___. Next ___, so we ___."" on the cause chain, off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, joining two reasons and answering a partner's ""why?"", the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an explanation that says what happened and then why; first, next and so, with the present tense for how a plan works; problem, try and work. Home language: partners may talk the problem through in the home language first; problema and problem are cognates, and the teacher names it. Carried, owned in grade 3. In grade 2 the explanation is about a plan that can fail, so ""so"" and ""did not"" join the steps; in grade 3 the photo cards are gone for levels 3 and 4."
si,2,g2-si-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Argue,g1-si-argue,g3-si-argue,claim chart,claim chart,I agree with; I disagree; my two reasons; and also; but; vote,"ela:e2-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e2-opinion (opinion with a reason, no ELD-LA Argue id before 4-5)","Students give two reasons for a class choice and answer a partner's reason before the class votes. Entry points (all give two reasons for a class choice and answer a partner's reason before the class votes): levels 1 and 2 with the choices and reasons pictured on the claim chart, the picture word bank and the cloze ""I think ___ because ___ and ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""I agree with ___, but ___."" and ""My two reasons are ___ and ___."", off before the end-of-unit vote; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering the partner's reason before giving their own, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a turn that gives a claim, two reasons and an answer to a partner; ""I think ___ because"" with ""and also""; agree, but and reason. Home language: a student may plan the two reasons in the home language first; razA3n and reason are cognates, and the teacher names it. Carried, owned in grade 3. In grade 2 a second reason and an answer to a partner join the opinion; in grade 3 the pictured reasons on the claim chart are gone for levels 3 and 4."
la,2,g2-la-narrate,~WIDA.ELD-LA.2-3.Narrate.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.2-3.Narrate.Expressive,g1-la-narrate,g3-la-narrate,story map,story map,problem; solution; then; after; finally; found,"ela:e2-fables-folktales (reading literature, same text); ela:e2-narrative (narrative writing); ela:e2-story-structure (reading literature, same text)","Students take apart the middle of the ELA unit's fable or folktale: how the writer moves from the problem to the solution with then, after and finally. They retell it on a story map, then build a story with a problem and a solution, with the teacher first and then alone. The full writing lesson stays in ELA. Entry points (all retell an ELA fable on a story map and write a story with a problem and a solution): levels 1 and 2 with the story map and picture cards, a word bank and the cloze ""The problem was ___. Then ___. Finally ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""The problem was ___. After ___, ___. Finally, ___ solved it by ___."", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, adding one line a character says, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a story with a problem, events and a solution; irregular past verbs (found, thought, began); then, after and finally. Home language: a student may plan the story in the home language first; problema and problem, soluciA3n and solution are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 3. In grade 2 stories are shorter and the problem and solution are the target; in grade 3, the owner year, the problem-solution cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."
la,2,g2-la-inform,~WIDA.ELD-LA.2-3.Inform.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.2-3.Inform.Expressive,g1-la-inform,g3-la-inform,text map,text map,topic sentence; also; another; in conclusion; they have,"ela:e2-informative (informative writing); ela:e2-main-topic-paragraphs (reading informational, same text); ela:e2-sentences (forms of English)","Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit text: its topic sentence and how also and another add facts. They find the main topic of each paragraph, then build an informative piece with a topic, linked facts and a conclusion, with the teacher first and then alone. The full report stays in ELA. Entry points (all find the main topic of an ELA text's paragraphs and write a piece with a topic, linked facts and a conclusion): levels 1 and 2 with a labeled diagram of the topic, a word bank and the cloze ""___ is about ___. It also ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""___ have ___. They also ___. Another fact is ___. In conclusion, ___."", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, adding a ""for example"" to one fact, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a paragraph with a topic sentence, facts and a conclusion; the general present (""Bees make honey""); also, another and in conclusion. Home language: a student may say the facts in the home language first; the teacher names cognates in topic words (insecto and insect). Carried, owned in grade 3. In grade 2 paragraphs and linking words are the target; in grade 3 the linking-word frame is gone for levels 3 and 4."
ma,2,g2-ma-explain,~WIDA.ELD-MA.2-3.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.2-3.Explain.Expressive,g1-si-explain; g1-ma-inform,g3-ma-explain,text map,cause chain,first; next; so; tens; ones; regroup,math:m2-explain-strategies (explaining strategies); math:m2-add-sub-within-100 (the problems explained),"Students explain how they added or subtracted within 100 in the math unit, step by step, with a drawing or base-ten model, and listen to a partner's steps. Entry points (all explain how they added or subtracted within 100, step by step, with a drawing or base-ten model, and follow a partner's steps): levels 1 and 2 with the base-ten blocks and their drawing, a word bank and the cloze ""First I ___. Next I ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""First I added the tens. Next ___, so ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the drawing only, saying why the strategy works, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 3, the owner year, the base-ten cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation in steps that ends with the answer; first, next and so, with present tense for how a strategy works (""you take ten from""); tens, ones and regroup. Home language: a student may count and talk through the steps in the home language first; the teacher names cognates in math words (estrategia and strategy). Carried, owned in grade 3. In grade 2 the explanations are of one strategy, with drawings; the grade 1 text map of facts grows here into the cause chain of steps. Builds on grade 1 SI Explain and grade 1 MA Inform, since ELD-MA has no Explain id before 2-3."
ma,2,g2-ma-argue,~WIDA.ELD-MA.2-3.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.2-3.Argue.Expressive,g1-si-argue; g1-ma-inform,g3-ma-argue,text map,claim chart,true; not true; odd; even; I know because; greater,math:m2-odd-even (claims about odd and even); math:m2-compare-three-digit (comparing numbers),"Students decide whether a number is odd or even, or which of two numbers is greater, and say why with a drawing, then answer a partner's claim. Entry points (all decide whether a number is odd or even, or which of two numbers is greater, say why with a drawing and answer a partner's claim): levels 1 and 2 with counters to pair and a drawing, a word bank and the cloze ""___ is even. I know because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""I think ___ is greater because ___. Do you agree?"", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the drawing only, answering a partner's different claim with a reason, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 3 the ""I know because"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a claim with a reason and an answer to a partner; claim plus ""the drawing shows""; true, not true, odd, even and greater. Home language: a student may count pairs in the home language first; par means both pair and even in Spanish (nA�mero par), which helps here. Carried, owned in grade 3. In grade 2 claims are about one number fact, shown with a drawing; the grade 1 text map of facts grows here into the claim chart. Builds on grade 1 SI Argue and grade 1 MA Inform, since ELD-MA has no Argue id before 2-3. A frame may argue from comparisons only when the host's odd and even unit comes later in the year; odd and even then return in grade 3."
sc,2,g2-sc-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SC.2-3.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.2-3.Explain.Expressive,g1-sc-explain; g1-sc-inform,g3-sc-explain,cause chain; text map,cause chain,property; so; because; melt; slowly; quickly,science:s2-material-properties (host investigation); science:s2-fast-slow-events (host investigation),"Students explain how materials' properties suit a use and how land changes quickly or slowly, from the science unit's tests. Entry points (all explain how a material's properties suit a use and how land changes quickly or slowly, from the class's tests): levels 1 and 2 with the test materials to show and labels, a word bank and the cloze ""It is ___, so it ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""We tested ___. It ___, so it is good for ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining a pattern from two observations, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 3 the property cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation from a test to a pattern; first, next and so, with present tense for how materials behave (""wood floats""); property, melt and change. Home language: a student may describe the test in the home language first; the teacher names cognates in material words (metal and metal, plA�stico and plastic). Carried, owned in grade 3. In grade 2 explanations link a property or event to its effect; the grade 1 describing text map is faded here into the cause chain. Also builds on grade 1 SC Inform (describing), which has no id after grade 1. Pace: a grade 2 frame may pair this row with the SC Argue row of the same grade on one host unit where one science unit asks for both; the grade 4 and 5 rows allow the same."
sc,2,g2-sc-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SC.2-3.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.2-3.Argue.Expressive,g1-si-argue; g1-sc-explain,g3-sc-argue,cause chain; claim chart,claim chart,claim; the data shows; best; I think because; test; strong,science:s2-best-material (host investigation); science:s2-heat-cool-change (host investigation),"Students argue which material is best for a purpose and whether heating or cooling a material can be reversed, from test data. Entry points (all argue which material is best for a purpose and whether heating or cooling a material can be reversed, from the class's test data): levels 1 and 2 with the data on the claim chart in pictures, a word bank and the cloze ""I think ___ is best because the test showed ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Our claim is ___. The data shows ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, adding a second piece of evidence and answering a different claim, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 3 the ""the test showed"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a claim with one piece of evidence from a test; claim plus ""the data shows""; best, test and strong. Home language: a student may weigh the test in the home language first; the teacher names cognates in claim words (evidencia and evidence). Carried, owned in grade 3. In grade 2 a claim rests on one test; the grade 1 cause chain is faded here into the claim chart. Builds on grade 1 SI Argue and grade 1 SC Explain, since ELD-SC has no Argue id before 2-3. Pace: a grade 2 frame may pair this row with the SC Explain row of the same grade on one host unit where one science unit asks for both; the grade 4 and 5 rows allow the same."
ss,2,g2-ss-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SS.2-3.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.2-3.Explain.Expressive,g1-si-explain; g1-ss-inform,g3-ss-explain,text map,cause chain,resource; people use; so; because; change; factory,social-studies:ss2-people-environment (host unit); social-studies:ss2-resources-industries (host unit),"Students explain how people use and change their environment and how resources lead to industries, from the social studies unit. Entry points (all explain how people use and change their environment and how a resource leads to an industry): levels 1 and 2 with picture cause and effect cards, a word bank and the cloze ""People use ___ to ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""People use ___, so ___. ___ changed because ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining one change and its effect in a short paragraph, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 3, the owner year, the ""people use"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation from a resource to its use; first, next and so, with present tense for how people use a place; resource, change and factory. Home language: a student may talk through the cards in the home language first; recurso and resource, industria and industry are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 3. In grade 2 explanations link a resource to a use; the grade 1 text map of facts grows here into the cause chain. Builds on grade 1 SI Explain and grade 1 SS Inform, since ELD-SS has no Explain id before 2-3."
ss,2,g2-ss-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SS.2-3.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.2-3.Argue.Expressive,g1-ss-argue,g3-ss-argue,claim chart,claim chart,both say; only one says; account; source; I think; because,social-studies:ss2-claim-sources (host unit); social-studies:ss2-two-accounts (host unit),"Students make a claim from sources and compare two accounts of one event in the social studies unit. Entry points (all compare the two accounts and state a claim from them): levels 1 and 2 with both accounts read aloud and pictured, a two-column picture chart (both say, only one says), the frame ""Both say ___. I think ___."" and talk in the home language first; 3 and 4 with the claim chart and ""Both accounts say ___, but ___. I think ___ because the source says ___.""; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, frame withdrawn, adding ""one account says ___ while the other ___"". The frame comes off for 1 and 2 before the check (the picture chart stays) and for 3 and 4 before the end-of-unit task. Forms: a claim built from two accounts; claim plus ""the source says"" and ""both say, but""; account, source and point of view. Home language: talk in the home language first is planned for levels 1 and 2; fuente and source are not cognates, so the picture chart carries it. Carried, owned in grade 3. In grade 2 claims use two simple sources; in grade 3 the two-column picture chart is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: the host's example pair is about newcomers arriving; the family note previews it and offers a pair Curio Class writes with the unit (two class reports of one school event, not a move); the host's own check pairs are not used; no student is asked about their own arrival, and prompts stay on the supplied sources while social studies asks for family interviews."
si,3,g3-si-narrate,WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Narrate,g2-si-narrate,g4-si-narrate,story map,story map,at the beginning; in the middle; in the end; finally; I felt; she said,ela:e3-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e3-speaking-reports (telling and describing aloud),"Students tell an event from school life with a beginning, middle and end and a feeling, the listener asks a question and retells it back; the class keeps a story map of each telling. Entry points (all tell an event from school life with a beginning, middle and end and a feeling, then answer a listener's question and retell the listener's telling back): levels 1 and 2 with the class story map of three boxes, a picture word bank and the cloze ""At the beginning ___. In the middle ___. In the end ___. I felt ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with the story map and frames for ""said"" and a feeling, off before the end-of-unit telling; 5 and 6 with the story map only, choosing the detail that matters for the listener, the frame gone after the first application day; the photo cards of grade 2 are gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a telling with a beginning, a middle and an end and a feeling in the closing; irregular past verbs and said-words (said, asked, shouted) with the words a person said; then, after and finally. Model: story map at the 2 to 3 stage, problem and solution. Home language: students may plan the telling in the home language on the story map first; the teacher points out cognates in feeling words (nervioso and nervous, furioso and furious). Owner year for SI K-3. Classroom talk for this key language use, heard and spoken (SI has no separate modes), with students running the partner routine themselves; builds on grade 2. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."
si,3,g3-si-inform,WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Inform,g2-si-inform,g4-si-inform,text map,text map,I learned that; for example; also; What do you mean by; Can you say more about,ela:e3-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e3-speaking-reports (telling and describing aloud),"Students share information about a class topic in a short sharing time, then ask and answer clarifying questions; the text map holds the facts. Entry points (all share information about a class topic in a short sharing time, then ask and answer a clarifying question): levels 1 and 2 with the text map of the topic, the picture word bank and the cloze ""I learned that ___. Can you say more?"", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""I learned that ___. For example, ___."" and the question cards ""What do you mean by ___?"", off before the end-of-unit sharing; 5 and 6 with the text map only, asking a clarifying question of their own, the frame gone after the first application day; the fact cards of grade 2 are gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a short share with a topic sentence and grouped facts; the general present and clarifying questions (""What do you mean by ___?""); also, another and for example. Model: text map at the 2 to 3 stage, grouped parts with headings. Home language: students may prepare the share in the home language first; cognates in topic words go on the text map in a second color, and a student who shares no language with the class uses pictures. Owner year for SI K-3. Classroom talk for this key language use, heard and spoken (SI has no separate modes), with students running the partner routine themselves; builds on grade 2."
si,3,g3-si-explain,WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Explain,g2-si-explain,g4-si-explain,cause chain,cause chain,we chose; so; next; instead of; the way we; it worked because,ela:e3-discussion (class discussion rules and turns),"Students explain how their group solved a problem and why they chose that way, using a cause chain on the board. Entry points (all explain how their group solved a problem and why they chose that way, on the cause chain): levels 1 and 2 with the cause chain on the board, the picture word bank and the cloze ""First we ___, so ___. We chose it because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""First ___. Next ___, so ___."" and ""We chose ___ because ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, comparing the way they chose with one they did not, the frame gone after the first application day; the photo cards of grade 2 are gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of a solution in order, with the reason for the choice; present tense for how the way works and ""so"" for what follows; first, next and so. Model: cause chain at the 2 to 3 stage, a chain linked with ""so"". Home language: groups that share a home language may talk the problem through in it first and then explain in English; soluciA3n and solution are cognates. Owner year for SI K-3. Classroom talk for this key language use, heard and spoken (SI has no separate modes), with students running the partner routine themselves; builds on grade 2."
si,3,g3-si-argue,WIDA.ELD-SI.K-3.Argue,g2-si-argue,g4-si-argue; g4-la-argue,claim chart,claim chart,Why do you think; Can we; instead; build on; we decided; I changed my mind,"ela:e3-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e3-opinion (opinion with a reason, no ELD-LA Argue id before 4-5)","Students negotiate a group choice: state a view with reasons, ask for a reason, build on it and agree on a decision; the claim chart records the reasons. Entry points (all negotiate a group choice: state a view with reasons, ask for a reason, build on it and agree on a decision): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of the options, the picture word bank and the cloze ""I think ___ because ___. Why do you think ___?"", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""I agree with ___ because ___."" and ""Can we ___ instead?"", off before the end-of-unit decision; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, building on another's reason and summing up the decision, the frame gone after the first application day; the pictured reasons of grade 2 are gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a short negotiation that ends in one group decision; ""I agree with ___ because"" and ""The data shows"" when the group has counted something; because, instead and decide. Model: claim chart at the 2 to 3 stage, a claim and a reason with its source. Home language: groups may negotiate in a shared home language first and report the decision in English; decidir and decide are cognates. Owner year for SI K-3. Classroom talk for this key language use, heard and spoken (SI has no separate modes), with students running the partner routine themselves; builds on grade 2."
la,3,g3-la-narrate,WIDA.ELD-LA.2-3.Narrate.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.2-3.Narrate.Expressive,g2-la-narrate,g4-la-narrate,story map,story map,dialogue; said; asked; at last; problem; feelings,"ela:e3-narrative (narrative writing); ela:e3-story-parts (reading literature, same text)","Students take apart one scene of the ELA unit's chapter or story: how dialogue and said-words show how the character responds to the problem. They map the problem, events and solution, then build their own story with dialogue and a clear ending, with the teacher first and then alone. The full chapter study stays in ELA. Entry points (all map the problem, events and solution of an ELA story and write one with dialogue and an ending): levels 1 and 2 with the story map and picture cards, a word bank and the cloze ""___ said, '___.' In the end ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with dialogue frames (""___ asked, '___?'"") and ""At last, ___."", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, showing a feeling through what a character says, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 2 problem-solution cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a story with a problem, events, a solution and dialogue; irregular past verbs and said-words with dialogue marks; then, after and finally. Model: story map at the 2 to 3 stage, problem and solution. Home language: a student may draft dialogue in the home language first; the teacher shows how Spanish marks speech with a dash and English with quotation marks, which helps explain an error a student may make. Owner year for LA 2-3. Both modes for 2-3: reading a story's parts and how a character responds, and writing one with dialogue and an ending. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."
la,3,g3-la-inform,WIDA.ELD-LA.2-3.Inform.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.2-3.Inform.Expressive,g2-la-inform,g4-la-inform; g4-la-argue,text map,text map,main idea; detail; heading; for example; also; grouped,"ela:e3-informative (informative writing); ela:e3-main-idea (reading informational, same text); ela:e3-sentences (forms of English)","Students take apart one section of the ELA unit text: how a heading groups facts under one main idea. They map the main idea and details, then build an informative piece in grouped parts with headings, linking words and a conclusion, with the teacher first and then alone. The full research piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all map an ELA text's main idea and details and write a piece in grouped parts with headings): levels 1 and 2 with a labeled diagram and the text map, a word bank and the cloze ""The main idea is ___. One detail is ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with heading frames and ""For example, ___. Also, ___."", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, choosing their own headings, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 2 linking-word frame is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a piece in grouped parts with headings and a conclusion; topic sentences and the general present; also, another and for example. Model: text map at the 2 to 3 stage, grouped parts with headings. Home language: a student may group the facts in the home language first; the teacher names cognates for detail and idea (detalle, idea). Owner year for LA 2-3. Both modes for 2-3: main idea and details read and a grouped informative piece written."
ma,3,g3-ma-explain,WIDA.ELD-MA.2-3.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-MA.2-3.Explain.Expressive,g2-ma-explain,g4-ma-explain,cause chain,cause chain,array; equal groups; rows of; times; so; because,math:m3-mult-div-problems (problems solved); math:m3-area-distributive (area model explained),"Students explain how they solved multiplication and division problems in the math unit using arrays or area models, saying why each step works. Entry points (all explain how they solved a multiplication or division problem with an array or area model, saying why each step works): levels 1 and 2 with the array or area model to point along, a word bank and the cloze ""There are ___ rows of ___, so ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""First I ___. Next I ___, so ___. This works because ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the model only, explaining why the strategy works for a new number, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 2 base-ten cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of a solution path from the problem to the answer; first, next and so, with present tense for how the model works; array, equal groups and rows of. Model: cause chain at the 2 to 3 stage, a chain linked with ""so"". Home language: a student may talk through the model in the home language first; the teacher names cognates (multiplicaciA3n and multiplication, divisiA3n and division). Owner year for MA 2-3. Both modes for 2-3: following and giving explanations of a solution path."
ma,3,g3-ma-argue,WIDA.ELD-MA.2-3.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-MA.2-3.Argue.Expressive,g2-ma-argue,g4-ma-argue,claim chart,claim chart,greater than; equal to; the number line shows; whole; claim; I agree with,math:m3-compare-fractions (comparing fractions); math:m3-equivalent-fractions (equivalent fractions),"Students claim which fraction is greater or that two fractions are equal and justify it with a number line or model, then critique a classmate's claim. Entry points (all claim which fraction is greater or that two fractions are equal, justify it with a number line or model, and critique a classmate's claim): levels 1 and 2 with fraction strips and the number line, a word bank and the cloze ""___ is greater than ___. The number line shows ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""I claim ___ because the model shows ___. I agree with ___ because ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering a classmate's claim with their own model, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 2 ""I know because"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a claim, its reason from a model and a critique; claim plus ""the number line shows""; greater than, equal to and whole. Model: claim chart at the 2 to 3 stage, a claim and a reason with its source (the model). Home language: a student may compare fractions in the home language first; fracciA3n and fraction are cognates. Owner year for MA 2-3. Both modes for 2-3: hearing and making claims about math with reasons."
sc,3,g3-sc-explain,WIDA.ELD-SC.2-3.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SC.2-3.Explain.Expressive,g2-sc-explain,g4-sc-explain,cause chain,cause chain,life cycle; first; force; push; balanced; so,science:s3-life-cycles (host investigation); science:s3-balanced-forces (host investigation),"Students explain life cycles and how balanced and unbalanced forces change motion, using models from the science unit. Entry points (all explain a life cycle and how balanced and unbalanced forces change motion, using the science unit's models): levels 1 and 2 with the life-cycle model or the force test to point to, a word bank and the cloze ""First it is a ___. Next it ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""First ___. Next ___, so ___."" and ""When the push is bigger, ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining a pattern from two observations, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 2 property cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation in stages or steps; first, next and so, with present tense for how things work; life cycle, force and balanced. Model: cause chain at the 2 to 3 stage, a chain linked with ""so"". Home language: a student may name the stages in the home language first; the teacher names cognates (ciclo and cycle, fuerza and force share a root but look different, so the card carries both). Owner year for SC 2-3. Both modes for 2-3: following and giving explanations with models."
sc,3,g3-sc-argue,WIDA.ELD-SC.2-3.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SC.2-3.Argue.Expressive,g2-sc-argue,g4-sc-argue,claim chart,claim chart,claim; the data shows; habitat; survive; trait; evidence,science:s3-habitat-survival (host investigation); science:s3-environment-traits (host investigation),"Students argue whether some organisms survive well in a habitat and whether the environment affects traits, from evidence in the science unit. Entry points (all argue whether some organisms survive well in a habitat and whether the environment affects traits, from the unit's evidence): levels 1 and 2 with the evidence on the claim chart in pictures, a word bank and the cloze ""I think ___ survives because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Our claim is ___. The data shows ___. Another clue is ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering a different claim with a second piece of evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 2 ""the test showed"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a claim with two pieces of evidence and an answer to another claim; claim plus ""the data shows""; habitat, survive and trait. Model: claim chart at the 2 to 3 stage, a claim and a reason with its source. Home language: a student may weigh the evidence in the home language first; hA�bitat and habitat are cognates. Owner year for SC 2-3. Both modes for 2-3: hearing and making claims with evidence."
ss,3,g3-ss-explain,WIDA.ELD-SS.2-3.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SS.2-3.Explain.Expressive,g2-ss-explain,g4-ss-explain,cause chain,cause chain,government; level; decides; so; because; land,social-studies:ss3-levels-of-government (host unit); social-studies:ss3-land-changes (host unit),"Students explain what each level of government does and how land changed over time in their region, from the social studies unit. Entry points (all explain what each level of government does and how land changed over time in the region): levels 1 and 2 with picture cards of each level and of the land then and now, a word bank and the cloze ""The ___ government ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 say what one level of government does and why, with ""so"" and ""because"", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining one change in the land and its effect in a short paragraph, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 2 ""people use"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of how and why in a short paragraph; first, next and so, with present tense for what a government does; level, decides and land. Model: cause chain at the 2 to 3 stage, a chain linked with ""so"". Home language: a student may talk through the levels in the home language first; the teacher names cognates (gobierno and government share a root but look different, and local and local look the same), and no student is asked how government works in another country. Owner year for SS 2-3. Both modes for 2-3: following and giving explanations of how and why."
ss,3,g3-ss-argue,WIDA.ELD-SS.2-3.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SS.2-3.Argue.Expressive,g2-ss-argue,g4-ss-argue,claim chart,claim chart,claim; the source says; I agree with; evidence; deliberate; local,social-studies:ss3-claim-evidence (host unit); social-studies:ss3-deliberation (host unit),"Students make a claim with evidence and take part in a class deliberation on a local question, from the social studies unit. Entry points (all make a claim with evidence and take part in a class deliberation on a local question): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of the question and its sources in pictures, a word bank and the cloze ""I think ___. The source says ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""My claim is ___. The source says ___. I agree with ___ because ___."", off before the end-of-unit deliberation; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, weighing two sources before stating the claim, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 2 two-column picture chart is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a claim from a source in a deliberation; claim plus ""the source says"" and ""I agree with ___ because""; claim, evidence and deliberate. Model: claim chart at the 2 to 3 stage, a claim and a reason with its source. Home language: a student may prepare the claim in the home language first; evidencia and evidence are cognates. Owner year for SS 2-3. Both modes for 2-3: hearing and making claims from sources."
si,4,g4-si-narrate,WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Narrate,g3-si-narrate,g5-si-narrate,story map,story map,after that; meanwhile; finally; our group; at first,ela:e4-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e4-presenting (telling and describing aloud),"Students recount a group task they shared (a project, a field study) for a classmate who was absent, in order, with linking words, then listen to a recount and ask a follow-up. Entry points (all recount a group task for a classmate who was absent, in order, then ask a follow-up of another recount): levels 1 and 2 with the group's picture notes on the story map, a word bank of time words and the cloze ""At first we ___. After that ___. Finally ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""At first ___. Meanwhile ___. After that ___."" and a follow-up card, off before the end-of-unit recount; 5 and 6 with the story map only, choosing what the absent classmate needs and leaving out the rest, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a recount for a listener who was not there, with an opening that says what the task was; time clauses (when, while, as soon as) and the past progressive (""we were measuring""); after that, meanwhile and finally. Model: story map at the 4 to 5 stage, scenes and pacing (which moments get more time). Home language: students may plan the recount in the home language on the story map first; the absent classmate hears it in English. Owner year for SI 4-12. The language of classroom life for this key language use at grades 4 to 12, owned here because students need it from the day they enter grade 4; builds on SI K-3 in grade 3. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."
si,4,g4-si-inform,WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Inform,g3-si-inform,g5-si-inform,text map,text map,the task is; step; plan; role; Do you mean; clarify,ela:e4-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e4-presenting (telling and describing aloud),"Students give and follow directions for a group task, report the group's plan to the class and ask for clarification (""Do you mean...?""). Entry points (all give and follow directions for a group task, report the group's plan to the class and ask ""Do you mean ___?""): levels 1 and 2 with the plan on a picture text map, gestures, a word bank and the cloze ""Our plan is to ___. First ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Our plan is to ___. Each person will ___. Do you mean ___?"", off before the end-of-unit report; 5 and 6 with the text map only, summing up another group's plan to check it, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a plan report that gives the goal first and then the steps and roles; ""will"" for the plan and relative clauses for roles (""the person who measures""); in addition and for instance. Model: text map at the 4 to 5 stage, the structure of the plan (goal, steps, roles). Home language: a group may plan in a shared home language and report in English; plan and plan, rol and role are cognates. Owner year for SI 4-12. The language of classroom life for this key language use at grades 4 to 12, owned here because students need it from the day they enter grade 4; builds on SI K-3 in grade 3."
si,4,g4-si-explain,WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Explain,g3-si-explain,g5-si-explain,cause chain,cause chain,because; as a result; so that; instead; the reason,ela:e4-discussion (class discussion rules and turns),"Students explain how the group divided a task and why they changed their plan, using cause and sequence linking words. Entry points (all explain how the group divided a task and why they changed their plan): levels 1 and 2 with the cause chain of the change, labeled step cards, a word bank and the cloze ""We ___. Then ___, so we changed to ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""At first we ___. As a result, ___. So we ___ instead."" off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, giving the reason for the change in their own words, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an explanation that moves from the first plan to the change and its reason; ""as a result"" and ""which caused"" to join a cause to its effect; instead, the reason and divide. Model: cause chain at the 4 to 5 stage, branching causes. Home language: students may talk through the change in the home language first; the teacher shows ""como resultado"" beside ""as a result"". Owner year for SI 4-12. The language of classroom life for this key language use at grades 4 to 12, owned here because students need it from the day they enter grade 4; builds on SI K-3 in grade 3."
si,4,g4-si-argue,WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Argue,g3-si-argue,g5-si-argue,claim chart,claim chart,I agree with; I see it differently; evidence; build on; decide; compromise,ela:e4-discussion (class discussion rules and turns),"Students persuade their group to a plan, disagree politely with a reason, and reach a compromise, recording reasons on a claim chart. Entry points (all persuade the group to a plan, disagree politely with a reason and reach a compromise, recorded on the claim chart): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of options and evidence, a word bank and the cloze ""I think we should ___ because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""I see it differently because ___."" and ""What if we ___?"", off before the end-of-unit decision; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, building on and summing up others' views to reach the compromise, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a short persuasion with a claim, grouped reasons and a compromise; ""should"" for a proposal and ""I see it differently because""; for example, therefore and compromise. Model: claim chart at the 4 to 5 stage, a claim with grouped reasons and evidence. Home language: a student may plan the case in the home language first; evidencia and evidence, compromiso and compromise are cognates (compromiso can also mean a promise, which the teacher names). Owner year for SI 4-12. The language of classroom life for this key language use at grades 4 to 12, owned here because students need it from the day they enter grade 4; builds on SI K-3 in grade 3."
la,4,g4-la-narrate,~WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Narrate.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Narrate.Expressive,g3-la-narrate,g5-la-narrate,story map,story map,narrator; describe; meanwhile; suddenly; as soon as; theme,"ela:e4-narrative (narrative writing); ela:e4-theme-summary (reading literature, same text)","Students take apart one passage of the ELA unit's story: how describing noun groups and a time clause (""As soon as the door opened"") slow a scene down. They retell the story with its theme, then build one scene with dialogue and description, rehearsing it aloud first, with the teacher and then alone. The full narrative stays in ELA. Entry points (all retell an ELA story with its theme and write one scene with dialogue and description): levels 1 and 2 with the story map, a word bank and the cloze ""When ___, ___ said, '___.'"", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with time-clause frames (""As soon as ___, ___."") and dialogue frames, off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, using description and pacing and saying why, the frame off by mid-unit after the first application day. Forms: a scene with an opening, dialogue and description; time clauses (when, while, as soon as) and describing noun groups (""the old wooden gate""); meanwhile, suddenly and as soon as. Home language: a student may draft the scene in the home language first; the teacher names cognates for describing words (enorme and enormous, misterioso and mysterious). Carried, owned in grade 5. In grade 4 stories are longer, and theme and description are added; in grade 5, the owner year, the time-clause frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."
la,4,g4-la-inform,~WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Inform.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Inform.Expressive,g3-la-inform,g5-la-inform,text map,text map,definition; a ___ is a ___ that; in addition; for instance; structure; subtopic,"ela:e4-informative (informative writing); ela:e4-text-structure (reading informational, same text); ela:e4-grammar (forms of English)","Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit text: how a definition sentence (""A ___ is a ___ that ___"") and a signal word show its structure. They map the text's structure, then build an informative section grouped by subtopic with a defined term, with the teacher first and then alone. The full piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all map an ELA text's structure and write a section grouped by subtopic with a defined term): levels 1 and 2 with the text map and a labeled diagram, a word bank and the cloze ""A ___ is a ___. It ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""A ___ is a ___ that ___. In addition, ___."" and signal-word cards, off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, choosing a structure that fits the subtopic, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a section grouped by subtopic with a defined term; definition sentences and relative clauses (""a tool that measures""); in addition, for instance and definition. Home language: a student may group the facts in the home language first; definiciA3n and definition, estructura and structure are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 5. In grade 4 text structure and defined terms are added; in grade 5 the definition cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
la,4,g4-la-argue,~WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Argue.Expressive,g3-si-argue; g3-la-inform,g5-la-argue,text map; claim chart,claim chart,opinion; reason; for example; therefore; in my opinion; should,ela:e4-opinion (opinion or argument writing); ela:e4-reasons-evidence (reading reasons and evidence),"Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit text where the author gives a reason and its evidence (""For example, ___""). They find the reasons and evidence, then build one paragraph of an opinion with grouped reasons, examples and a conclusion, with the teacher first and then alone. The full opinion piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all find an author's reasons and evidence and write an opinion paragraph with grouped reasons): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of the author's reasons, a word bank and the cloze ""I think ___ because ___. For example, ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""In my opinion, ___. One reason is ___. For example, ___. Therefore, ___."", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, grouping two reasons with their evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 3 text map of grouped facts is faded here into the claim chart. Forms: an opinion paragraph with an opinion, grouped reasons and a conclusion; ""should"" and ""in my opinion""; for example, therefore and one reason is. Home language: a student may plan the reasons in the home language first; opiniA3n and opinion, evidencia and evidence are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 5. In grade 4 reasons and their evidence are the target; in grade 5 the opinion cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Builds on grade 3 SI Argue (talk with reasons) and grade 3 LA Inform (grouped facts), since ELD-LA has no Argue id before 4-5."
ma,4,g4-ma-explain,~WIDA.ELD-MA.4-5.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.4-5.Explain.Expressive,g3-ma-explain,g5-ma-explain,cause chain,cause chain,place value; product; partial products; as a result; which gives; break apart,math:m4-multiply-multidigit (multiplication explained); math:m4-divide-multidigit (division explained),"Students explain multi-digit multiplication and division from the math unit with area models and place value, step by step. Entry points (all explain multi-digit multiplication or division with an area model and place value, step by step): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled area model, a word bank and the cloze ""___ times ___ is ___. Then I add ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""First I break ___ into ___. As a result, ___, which gives ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the model only, explaining the method so a partner could use it on a new problem, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 5, the owner year, the area-model cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a method explained step by step so another person could follow it; ""as a result"" and ""which gives""; place value, product and partial products. Home language: a student may talk through the method in the home language first; the teacher names cognates (producto and product, valor posicional and place value are close). Carried, owned in grade 5. In grade 4 the steps are multi-digit; in grade 5 the area-model cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Pace: a grade 4 frame may pair this row with the MA Argue row of the same grade on one host unit where the hosts share a core unit."
ma,4,g4-ma-argue,~WIDA.ELD-MA.4-5.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.4-5.Argue.Expressive,g3-ma-argue,g5-ma-argue,claim chart,claim chart,equivalent; justify; the model shows; therefore; I agree because; greater than,math:m4-equivalent-fractions (equivalence justified); math:m4-compare-fractions (comparison justified),"Students justify that two fractions are equivalent or compare them with a model, and respond to a classmate's reasoning. Entry points (all justify that two fractions are equivalent, or compare them, with a model, and respond to a classmate's reasoning): levels 1 and 2 with fraction strips and the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""___ is equivalent to ___. The model shows ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""I claim ___. One reason is ___. Therefore, ___. I agree because ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, critiquing another solution and saying which is stronger, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 5 the equivalence cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a justification with a claim, grouped reasons and a response to a partner; ""therefore"" and ""the model shows""; equivalent, justify and greater than. Home language: a student may compare the fractions in the home language first; equivalente and equivalent are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 5. In grade 4 claims rest on a visual model; in grade 5 the equivalence cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
sc,4,g4-sc-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SC.4-5.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.4-5.Explain.Expressive,g3-sc-explain,g5-sc-explain,cause chain,cause chain,energy; transfer; as a result; which causes; erosion; model,science:s4-energy-transfer (host investigation); science:s4-weathering-erosion (host investigation),"Students explain how energy moves from place to place and how weathering and erosion shape land, from models in the science unit. Entry points (all explain how energy moves from place to place and how weathering and erosion shape land, from the science unit's models): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled model or data, a word bank and the cloze ""Energy moves from ___ to ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""When ___, energy ___. As a result, ___, which causes ___."", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining the mechanism and linking it to the evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 5 the energy cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a cause-and-effect explanation through a system; ""as a result"" and ""which causes"", with one process noun (erosion); energy, transfer and model. Home language: a student may explain the model in the home language first; energA-a and energy, erosiA3n and erosion are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 5. In grade 4 explanations follow energy and change through a system; in grade 5 the energy cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Pace: a grade 4 frame may pair this row with the SC Argue row of the same grade on one host unit where the hosts share a core unit."
sc,4,g4-sc-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SC.4-5.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.4-5.Argue.Expressive,g3-sc-argue,g5-sc-argue,claim chart,claim chart,claim; evidence; reasoning; structure; works best; therefore,science:s4-structures-function (host investigation); science:s4-information-codes (comparing solutions),"Students argue how an animal's structures help it survive and which way of sending a coded message works best, from the class's tests. Entry points (all argue how an animal's structures help it survive, or which way of sending a coded message works best, from the class's tests): levels 1 and 2 with the test results on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""___ works best because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Our claim is ___. The evidence is ___. This shows ___ because ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering another group's claim with evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 5 the ""works best"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a claim with evidence and reasoning; ""should"", ""therefore"" and ""this shows""; structure, works best and reasoning. Home language: a student may weigh the tests in the home language first; estructura and structure, cA3digo and code are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 5. In grade 4 claims rest on gathered evidence; in grade 5 the ""works best"" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
ss,4,g4-ss-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SS.4-5.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.4-5.Explain.Expressive,g3-ss-explain,g5-ss-explain,cause chain,cause chain,region; resource; led to; as a result; respond; which causes,social-studies:ss4-regions-resources (host unit); social-studies:ss4-disasters-response (host unit),"Students explain how a region's resources shape its life and how people respond to natural disasters, from the social studies unit. Entry points (all explain how a region's resources shape its life and how people respond to a natural disaster, weighing which cause matters more): levels 1 and 2 with pictured cause cards on the cause chain, a word bank and the cloze ""One cause was ___. This led to ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Because of ___, ___. As a result, ___. ___ mattered more."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, joining causes with ""which causes"" and saying which mattered more, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 5, the owner year, the cause-card cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation with several causes linked to one effect; ""as a result"" and ""which causes"", with one process noun (the response); region, led to and respond. Home language: a student may sort the causes in the home language first; regiA3n and region, recurso and resource are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 5. In grade 4 explanations connect several causes in a region; in grade 5 the cause-card cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: the host's disaster unit touches disaster and loss, and some students lived through a hurricane, flood or earthquake; the family note previews it and offers the regions host for the talk task, and no student is asked about their own experience. Pace: a grade 4 frame may pair this row with the SS Argue row of the same grade on one host unit where one host unit asks for both."
ss,4,g4-ss-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SS.4-5.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.4-5.Argue.Expressive,g3-ss-argue,g5-ss-argue,claim chart,claim chart,primary source; the source shows; for example; therefore; claim; research,social-studies:ss4-primary-source (host unit); social-studies:ss4-canada-mexico-research (host unit),"Students make claims from primary sources and research in the social studies unit, citing the source. Entry points (all make a claim from primary sources and research, corroborating one source with another): levels 1 and 2 with the sources on the claim chart in pictures, a word bank and the cloze ""The source shows ___. Another source ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""My claim is ___. For example, the source says ___. Therefore, ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering another reading of the source, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 5 the ""source shows"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a claim from grouped evidence in sources; ""for example"" and ""therefore""; primary source, research and claim. Home language: a student may read a source's summary in the home language first if the school has one; investigaciA3n and investigation are cognates; research shares no root with it, so the card carries both. Carried, owned in grade 5. In grade 4 claims rest on one or two primary sources; in grade 5 the ""source shows"" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: a student from the country studied is never made the class expert or asked to translate; they choose their topic like everyone else."
si,5,g5-si-narrate,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Narrate,g4-si-narrate,g6-si-narrate,story map,story map,at first; over several days; the key moment was; by the end; our group,ela:e5-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e5-presenting (telling and describing aloud),"Students recount a longer group project across several days for a new classmate, choosing the key moments and leaving out the rest. Entry points (all recount a group project across several days for a new classmate, choosing the key moments): levels 1 and 2 with a story map of the days and a time-word bank, the cloze ""On the first day ___. By the end ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""On the first day ___. Over the next days ___. The key moment was ___."", off before the end-of-unit recount; 5 and 6 with the story map only, cutting what the new classmate does not need, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a recount across days that slows down at the key moment; time clauses (when, as soon as) and the past progressive, with describing noun groups (""the tall tower we built""); over several days, by the end and at first. Home language: students may plan which moments to keep in the home language first; the teacher names the cognate momento and moment. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 5 the tasks last several days, so choosing the key moments is the new step; the grade 4 picture notes are gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."
si,5,g5-si-inform,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Inform,g4-si-inform,g6-si-inform,text map,text map,so far; we have finished; next we will; So you mean; summarize,ela:e5-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e5-presenting (telling and describing aloud),"Students give a short report on a group's progress and summarize classmates' reports, checking they understood. Entry points (all give a short report on the group's progress and sum up a classmate's report, checking they understood): levels 1 and 2 with the group's progress on the text map, a word bank and the cloze ""We finished ___. Next we will ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""So far we have ___. Next we will ___. So you mean ___?"", off before the end-of-unit report; 5 and 6 with the text map only, summing up a classmate's report in two sentences, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a progress report in two parts, done and next; present perfect for what is done (""we have finished"") and ""will"" for next steps; so far, next and in addition. Home language: a student may draft the report in the home language and give it in English; progreso and progress are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 5 the report is to the whole class and the listener sums it up; the grade 4 gestures are gone for levels 3 and 4."
si,5,g5-si-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Explain,g4-si-explain,g6-si-explain,cause chain,cause chain,so that; as a result; we decided to; process; why not,ela:e5-discussion (class discussion rules and turns),"Students explain a group's process on a longer project and the reasons for its choices to another group. Entry points (all explain the group's process on a longer project and the reasons for its choices to another group): levels 1 and 2 with the cause chain of choices, labeled step cards, a word bank and the cloze ""We chose ___ because ___. As a result ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""We decided to ___ so that ___."" and ""This caused ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, answering the other group's ""why not ___?"", the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an explanation of a process with the reason for each choice; ""so that"" for purpose and ""which causes"" for effect; as a result, process and choice. Home language: groups may plan the explanation in a shared home language first; proceso and process are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 5 the explanation goes to another group, who ask ""why not?"", so reasons must answer another way; the grade 4 step cards are gone for levels 3 and 4."
si,5,g5-si-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Argue,g4-si-argue,g6-si-argue,claim chart,claim chart,should; I see it differently; the other plan; therefore; for example,ela:e5-discussion (class discussion rules and turns),"Students make the case for a group plan to the class and answer a different plan with reasons. Entry points (all make the case for the group's plan to the class and answer a different plan with reasons): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of both plans, a word bank and the cloze ""Our plan is better because ___. For example, ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""We think ___ because ___. The other plan ___, but ___."", off before the end-of-unit case; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering the other plan's strongest reason, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a case for the class with a claim, grouped reasons and an answer to the other plan; ""should"" and ""the other plan ___, but""; for example, therefore and better. Home language: a student may plan the case in the home language first; the teacher names the cognates plan and plan, and razA3n and reason as sharing a root but looking different. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 5 the case is made to the whole class against another plan, not inside one group; the grade 4 ""What if"" frame is gone for levels 3 and 4."
la,5,g5-la-narrate,WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Narrate.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Narrate.Expressive,g4-la-narrate,g6-la-narrate,story map,story map,chapter; scene; while; was running; looking back; theme,"ela:e5-narrative (narrative writing); ela:e5-whole-structure (reading literature, same text)","Students take apart the passage where the ELA unit novel's chapters join: how the past progressive and a time clause carry the reader across. They map how the scenes fit together, then build a narrative scene with pacing and a reflective ending, with the teacher first and then alone. The full novel study and narrative stay in ELA. Entry points (all map how an ELA novel's scenes fit together and write a scene with pacing and a reflective ending): levels 1 and 2 with the story map of scenes, a word bank and the cloze ""While ___ was ___, ___. In the end, ___ learned ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with past-progressive frames and an ending frame (""Looking back, ___.""), off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, choosing which moment to slow down, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 4 time-clause frame is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a scene with pacing and a reflective ending; time clauses, describing noun groups and the past progressive (""she was running when""); while, looking back and chapter. Model: story map at the 4 to 5 stage, theme, scenes and pacing. Home language: a student may draft the ending in the home language first; the teacher names escena and scene as cognates, and capA-tulo and chapter as sharing a root but looking different. Owner year for LA 4-5. Both modes for 4-5: how a story's structure carries its theme, and writing with description, dialogue and pacing. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."
la,5,g5-la-inform,WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Inform.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Inform.Expressive,g4-la-inform,g6-la-inform,text map,text map,both texts; while; which; compare; for instance; precise,"ela:e5-informative (informative writing); ela:e5-compare-structures (reading informational, same text); ela:e5-grammar (forms of English)","Students take apart one paragraph from each of two ELA unit texts on one topic: how each signals its structure (compare or cause and effect) with relative clauses and linking words. They compare the structures, then build an informative paragraph with precise words in the structure that fits, with the teacher first and then alone. The full report stays in ELA. Entry points (all compare two ELA texts' structures and write an informative paragraph in a fitting structure): levels 1 and 2 with both texts on a text map, a word bank and the cloze ""Text 1 is about ___. Text 2 ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Both texts ___. Text 1 ___, while text 2 ___."" and relative-clause frames, off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, choosing precise words for the topic, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 4 definition cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an informative paragraph in a structure that fits the topic; definition sentences and relative clauses (""which"", ""that""); in addition, for instance and while. Model: text map at the 4 to 5 stage, the text's structure (cause, compare, sequence). Home language: a student may compare the texts in the home language first; comparar and compare, causa and cause are cognates. Owner year for LA 4-5. Both modes for 4-5: comparing text structures and writing an organized piece with precise language."
la,5,g5-la-argue,WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.4-5.Argue.Expressive,g4-la-argue,g6-la-argue,claim chart,claim chart,this shows; therefore; the strongest reason; for example; should; point,ela:e5-opinion (opinion or argument writing); ela:e5-reasons-to-points (reading reasons and evidence),"Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit text where the author links a reason to its evidence with therefore and for example. They trace which reasons support which points, then build an opinion paragraph that links reasons to evidence, with the teacher first and then alone. The full opinion piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all trace which reasons support which points and write an opinion paragraph that links reasons to evidence): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of points and reasons, a word bank and the cloze ""___ should ___ because ___. For example, ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""One reason is ___. This shows ___. Therefore, ___."", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, ordering reasons from strongest to weakest, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 4 opinion cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an opinion paragraph with grouped reasons linked to evidence and a conclusion; ""should"" and ""this shows""; for example, therefore and the strongest reason. Model: claim chart at the 4 to 5 stage, a claim with grouped reasons and evidence. Home language: a student may plan the reasons in the home language first; the teacher names ejemplo and example, which share a root but look different. Owner year for LA 4-5. Both modes for 4-5: reading reasons and evidence, writing an opinion with linked reasons."
ma,5,g5-ma-explain,WIDA.ELD-MA.4-5.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-MA.4-5.Explain.Expressive,g4-ma-explain,g6-ma-explain,cause chain,cause chain,decimal; tenths; quotient; product; which means; as a result,math:m5-decimal-operations (decimal steps); math:m5-fraction-mult-problems (problem solutions),"Students explain decimal operations and fraction word problems from the math unit, saying why each step fits the problem. Entry points (all explain decimal operations and fraction word problems, saying why each step fits the problem): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled model (grid or tape diagram), a word bank and the cloze ""I multiply ___ by ___ because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""First ___. This step fits because ___, which means ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the model only, explaining the method so a partner could use it on a new problem, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 4 area-model cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of a problem's solution, step by step with why each fits; ""as a result"" and ""which means"", with one process noun (the product, the quotient); decimal, tenths and quotient. Model: cause chain at the 4 to 5 stage, branching causes (more than one step can lead on). Home language: a student may talk through the problem in the home language first; the teacher names the cognates decimal and decimal, cociente and quotient, and that many countries write 3,5 where the United States writes 3.5. Owner year for MA 4-5. Both modes for 4-5: following and writing explanations of procedures and problem solutions. Pace: a grade 5 frame may pair this row with the MA Argue row of the same grade on one host unit where the hosts share a core unit."
ma,5,g5-ma-argue,WIDA.ELD-MA.4-5.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-MA.4-5.Argue.Expressive,g4-ma-argue,g6-ma-argue,claim chart,claim chart,larger; smaller; scaling; for example; therefore; should,math:m5-scaling (scaling claims); math:m5-shape-hierarchy (classifying shapes),"Students argue whether multiplying by a fraction makes a number larger or smaller, and classify shapes in a hierarchy, defending each placement. Entry points (all argue whether multiplying by a fraction makes a number larger or smaller, and defend where a shape goes in a hierarchy): levels 1 and 2 with the model and the hierarchy chart, a word bank and the cloze ""It gets smaller because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""I claim ___. For example, ___. Therefore, ___."" and a response frame, off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, critiquing another solution and saying which is stronger, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 4 equivalence cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument with a claim, grouped reasons and an example; ""should"" and ""for example""; larger, smaller and scaling. Model: claim chart at the 4 to 5 stage, a claim with grouped reasons and evidence. Home language: a student may test examples in the home language first; the teacher names cognates for shape names (rectA�ngulo and rectangle, cuadrado and square share a root but look different, so the card carries both). Owner year for MA 4-5. Both modes for 4-5: making and critiquing claims with reasons from the work."
sc,5,g5-sc-explain,WIDA.ELD-SC.4-5.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SC.4-5.Explain.Expressive,g4-sc-explain,g6-sc-explain,cause chain,cause chain,energy; particle; matter; as a result; which causes; the model shows,science:s5-food-energy-sun (host investigation); science:s5-particles (host investigation),"Students explain how energy in food came from the sun and that matter is made of particles too small to see, from the science unit. Entry points (all explain how energy in food came from the sun and that matter is made of particles too small to see): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled model, a word bank and the cloze ""The energy came from ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""The sun ___. As a result, ___, which causes ___."", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining the mechanism and linking it to evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 4 energy cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of a system with a model; ""as a result"" and ""which causes"", with process nouns; particle, matter and the model shows. Model: cause chain at the 4 to 5 stage, branching causes. Home language: a student may explain the model in the home language first; partA-cula and particle, materia and matter are cognates. Owner year for SC 4-5. Both modes for 4-5: explanations of systems with models. Pace: a grade 5 frame may pair this row with the SC Argue row of the same grade on one host unit where the hosts share a core unit."
sc,5,g5-sc-argue,WIDA.ELD-SC.4-5.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SC.4-5.Argue.Expressive,g4-sc-argue,g6-sc-argue,claim chart,claim chart,evidence; reasoning; gravity; supports; for example; should,science:s5-gravity-down (host investigation); science:s5-plant-matter (host investigation),"Students argue that gravity pulls objects down and that plants get matter mainly from air and water, from evidence in the science unit. Entry points (all argue that gravity pulls objects down and that plants get matter mainly from air and water, from the unit's evidence): levels 1 and 2 with the evidence on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""Gravity pulls ___ because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Our claim is ___. For example, ___. Therefore, ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering another claim with evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 4 ""works best"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a claim with grouped evidence and reasoning; ""should"" and ""for example""; gravity, supports and evidence. Model: claim chart at the 4 to 5 stage, a claim with grouped reasons and evidence. Home language: a student may weigh the evidence in the home language first; gravedad and gravity are cognates. Owner year for SC 4-5. Both modes for 4-5: claims with evidence and reasoning."
ss,5,g5-ss-explain,WIDA.ELD-SS.4-5.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SS.4-5.Explain.Expressive,g4-ss-explain,g6-ss-explain,cause chain,cause chain,cause; as a result; led to; tax; branches; which caused,social-studies:ss5-revolution-causes (host unit); social-studies:ss5-constitution (host unit),"Students explain the causes of the Revolution and how the Constitution organizes government, from the social studies unit. Entry points (all explain the causes of the Revolution and how the Constitution organizes government, weighing which cause mattered most): levels 1 and 2 with pictured cause cards on the cause chain, a word bank and the cloze ""One cause was ___. It led to ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Because of ___, ___. As a result, ___, which caused ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, weighing several causes in their own words, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 4 cause-card cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of historical causes and a system; ""as a result"" and ""which caused"", with process nouns; tax, branch and led to. Model: cause chain at the 4 to 5 stage, branching causes. Home language: a student may sort the causes in the home language first; revoluciA3n and revolution, constituciA3n and constitution are cognates. Owner year for SS 4-5. Both modes for 4-5: explanations of historical causes and systems. Teacher note: the host is about the causes of a war, and some students have lived through war or left one; the family note previews it and offers the Constitution host for the talk task, and the work stays on causes. Pace: a grade 5 frame may pair this row with the SS Argue row of the same grade on one host unit where one host unit asks for both."
ss,5,g5-ss-argue,WIDA.ELD-SS.4-5.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SS.4-5.Argue.Expressive,g4-ss-argue,g6-ss-argue,claim chart,claim chart,corroborate; both sources; therefore; for example; civil rights; should,social-studies:ss5-sourcing-corroboration (host unit); social-studies:ss5-civil-rights-research (host unit),"Students corroborate sources and argue a claim from research on a civil rights topic, from the social studies unit. Entry points (all corroborate sources and argue a claim from research on a civil rights topic): levels 1 and 2 with both sources on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""Both sources say ___. I think ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Both sources show ___. For example, ___. Therefore, ___ should ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering another reading of the sources, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 4 ""source shows"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a claim from corroborated sources; ""should"", ""for example"" and ""therefore""; corroborate, both sources and civil rights. Model: claim chart at the 4 to 5 stage, a claim with grouped reasons and evidence. Home language: a student may compare the sources in the home language first; corroborar and corroborate are cognates. Owner year for SS 4-5. Both modes for 4-5: claims from corroborated sources. Teacher note: the civil rights topic comes from the host unit's list; no student is asked to speak for a group or to share family experience of discrimination."
si,6,g6-si-narrate,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Narrate,g5-si-narrate,g7-si-narrate,story map,story map,later that day; by then; in first period; I had already; the key moment was,ela:e6-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e6-present-claims (presenting in order to an audience),"Students recount a school-life event in a new middle school setting (a first week with several teachers, a club meeting) for a peer, with clear time shifts. Entry points (all recount a school-life event in middle school, a first week with several teachers or a club meeting, for a peer, with clear time shifts): levels 1 and 2 with a story map by class period, a word bank and the cloze ""In first period ___. Later that day ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Before ___, I had ___. Later that day ___."", off before the end-of-unit recount; 5 and 6 with the story map only, keeping the point of view steady across periods, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a recount ordered by time and place; past perfect for an earlier event (""I had already left"") and pronouns kept steady (I, we, they); later that day, before and by then. Model: story map at the 6 to 8 stage, point of view and episodes. Home language: students may plan the recount in the home language first; the peer hears it in English, and the class word wall shows the Spanish periodo beside period. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 6 the settings multiply (several teachers, new routines), so time shifts between places are the new step; the grade 5 day-by-day cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."
si,6,g6-si-inform,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Inform,g5-si-inform,g7-si-inform,text map,text map,in this class; first of all; Could you explain; students; routine,ela:e6-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e6-present-claims (telling and describing aloud),"Students explain a class routine to a new student and ask teachers clarifying questions across several classes. Entry points (all explain a class routine to a new student and ask a teacher a clarifying question in another class): levels 1 and 2 with the routine on a text map, a word bank and the cloze ""In this class you ___. First ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""In this class, students ___. Could you explain ___?"", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, comparing two classes' routines, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an explanation of a routine in sections (arriving, working, leaving); formal register with no ""you"" in the written version (""students hand in""); first of all, in this class and could you explain. Model: text map at the 6 to 8 stage, sections building a central idea. Home language: a new student who shares a home language may hear the routine in it from a buddy first, by choice, then in English; no student is made the class interpreter. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 6 routines differ from class to class, so informing includes asking each teacher; the grade 5 progress cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
si,6,g6-si-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Explain,g5-si-explain,g7-si-explain,cause chain,cause chain,"deadline; due; in order to; if, then; that is why",ela:e6-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns),"Students explain how they will meet a deadline across classes and why they planned it that way. Entry points (all explain how they will meet a deadline across classes and why they planned it that way): levels 1 and 2 with the plan on a cause chain by day, a word bank and the cloze ""I will ___ on ___ so that ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""If I ___, then ___. That is why ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, naming a risk to the plan and what they will do, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an explanation of a plan with its reasons and an if-then step; ""if ___, then ___"" and ""that is why""; deadline, due and in order to. Model: cause chain at the 6 to 8 stage, a mechanism with one unseen step (the risk). Home language: students may plan in the home language first; the teacher names the false friend that can trip Spanish speakers here (asistir means attend, not assist). Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 6 the plan crosses several classes, so if-then reasoning joins the because; the grade 5 step cards are gone for levels 3 and 4."
si,6,g6-si-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Argue,g5-si-argue,g7-si-argue,claim chart,claim chart,however; some people think; What is your reason; weigh; role,ela:e6-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns),"Students take part in a structured group decision with roles, asking for and weighing reasons. Entry points (all take part in a structured group decision with roles, asking for and weighing reasons): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart and a role card, a word bank and the cloze ""I think ___ because ___. What is your reason?"", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Some people think ___. However, ___."" and the role's question card, off before the end-of-unit decision; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, weighing two reasons aloud before the vote, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a structured decision with roles (chair, timekeeper, note taker); ""Some people think ___. However, ___"" and ""according to""; however, weigh and role. Model: claim chart at the 6 to 8 stage, a counterclaim and a response. Home language: a group may weigh reasons in a shared home language before speaking in English; the teacher names the cognate argumento and argument. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 6 roles structure the decision, so asking for a reason is a job, not a favor; the grade 5 case frame is gone for levels 3 and 4."
la,6,g6-la-narrate,~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Narrate.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Narrate.Expressive,g5-la-narrate,g7-la-narrate,story map,story map,point of view; had already; later that night; episode; narrator,"ela:e6-narrative-writing (narrative writing); ela:e6-plot-episodes (reading literature, same text)","Students take apart one episode of the ELA unit's novel: how the narrator's pronouns hold the point of view and the past perfect reaches back. They map the novel's episodes, then build a narrative scene with dialogue and a steady point of view, with the teacher first and then alone. The full narrative stays in ELA. Entry points (all map an ELA novel's episodes and write a scene with dialogue and a steady point of view): levels 1 and 2 with the story map of episodes, a word bank and the cloze ""I ___. Before that, I had ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with point-of-view frames and transition frames (""Later that night, ___.""), off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, explaining one point-of-view choice, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a scene told from one point of view; past perfect for an earlier event and point-of-view pronouns kept steady; later that night, had already and transition. Home language: a student may draft the scene in the home language first; narrador and narrator are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 point of view and episodes are the target; texts are grade 6 literature with reading support; in grade 8, the owner year, the point-of-view frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."
la,6,g6-la-inform,~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Inform.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Inform.Expressive,g5-la-inform,g7-la-inform,text map,text map,central idea; can be divided into; types of; section; formal style,"ela:e6-informative-writing (informative writing); ela:e6-section-structure (reading informational, same text); ela:e6-pronouns (forms of English)","Students take apart one section of the ELA unit text: how its heading and a classifying sentence (""can be divided into"") build the central idea. They map the sections, then build an informative section with a heading, a definition and transitions, with the teacher first and then alone. The full piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all map how an ELA text's sections build the central idea and write a section with a heading, a definition and transitions): levels 1 and 2 with the text map of sections, a word bank and the cloze ""The central idea is ___. One section ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""___ can be divided into ___. The first type is ___."" and heading frames, off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, keeping a formal style with no ""you"", the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a section with a heading that builds a central idea; classifying (""types of"", ""can be divided into"") and a formal register with no ""you""; section transitions and central idea. Home language: a student may plan the section in the home language first; clasificar and classify, secciA3n and section are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 sections and formal style are the target; texts are grade 6 with reading support; in grade 8 the classifying cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
la,6,g6-la-argue,~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Argue.Expressive,g5-la-argue,g7-la-argue,claim chart,claim chart,claims; according to; the author states; cite; evidence,ela:e6-argument-writing (opinion or argument writing); ela:e6-trace-argument (reading reasons and evidence),"Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit text where the writer cites evidence with ""according to"" and a reporting verb. They trace the writer's argument on a claim chart, then build one argument paragraph with a claim, a reason and cited evidence, with the teacher first and then alone. The full argument stays in ELA. Entry points (all trace an ELA writer's argument and write a paragraph with a claim, a reason and cited evidence): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""The writer claims ___. According to ___, ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with citation frames (""The author states that ___."") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering one counterclaim in their own words, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an argument paragraph with a claim, a reason and cited evidence; reporting verbs (claims, states) and ""according to""; cite, evidence and claims. Home language: a student may plan the argument in the home language first; citar and cite are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 cited evidence and claim charts are the target; in grade 8 the citation cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
ma,6,g6-ma-explain,~WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Explain.Expressive,g5-ma-explain,g7-ma-explain,cause chain,cause chain,ratio; rate; for every; unit rate; is divided by; represents,math:m6-ratio-parts-problems (ratio problems); math:m6-one-step-equations (equations explained),"Students explain how they solved ratio problems and one-step equations in the math unit, using tables and tape diagrams. Entry points (all explain how they solved a ratio problem or a one-step equation, using a table or tape diagram): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled table or tape diagram, a word bank and the cloze ""For every ___, there are ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""If ___, then ___. The variable represents ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the model only, explaining the method so a partner could use it, with precise terms, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 8, the owner year, the ""for every"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a method explained in steps; passive voice for what is done to a number (""both sides are divided by 3"") and if-then; ratio, rate and for every. Home language: a student may talk through the method in the home language first; razA3n is the Spanish for ratio as well as reason, which the teacher names. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 explanations use ratio and variable language; in grade 8 the ""for every"" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
ma,6,g6-ma-argue,~WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Argue.Expressive,g5-ma-argue,g7-ma-argue,claim chart,claim chart,equivalent; counterexample; always; sometimes; however; claims that,math:m6-equivalent-expressions (equivalence claims); math:m6-statistical-questions (statistical questions),"Students argue whether two expressions are equivalent and whether a question is statistical, giving reasons and an example. Entry points (all argue whether two expressions are equivalent and whether a question is statistical, giving reasons and an example): levels 1 and 2 with the expressions on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""They are equivalent because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Some people think ___. However, ___. A counterexample is ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, critiquing another solution using conditions and definitions, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 8 the equivalence cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a claim with a reason, an example and an answer to another view; ""Some people think ___. However, ___"" and reporting verbs; counterexample, always and sometimes. Home language: a student may test examples in the home language first; the teacher names cognates (expresiA3n and expression, estadA-stica and statistical). Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 claims are checked with examples; in grade 8 the equivalence cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
sc,6,g6-sc-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Explain.Expressive,g5-sc-explain,g7-sc-explain,cause chain,cause chain,"is heated; evaporation; condensation; if, then; air mass; mechanism",science:s6-water-cycle-energy (host investigation); science:s6-air-masses-weather (host investigation),"Students explain how energy drives the water cycle and how air masses make weather, using the science unit's models. Entry points (all explain how energy drives the water cycle and how air masses make weather, using the science unit's models): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled model, a word bank and the cloze ""Water is heated, so ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""When water is heated, it ___. If ___, then ___."", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, naming the mechanism and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 8 the ""is heated"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of a mechanism in stages; passive voice for processes (""water is heated"") and nominalization (evaporation, condensation); if, then and air mass. Home language: a student may explain the model in the home language first; evaporaciA3n and evaporation, condensaciA3n and condensation are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 explanations name the mechanism; in grade 8 the ""is heated"" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
sc,6,g6-sc-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Argue.Expressive,g5-sc-argue,g7-sc-argue,claim chart,claim chart,according to the data; fossil; however; counterclaim; supports; claims,science:s6-plate-evidence (host investigation); science:s6-particle-temperature (host investigation),"Students argue from fossil and rock evidence that plates have moved and about how particle motion relates to temperature. Entry points (all argue from fossil and rock evidence that plates have moved, and how particle motion relates to temperature): levels 1 and 2 with the data sets on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""The fossils show ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""According to the data, ___. Some people think ___. However, ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering another claim and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 8 the ""fossils show"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument with a claim, data and a counterclaim answered; reporting verbs and ""according to""; fossil, supports and counterclaim. Home language: a student may weigh the data in the home language first; fA3sil and fossil are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 claims use data sets; in grade 8 the ""fossils show"" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
ss,6,g6-ss-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Explain.Expressive,g5-ss-explain,g7-ss-explain,cause chain,cause chain,"flood; surplus; specialization; led to; was watered; if, then",social-studies:ss6-egypt-nile (host unit); social-studies:ss6-surplus-specialization (host unit),"Students explain how the Nile shaped Egyptian life and how surplus led to specialization, from the social studies unit. Entry points (all explain how the Nile shaped Egyptian life and how surplus led to specialization, saying which cause mattered more): levels 1 and 2 with pictured cause cards on the cause chain, a word bank and the cloze ""The Nile flooded, so ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""When the river flooded, ___. If ___, then ___. This led to ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, weighing two causes and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 8, the owner year, the flood cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation that links geography to society; passive voice (""the land was watered"") and nominalization (specialization, surplus); led to, if and then. Home language: a student may sort the causes in the home language first; especializaciA3n and specialization are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 explanations link geography and society; in grade 8 the flood cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
ss,6,g6-ss-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Argue.Expressive,g5-ss-argue,g7-ss-argue,claim chart,claim chart,interpretation; historians; according to; however; claims; perspective,social-studies:ss6-changing-interpretations (host unit); social-studies:ss6-research-contribution (host unit),"Students argue how and why interpretations of the past change and present a research claim about a contribution, from the social studies unit. Entry points (all argue how and why interpretations of the past change and present a research claim about a contribution, citing sources): levels 1 and 2 with two interpretations on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""Historians used to think ___. Now ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""According to ___, ___. However, ___ claims ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering another reading and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 8 the ""used to think"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument about an interpretation with another view answered; reporting verbs and ""according to""; interpretation, historians and perspective. Home language: a student may read a source's summary in the home language first if the school has one; interpretaciA3n and interpretation are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 6 claims cite sources; in grade 8 the ""used to think"" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
si,7,g7-si-narrate,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Narrate,g6-si-narrate,g8-si-narrate,story map,story map,during the lab; while; my teammate; had already; by then,ela:e7-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e7-present-salient (presenting in order to an audience),"Students recount what happened in a lab or group session for a teammate who missed it, keeping each member's part straight. Entry points (all recount a lab or group session for a teammate who missed it, keeping each member's part straight): levels 1 and 2 with the group's role list on a story map, a word bank and the cloze ""First ___ measured ___. Then ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""While ___ was ___, ___ had already ___."", off before the end-of-unit recount; 5 and 6 with the story map only, choosing varied speech verbs for what each member said, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a recount that tracks several people at once; past progressive with past perfect (""while she was timing, he had already poured"") and pronouns kept clear; while, during the lab and my teammate. Home language: students may plan the recount in the home language first; laboratorio and lab, experimento and experiment are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 7 group roles and lab routines add several people to one recount, so pronouns must stay clear; the grade 6 period-by-period map is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."
si,7,g7-si-inform,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Inform,g6-si-inform,g8-si-inform,text map,text map,materials; can be divided into; each member will; in summary; role,ela:e7-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e7-present-salient (telling and describing aloud),"Students report a lab group's plan with roles and materials and summarize another group's plan. Entry points (all report a lab group's plan with roles and materials and sum up another group's plan): levels 1 and 2 with the plan on a text map with materials pictured, a word bank and the cloze ""We need ___. ___ will ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""The materials are ___. The roles can be divided into ___."", off before the end-of-unit report; 5 and 6 with the text map only, summing up the other group's plan and naming one difference, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a plan report in sections (materials, roles, steps); classifying (""can be divided into"") and formal register (""each member will""); materials, the roles are and in summary. Home language: a group may plan in a shared home language first; materiales and materials are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 7 the plan has materials and roles, so classifying joins reporting; the grade 6 routine frame is gone for levels 3 and 4."
si,7,g7-si-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Explain,g6-si-explain,g8-si-explain,cause chain,cause chain,procedure; is heated; so that; if we skip; Why did you,ela:e7-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns),"Students explain a group's procedure and the reason for each step, and ask ""why"" questions of another group. Entry points (all explain the group's procedure and the reason for each step, and ask ""why"" of another group): levels 1 and 2 with the procedure on a cause chain, a word bank and the cloze ""We ___ so that ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""The water is heated so that ___. If we skip this step, ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, asking another group a ""why"" question about one step, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a procedure explained step by step with a reason for each; passive voice for steps (""the water is heated"") and if-then for what goes wrong; so that, if we skip and procedure. Home language: groups may talk the procedure through in a shared home language first; procedimiento and procedure are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 7 the explanation is of a set procedure, so the passive voice appears; the grade 6 deadline frame is gone for levels 3 and 4."
si,7,g7-si-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Argue,g6-si-argue,g8-si-argue,claim chart,claim chart,claims that; on the other hand; according to; however; question,ela:e7-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns),"Students discuss a class issue in a structured debate format, building on and questioning others' reasons. Entry points (all discuss a class issue in a structured debate, building on and questioning others' reasons): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of both sides, a word bank and the cloze ""I agree with ___ because ___. However ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""___ claims that ___. However, ___."" and a question card, off before the end-of-unit debate; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, questioning the strongest reason on the other side, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a debate turn with a claim, a reason and a response to the other side; reporting verbs (claims, argues, states) and ""However, ___""; according to, claims and on the other hand. Home language: a student may prepare the turn in the home language first; the teacher names the cognates debate and debate, opiniA3n and opinion. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 7 the debate format means answering a named speaker's reason, so reporting verbs join; the grade 6 role card is gone for levels 3 and 4."
la,7,g7-la-narrate,~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Narrate.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Narrate.Expressive,g6-la-narrate,g8-la-narrate,story map,story map,setting; because of the; had to; tension; muttered,"ela:e7-narrative-writing (narrative writing); ela:e7-story-elements-interact (reading literature, same text)","Students take apart one passage of the ELA unit text where the setting changes what a character does, and the verbs that carry the change. They trace how setting and character shape the plot, then build a scene where a setting changes what a character does, with the teacher first and then alone. The full narrative stays in ELA. Entry points (all trace how setting shapes a character in an ELA text and write a scene where the setting changes what a character does): levels 1 and 2 with the story map of setting and character, a word bank and the cloze ""Because the ___ was ___, ___ had to ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""The storm had ___, so ___."" and dialogue-tag frames, off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, explaining one choice of setting, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 6 point-of-view frame is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a scene where setting drives the action; past perfect for what the setting had done and varied dialogue tags; because of, had to and setting. Home language: a student may draft the scene in the home language first; the teacher names the cognate tensiA3n and tension. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 the interaction of story elements is added; in grade 8 the setting cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."
la,7,g7-la-inform,~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Inform.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Inform.Expressive,g6-la-inform,g8-la-inform,text map,text map,unlike; which means; are both; according to the text; classify,"ela:e7-informative-writing (informative writing); ela:e7-text-organization (reading informational, same text); ela:e7-phrases-clauses (forms of English)","Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit text that classifies or compares, and the phrases and clauses that carry it (""unlike"", ""which means""). They map how the text is organized, then build an informative paragraph that classifies or compares, citing the text, with the teacher first and then alone. The full piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all map how an ELA text is organized and write a paragraph that classifies or compares, citing the text): levels 1 and 2 with the text map, a word bank and the cloze ""___ and ___ are both ___. ___ is different because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Unlike ___, ___. According to the text, ___."", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, choosing the order of the comparison, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 6 classifying cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a paragraph that classifies or compares, citing the text; classifying and formal register with phrases and clauses (""unlike"", ""which means""); section transitions and according to the text. Home language: a student may group the comparison in the home language first; diferente and different, similar and similar are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 organization by classifying and comparing is added; in grade 8 the comparison cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
la,7,g7-la-argue,~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Argue.Expressive,g6-la-argue,g8-la-argue,claim chart,claim chart,some argue; however; enough; sufficient; counterclaim,ela:e7-argument-writing (opinion or argument writing); ela:e7-evaluate-argument (reading reasons and evidence),"Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit text and judge whether its evidence is enough, marking the reporting verbs and ""however"". They then build one paragraph that answers a counterclaim, with the teacher first and then alone. The full argument stays in ELA. Entry points (all judge whether an ELA text's evidence is enough and write a paragraph that answers a counterclaim): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of claim and evidence, a word bank and the cloze ""Some people think ___. However, ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""The evidence is (not) enough because ___. Some argue that ___. However, ___."", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, judging sufficiency in their own words, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 6 citation cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a paragraph that judges evidence and answers a counterclaim; ""Some people think ___. However, ___"" and reporting verbs; enough, however and some argue. Home language: a student may weigh the evidence in the home language first; suficiente and sufficient are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 a counterclaim is added; in grade 8 the ""Some people think"" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
ma,7,g7-ma-explain,~WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Explain.Expressive,g6-ma-explain,g8-ma-explain,cause chain,cause chain,proportional; constant; represents; is subtracted from; two-step; for every,math:m7-proportional-equations (proportional equations); math:m7-two-step-equations (two-step equations),"Students explain how they wrote and solved proportional and two-step equations from the math unit, saying what each part means. Entry points (all explain how they wrote and solved a proportional or two-step equation, saying what each part means): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled equation and tape diagram, a word bank and the cloze ""___ represents ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""First ___ is subtracted from both sides. If ___, then ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the model only, explaining what each symbol represents, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 6 ""for every"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation that names what each symbol stands for; passive voice for steps and if-then; proportional, constant and represents. Home language: a student may talk through the equation in the home language first; proporcional and proportional, constante and constant are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 students explain what each symbol represents; in grade 8 the ""represents"" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
ma,7,g7-ma-argue,~WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Argue.Expressive,g6-ma-argue,g8-ma-argue,claim chart,claim chart,condition; never; sample; according to the data; argues; however,math:m7-triangle-conditions (triangle conditions); math:m7-compare-populations (comparing samples),"Students argue whether three lengths make a triangle and compare two populations from samples, citing the data. Entry points (all argue whether three lengths make a triangle and compare two populations from samples, citing the data): levels 1 and 2 with the strips or data on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""They make a triangle because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""According to the data, ___. However, ___."" and a condition frame (""This works only if ___.""), off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, critiquing another solution using conditions, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 6 counterexample cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument from a condition or from data, with another view answered; reporting verbs and ""according to the data""; condition, never and sample. Home language: a student may test the lengths in the home language first; condiciA3n and condition, poblaciA3n and population are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 conditions and data are the evidence; in grade 8 the triangle cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
sc,7,g7-sc-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Explain.Expressive,g6-sc-explain,g8-sc-explain,cause chain,cause chain,photosynthesis; is converted; glucose; is taken in; mechanism; energy,science:s7-photosynthesis (host investigation); science:s7-cell-respiration-growth (host investigation),"Students explain photosynthesis and how cells use food for energy and growth, from the science unit. Entry points (all explain photosynthesis and how cells use food for energy and growth): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled model, a word bank and the cloze ""Light is used to ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Carbon dioxide is taken in and ___. If ___, then ___."", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining the unseen step and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 6 ""is heated"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation that follows matter and energy through a process; passive voice (""is converted"", ""is taken in"") and nominalization (photosynthesis, respiration); if, then and glucose. Home language: a student may explain the model in the home language first; fotosA-ntesis and photosynthesis, glucosa and glucose are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 explanations follow matter and energy; in grade 8 the ""light is used"" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
sc,7,g7-sc-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Argue.Expressive,g6-sc-argue,g8-sc-argue,claim chart,claim chart,population; according to; some argue; reasoning; ecosystem; however,science:s7-ecosystem-disruption (host investigation); science:s7-body-systems-food (host investigation),"Students argue how a change to an ecosystem affects its populations and how body systems work together, from evidence. Entry points (all argue how a change to an ecosystem affects its populations and how body systems work together, from evidence): levels 1 and 2 with the data on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""When ___ changed, the population ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""According to ___, ___. Some argue that ___. However, ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, linking evidence to claim with reasoning and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 6 ""fossils show"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument with reasoning that links evidence to claim; reporting verbs and ""Some argue that ___. However, ___""; population, argues and reasoning. Home language: a student may weigh the evidence in the home language first; ecosistema and ecosystem are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 reasoning that links evidence to claim is the target; in grade 8 the population cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
ss,7,g7-ss-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Explain.Expressive,g6-ss-explain,g8-ss-explain,cause chain,cause chain,"invasion; the main reason was; not only, but also; led to; was spread; trade",social-studies:ss7-rome-fall (host unit); social-studies:ss7-silk-road-trade (host unit),"Students explain why Rome fell and how Silk Road trade spread goods and ideas, from the social studies unit. Entry points (all explain two causes of Rome's fall and say which mattered more): levels 1 and 2 with pictured cause cards on the cause chain and ""One cause was ___. This led to ___. ___ mattered more.""; 3 and 4 with the cause chain and frames using ""because of"", ""as a result"" and ""led to""; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, joining causes with ""not only ... but also"" and ""the main reason was"". The cloze comes off for 1 and 2 before the check (the word bank stays), the frames for 3 and 4 before the end-of-unit writing task and for 5 and 6 after the first application day; the grade 6 flood cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation that weighs several causes; passive voice for processes (""the empire was divided"") and ""not only ... but also""; the main reason was, invasion and was spread. Home language: a student may sort the causes in the home language first; invasiA3n and invasion, imperio and empire are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 explanations weigh several causes; in grade 8 the cause-card frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: the fall of Rome includes invasion and the movement of peoples; the family note previews it and offers the Silk Road host, and no student is asked to link it to their own family's movement."
ss,7,g7-ss-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Argue.Expressive,g6-ss-argue,g8-ss-argue,claim chart,claim chart,compare; whereas; society; argues; according to; however,social-studies:ss7-comparing-societies (host unit); social-studies:ss7-research-greek (host unit),"Students compare societies and argue a research claim about ancient Greece, citing sources. Entry points (all compare two societies and argue a research claim about ancient Greece, citing sources): levels 1 and 2 with both societies on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""___ had ___, whereas ___ had ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""According to ___, ___. Some argue that ___. However, ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering another reading and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 6 ""used to think"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a comparison claim with sources and another view answered; reporting verbs and ""Some argue that ___. However, ___""; whereas, compare and society. Home language: a student may plan the comparison in the home language first; sociedad and society, comparar and compare are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 8. In grade 7 comparison claims are added; in grade 8 the ""whereas"" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
si,8,g8-si-narrate,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Narrate,g7-si-narrate,g9-si-narrate,story map,story map,last week; before that; added; announced; as a result,ela:e8-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e8-present-reasoning (presenting in order to an audience),"Students recount a group decision or event for the class newsletter or an announcement, telling it in order for an audience who was not there. Entry points (all recount a group decision or event for the class newsletter or an announcement, in order, for an audience who was not there): levels 1 and 2 with the story map of the event, a word bank and the cloze ""On ___, our class ___. After that ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Last week, our class ___. Before that, we had ___."", off before the end-of-unit recount; 5 and 6 with the story map only, choosing an opening that hooks a reader and varied tags for quoted speech, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a newsletter recount with a headline, an opening that says what and when, and a close; past perfect for flashback and varied dialogue tags (explained, added, asked); last week, before that and as a result. Model: story map at the 6 to 8 stage, point of view and episodes, here the episodes an absent reader needs. Home language: students may draft in the home language first; a family who wants the newsletter in another language asks the school, and no student is asked to translate it. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 8 the audience is a whole school who were not there, so the opening must say what and when; the grade 7 role list is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."
si,8,g8-si-inform,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Inform,g7-si-inform,g9-si-inform,text map,text map,brief; the main steps are; first of all; any questions; to clarify,ela:e8-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e8-present-reasoning (telling and describing aloud),"Students brief a group on a task and its steps, and take notes on a classmate's briefing. Entry points (all brief a group on a task and its steps, and take notes on a classmate's briefing): levels 1 and 2 with the task on a text map, a word bank and the cloze ""The main steps are ___. Any questions?"", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""First of all, ___. The main steps are ___. To clarify, ___."", off before the end-of-unit briefing; 5 and 6 with the text map only, taking notes on a briefing and asking one question to clarify, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a briefing with a goal, the main steps and a call for questions; formal register and section transitions (""first of all"", ""to clarify""); brief, the main steps and any questions. Model: text map at the 6 to 8 stage, sections building a central idea (the goal of the task). Home language: a student may take notes in any language; the teacher names the cognate clarificar and clarify. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 8 students lead the briefing and others take notes, so the listener's job is written; the grade 7 materials frame is gone for levels 3 and 4."
si,8,g8-si-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Explain,g7-si-explain,g9-si-explain,cause chain,cause chain,the disagreement; we agreed to; as a result of; the solution; worked for,ela:e8-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns),"Students explain how a group conflict was solved and why the solution worked, without blaming. Entry points (all explain how a group conflict was solved and why the solution worked, without blaming): levels 1 and 2 with the cause chain of the conflict and solution, a word bank and the cloze ""The problem was ___. We agreed to ___, so ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""The disagreement was about ___. As a result of ___, ___."" and no-blame phrases, off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining why the solution worked for everyone, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an explanation from problem to solution to result; nominalization to keep blame off people (""the disagreement"", ""the solution"") and if-then; we agreed to, as a result of and the solution. Model: cause chain at the 6 to 8 stage, a mechanism with an unseen step (what each side needed). Home language: students may talk the conflict through in a shared home language first; soluciA3n and solution, conflicto and conflict are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 8 the explanation is of a conflict, so words that name the problem, not the person, are new; the grade 7 procedure frame is gone for levels 3 and 4."
si,8,g8-si-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Argue,g7-si-argue,g9-si-argue,claim chart,claim chart,What do you think about; Can you give evidence; to sum up; outcome; respectfully,ela:e8-collab-discussion (class discussion rules and turns),"Students lead a short group decision: invite views, challenge a reason respectfully and sum up the outcome. Entry points (all lead a short group decision: invite views, challenge a reason respectfully and sum up the outcome): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart and a leader's script, a word bank and the cloze ""What do you think about ___? So we decided ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""That is interesting, but ___. Can you give evidence?"", off before the end-of-unit decision; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, summing up both sides fairly before the outcome, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a led decision that opens, invites, challenges and sums up; reporting verbs and ""Some people think ___. However, ___""; respectfully, evidence and outcome. Model: claim chart at the 6 to 8 stage, a counterclaim and a response. Home language: a student may plan the leader's turns in the home language first; the teacher names the cognate respetuosamente and respectfully. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 8 students lead the exchange, so inviting and summing up are new; the grade 7 debate card is gone for levels 3 and 4."
la,8,g8-la-narrate,WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Narrate.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Narrate.Expressive,g7-la-narrate,g9-la-narrate,story map,story map,flashback; years before; whispered; reveals; incident,"ela:e8-narrative-writing (narrative writing); ela:e8-dialogue-incidents (reading literature, same text)","Students take apart one passage of the ELA unit text where dialogue and an incident reveal a character, and the dialogue tags that carry tone. They then build a narrative scene with a flashback or rising tension, explaining one craft choice, with the teacher first and then alone. The full narrative stays in ELA. Entry points (all study how dialogue reveals character in an ELA text and write a scene with a flashback or tension, explaining one choice): levels 1 and 2 with the story map of the incident, a word bank and the cloze ""Years before, ___ had ___. Now ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with flashback frames and dialogue-tag frames (""___ whispered, as if ___.""), off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, explaining why they used a flashback, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 7 setting cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a scene with a flashback or rising tension; past perfect for flashback, point-of-view pronouns kept steady and varied dialogue tags; years before, whispered and reveals. Model: story map at the 6 to 8 stage, point of view and episodes. Home language: a student may draft the scene in the home language first; the teacher names the false friend that can trip Spanish speakers (molestar means bother, not molest). Owner year for LA 6-8. Both modes for 6-8: analyzing narrative craft and writing with point of view, pacing and transitions. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."
la,8,g8-la-inform,WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Inform.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Inform.Expressive,g7-la-inform,g9-la-inform,text map,text map,furthermore; consequently; central idea; transition; formal style,"ela:e8-informative-writing (informative writing); ela:e8-paragraph-structure (reading informational, same text); ela:e8-verbals-voice-mood (forms of English)","Students take apart one key paragraph of the ELA unit text: how its sentences build one idea and which transitions move it on. They trace how the paragraph builds the idea, then build an informative section with varied transitions in a formal style, with the teacher first and then alone. The full piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all trace how a key ELA paragraph builds an idea and write a formal section with varied transitions): levels 1 and 2 with the text map of the paragraph, a word bank and the cloze ""First, the writer ___. Then ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with transition frames (""Furthermore, ___. As a result, ___."") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, choosing the structure and keeping a formal style, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 7 comparison cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a formal section that builds one central idea; classifying, formal register with no ""you"" and varied section transitions; furthermore, consequently and central idea. Model: text map at the 6 to 8 stage, sections building a central idea. Home language: a student may outline the section in the home language first; consecuentemente and consequently are cognates. Owner year for LA 6-8. Both modes for 6-8: tracing how a text develops ideas and writing a structured, formal piece."
la,8,g8-la-argue,WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Argue.Expressive,g7-la-argue,g9-la-argue,claim chart,claim chart,argues that; credible; some argue; counterclaim; according to,ela:e8-argument-writing (opinion or argument writing); ela:e8-delineate-argument (reading reasons and evidence),"Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit's argument: how the writer brings in a counterclaim (""Some argue that ... However, ..."") and which reporting verbs carry the sources. They then build one paragraph that answers a counterclaim, with the teacher first and then alone. The full essay stays in ELA. Entry points (all take apart an ELA argument paragraph and write one that answers a counterclaim from a credible source): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""Some argue that ___. However, ___ states that ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with reporting-verb frames (""___ argues that ___."") and a counterclaim frame, off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, judging whether a source is credible, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 7 ""Some people think"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument paragraph with a claim, a counterclaim and a response; reporting verbs (claims, argues, states) with ""Some argue that ___. However, ___"" and according to; credible, argues and counterclaim. Model: claim chart at the 6 to 8 stage, a counterclaim and a response. Home language: a student may read a source's summary in the home language first if the school has one; creA-ble and credible are cognates. Owner year for LA 6-8. Both modes for 6-8: evaluating arguments and writing argument with counterclaims."
ma,8,g8-ma-explain,WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Explain.Expressive,g7-ma-explain,g9-ma-explain,cause chain,cause chain,"linear equation; slope; function; is represented by; therefore; if, then",math:m8-linear-equations (linear equations); math:m8-function-model (functions as models),"Students explain how they solved linear equations and how a function models a situation in the math unit. Entry points (all explain how they solved a linear equation and how a function models a situation): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled graph and table, a word bank and the cloze ""The slope represents ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""If ___, then ___. Therefore, ___ is represented by ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the model only, explaining the method so a partner could use it on a new situation, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 7 ""represents"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of solving and of what a model shows; passive voice (""is represented by"") and if-then; slope, function and therefore. Model: cause chain at the 6 to 8 stage, a mechanism with an unseen step (why the method works). Home language: a student may talk through the model in the home language first; funciA3n and function, ecuaciA3n and equation are cognates. Owner year for MA 6-8. Both modes for 6-8: following and giving explanations of solving and modeling."
ma,8,g8-ma-argue,WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-MA.6-8.Argue.Expressive,g7-ma-argue,g9-ma-argue,claim chart,claim chart,proof; congruent; assumption; this proves; however; counterexample,math:m8-congruence (congruence claims); math:m8-angle-facts (angle-sum argument from transformations),"Students explain and defend the transformation argument for the triangle angle sum and argue whether figures are congruent, critiquing another proof. Entry points (all explain and defend the transformation argument for the triangle angle sum and argue whether figures are congruent, critiquing another proof): levels 1 and 2 with the proof diagram on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""The figures are congruent because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Some argue that ___. However, ___. This proves ___ because ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, critiquing another proof using definitions, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 7 triangle cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a defended proof with a claim, its steps and a critique; reporting verbs and ""Some argue that ___. However, ___""; proof, congruent and assumption. Model: claim chart at the 6 to 8 stage, a counterclaim and a response. Home language: a student may follow the proof in the home language first; teorema and theorem, congruente and congruent are cognates. Owner year for MA 6-8. Both modes for 6-8: hearing and making mathematical arguments with conditions and definitions."
sc,8,g8-sc-explain,WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Explain.Expressive,g7-sc-explain,g9-sc-explain,cause chain,cause chain,"natural selection; is passed on; variation; if, then; force; mass",science:s8-natural-selection (host investigation); science:s8-forces-motion-mass (host investigation),"Students explain natural selection and how force and mass change motion, from the science unit. Entry points (all explain natural selection and how force and mass change motion): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled model, a word bank and the cloze ""The trait is passed on because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Variation is ___. If ___, then ___ is passed on."", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining the mechanism and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 7 ""light is used"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a mechanism explanation tied to evidence; passive voice (""is passed on"") and nominalization (variation, selection); if, then and force. Model: cause chain at the 6 to 8 stage, a mechanism with an unseen step. Home language: a student may explain the model in the home language first; selecciA3n natural and natural selection, variaciA3n and variation are cognates. Owner year for SC 6-8. Both modes for 6-8: mechanism explanations tied to evidence."
sc,8,g8-sc-argue,WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SC.6-8.Argue.Expressive,g7-sc-argue,g9-sc-argue,claim chart,claim chart,kinetic energy; counterclaim; claims; according to; mass; however,science:s8-energy-transfer-argument (host investigation); science:s8-gravity-mass (host investigation),"Students argue that energy transferred when kinetic energy changes and that gravity depends on mass, from data, and answer a counterclaim. Entry points (all argue that energy transferred when kinetic energy changed and that gravity depends on mass, from data, answering a counterclaim): levels 1 and 2 with the data on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""The data shows that energy ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""According to ___, ___. Some argue that ___. However, ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering the counterclaim and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 7 population cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument from data with a counterclaim answered; reporting verbs, ""according to"" and ""Some argue that ___. However, ___""; kinetic energy, claims and counterclaim. Model: claim chart at the 6 to 8 stage, a counterclaim and a response. Home language: a student may weigh the data in the home language first; energA-a cinActica and kinetic energy are cognates. Owner year for SC 6-8. Both modes for 6-8: argument from evidence with a counterclaim."
ss,8,g8-ss-explain,WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Explain.Expressive,g7-ss-explain,g9-ss-explain,cause chain,cause chain,"branch; checks; is divided; federal; power; if, then",social-studies:ss8-three-branches (host unit); social-studies:ss8-federal-state-powers (host unit),"Students explain how the three branches check each other and how federal and state powers divide, from the civics unit. Entry points (all explain how the three branches check each other and how federal and state powers divide): levels 1 and 2 with the branches on the cause chain, a word bank and the cloze ""The ___ branch can ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Power is divided so that ___. If ___, then ___ can ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining the unseen step (why a check works) and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 7 cause-card frame is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of a system and how its parts check each other; passive voice (""power is divided"") and if-then; branch, federal and checks. Model: cause chain at the 6 to 8 stage, a mechanism with an unseen step. Home language: a student may talk through the system in the home language first; federal and federal, legislativo and legislative are cognates. Owner year for SS 6-8. Both modes for 6-8: explaining systems and causes."
ss,8,g8-ss-argue,WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SS.6-8.Argue.Expressive,g7-ss-argue,g9-ss-argue,claim chart,claim chart,ratification; editorial; argues; some argue; counterclaim; however,social-studies:ss8-ratification-debate (host unit); social-studies:ss8-editorial-analysis (host unit),"Students argue a side of the ratification debate and analyze an editorial's claim, answering the other side. Entry points (all argue a side of the ratification debate and analyze an editorial's claim, answering the other side): levels 1 and 2 with both sides on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""The editorial argues ___. Some argue ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""___ argues that ___. Some argue that ___. However, ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering the other side and citing a source, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 7 ""whereas"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument from sources with the other side answered; reporting verbs (claims, argues, states) and ""Some argue that ___. However, ___""; ratification, editorial and counterclaim. Model: claim chart at the 6 to 8 stage, a counterclaim and a response. Home language: a student may plan the side in the home language first; ratificaciA3n and ratification, editorial and editorial are cognates. Owner year for SS 6-8. Both modes for 6-8: argument from sources with another perspective answered."
si,9,g9-si-narrate,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Narrate,g8-si-narrate,g10-si-narrate,story map,story map,during the meeting; the group discussed; looking back; at the meeting; I changed,ela:e9-sl-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e9-sl-presentation (presenting in order to an audience),"Students recount an event (a team meeting, an orientation day) for a peer and then for an adult, noticing what they change between the two tellings. Entry points (all recount one event, a team meeting or an orientation day, for a peer and then for an adult, and say what they changed): levels 1 and 2 with the story map and two audience cards, a word bank and the cloze ""Yesterday we had ___. First ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""At the meeting, we ___."" for the peer and ""During the meeting, the group ___."" for the adult, off before the end-of-unit recount; 5 and 6 with the story map only, naming two changes they made for the adult, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: one recount told twice, for a peer and for an adult; tense shifts on purpose and reflective language (""looking back, I""); during, at the meeting and the group discussed. Model: story map at the 9 to 12 stage, structure across time. Home language: a student may notice how the home language changes for an elder or a teacher and use that as a way into English register; this is offered, never asked. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 9 seminar talk and a register for adults join peer talk, so the same event is told two ways; the grade 8 newsletter frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed. Teacher note: informal English and the home language are not wrong; the task is choosing for the listener."
si,9,g9-si-inform,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Inform,g8-si-inform,g10-si-inform,text map,text map,the seminar focused on; both sides agreed; whereas; I was wondering whether; main point,ela:e9-sl-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e9-sl-presentation (telling and describing aloud),"Students summarize a seminar's main points for a classmate who missed it and ask a teacher for information in a formal register. Entry points (all sum up a seminar's main points for a classmate who missed it and ask a teacher for information in a formal register): levels 1 and 2 with a text map of the seminar's points, a word bank and the cloze ""The main point was ___. Could you tell me ___?"", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""The seminar focused on ___. Both sides agreed that ___. I was wondering whether ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, writing the summary once for the classmate and once as a note to the teacher, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a summary of a discussion by main point; synthesis (""both sides agreed"", ""whereas"") and the polite question ""I was wondering whether""; focused on, main point and I was wondering. Model: text map at the 9 to 12 stage, two or more sources (speakers) synthesized. Home language: a student may take seminar notes in any language; the teacher names the cognate seminario and seminar. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 9 the information comes from a seminar of several speakers, and a formal question to a teacher joins; the grade 8 briefing frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: informal English and the home language are not wrong; the task is choosing for the listener."
si,9,g9-si-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Explain,g8-si-explain,g10-si-explain,cause chain,cause chain,my reasoning was; which suggests; What led you to; it seems; may,ela:e9-sl-discussion (class discussion rules and turns),"Students explain their reasoning aloud in seminar (""I said that because..."") and ask others to explain theirs. Entry points (all explain their reasoning aloud in seminar and ask others to explain theirs): levels 1 and 2 with their reasoning on a cause chain, a word bank and the cloze ""I said that because ___. Why do you think ___?"", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""My reasoning was that ___, which suggests ___. What led you to ___?"", off before the end-of-unit seminar; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, hedging a point they are not sure of, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a reasoning turn that gives the point and then the chain behind it; hedging (may, suggests, it seems) and cause verbs (led to, results in); my reasoning, suggests and what led you. Model: cause chain at the 9 to 12 stage, weighted causes (which reason counts most). Home language: a student may think the reasoning through in the home language before speaking; the teacher names the cognate razonamiento and reasoning. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 9 the explanation is of one's own thinking in a seminar, so hedging is new; the grade 8 conflict frame is gone for levels 3 and 4."
si,9,g9-si-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Argue,g8-si-argue,g10-si-argue,claim chart,claim chart,although; I still think; That changes my view; convinced me; building on,ela:e9-sl-discussion (class discussion rules and turns),"Students take part in a seminar: state a view, build on or question others and change their view when given a good reason. Entry points (all take part in a seminar: state a view, build on or question others and change their view when given a good reason): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of the seminar question, a word bank and the cloze ""I think ___. I agree with ___ because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Although ___, I still think ___."" and ""That changes my view because ___."", off before the end-of-unit seminar; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, saying what reason changed their view, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a seminar turn that states, builds on, questions or revises a view; concession (""Although ___, ___"") and ""That changes my view""; although, I still think and convinced. Model: claim chart at the 9 to 12 stage, rebuttal and the limits of the evidence. Home language: a student may prepare a view in the home language first; the teacher names the cognate convencer and convince. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 9 changing one's view for a good reason is taught as a strength; the grade 8 leader's script is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: informal English and the home language are not wrong; the task is choosing for the listener."
la,9,g9-la-narrate,~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Narrate.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Narrate.Expressive,g8-la-narrate,g10-la-narrate,story map,story map,time shift; back then; even now; parallel; voice,"ela:e9-w-narrative (narrative writing); ela:e9-rl-structure-time (reading literature, same text)","Students take apart one passage of the ELA unit's story where time shifts, flashback or a parallel plot, and the tense changes that signal it. They then build a narrative scene with one time shift, with the teacher first and then alone. The full narrative stays in ELA. Entry points (all study how time is ordered in an ELA story and write a scene with one time shift): levels 1 and 2 with the story map of the two time lines, audio of the passage, a word bank and the cloze ""Now ___. Years earlier, ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with tense-shift frames (""Even now, ___. Back then, ___."") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, shaping the shift on purpose and naming its effect, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a scene with one time shift; tense shifts on purpose and reflective language (""looking back""); back then, even now and time shift. Home language: a student may draft the scene in the home language first; the teacher names how Spanish and English both shift tense to show memory. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 9 structure across time is the target, with a grade 9 text and audio support; in grade 12, the owner year, the tense-shift frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."
la,9,g9-la-inform,~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Inform.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Inform.Expressive,g8-la-inform,g10-la-inform,text map,text map,develops; whereas; which; the development of; analyze,"ela:e9-w-informative (informative writing); ela:e9-ri-unfolding-ideas (reading informational, same text); ela:e9-l-phrases-clauses (forms of English)","Students take apart one passage of the ELA unit text where an idea unfolds, and the clauses and phrases that join and define it (""which"", ""whereas""). They trace how the ideas unfold, then build one analytical paragraph that links ideas with clause and phrase choices, with the teacher first and then alone. The full analysis stays in ELA. Entry points (all trace how ideas unfold in an ELA text and write an analytical paragraph that links them): levels 1 and 2 with the text map of the passage, a word bank and the cloze ""At first the writer ___. Later ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with clause frames (""___, which ___, ___."" ""Whereas ___, ___."") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, keeping a formal tone for a named reader, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an analytical paragraph that traces an idea; nominalization (""the development of"") and clauses that join and define (which, whereas); whereas, develops and analyze. Home language: a student may outline the analysis in the home language first; analizar and analyze are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 9 the work is analysis of a whole text's development; in grade 12 the clause frame is gone for levels 3 and 4."
la,9,g9-la-argue,~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Argue.Expressive,g8-la-argue,g10-la-argue,claim chart,claim chart,although; reasoning; precise; might; concede,ela:e9-w-argument (opinion or argument writing); ela:e9-ri-argument-evaluate (reading reasons and evidence),"Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit text and evaluate its reasoning, marking concession (""Although ___"") and its modals. They then build one argument paragraph with a precise claim and an answered counterclaim, with the teacher first and then alone. The full argument stays in ELA. Entry points (all evaluate an ELA argument's reasoning and write a paragraph with a precise claim and an answered counterclaim): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart built with a partner, a word bank and the cloze ""The claim is ___. Although ___, ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with concession and reasoning frames off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, weighing the counterclaim and choosing tone for the audience, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an argument paragraph with a precise claim and an answered counterclaim; concession (""Although ___, ___"") and hedging modals (may, might); although, reasoning and precise. Home language: a student may plan the argument in the home language first; aunque and although are not cognates, so the word card carries it. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 9 evaluating reasoning is the target; in grade 12 the concession cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
ma,9,g9-ma-explain,~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Explain.Expressive,g8-ma-explain,g10-ma-explain,cause chain,cause chain,is equivalent to; justify each step; intercept; inverse operation; therefore; slope,math:alg1-equation-reasoning (reasoning with equations); math:alg1-linear-equation-graphs (graphs explained),"Students explain each step of solving an equation and what a graph shows, in Algebra I. Entry points (all explain each step of solving an equation and what a graph shows, in Algebra I): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled equation and graph, a word bank and the cloze ""This step is true because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Since ___, it follows that ___. The intercept means ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the model only, justifying each step with precise terms, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 12, the owner year, the ""this step"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a step-by-step justification of a solution; dense noun groups (""the y-intercept of the line"") and cause verbs; is equivalent to, intercept and inverse operation. Home language: a student may talk through the steps in the home language first; equivalente and equivalent, operaciA3n inversa and inverse operation are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 9 explanations justify each step of an equation; in grade 12 the ""this step"" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
ma,9,g9-ma-argue,~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Argue.Expressive,g8-ma-argue,g10-ma-argue,claim chart,claim chart,correlation; causation; does not prove; although; may; strong evidence,math:alg1-correlation-causation (correlation claims); math:alg1-linear-vs-exponential (growth claims),"Students argue whether a correlation shows causation and whether data are linear or exponential, citing the data. Entry points (all argue whether a correlation shows causation and whether data are linear or exponential, citing the data): levels 1 and 2 with the graphs on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""The data shows ___, but it does not prove ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Although ___, this may ___. The data is strong evidence that ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, critiquing another claim using definitions, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 12 the ""does not prove"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument from data that marks what it can and cannot show; concession and hedging modals (may, might); correlation, causation and does not prove. Home language: a student may weigh the data in the home language first; correlaciA3n and correlation, causalidad and causation are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 9 claims rest on data and definitions; in grade 12 the ""does not prove"" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
sc,9,g9-sc-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Explain.Expressive,g8-sc-explain,g10-sc-explain,cause chain,cause chain,codes for; results in; structure; function; suggests; may,science:bio-dna-to-protein (host investigation); science:bio-photosynthesis-energy (host investigation),"Students explain how DNA codes for proteins and how photosynthesis stores energy, in Biology. Entry points (all explain how DNA codes for proteins and how photosynthesis stores energy, in Biology): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled model, a word bank and the cloze ""DNA codes for ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""The sequence of ___ results in ___, which suggests ___."", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining the mechanism, citing sources and naming a limit of the evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 12 the ""codes for"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation that links structure and function; dense noun groups and cause verbs (results in, codes for) with hedging; structure, function and suggests. Home language: a student may explain the model in the home language first; proteA-na and protein, estructura and structure are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 9 explanations link structure and function; in grade 12 the ""codes for"" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
sc,9,g9-sc-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Argue.Expressive,g8-sc-argue,g10-sc-argue,claim chart,claim chart,the evidence suggests; common ancestry; limitation; although; rebuttal; may,science:bio-evidence-common-ancestry (host investigation); science:bio-ecosystem-stability-change (host investigation),"Students argue for common ancestry and about ecosystem stability from evidence, in Biology. Entry points (all argue for common ancestry and about ecosystem stability from evidence, in Biology): levels 1 and 2 with several lines of evidence on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""The evidence suggests ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Although ___, the evidence suggests ___. One limitation is ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering a rebuttal and naming a limit of the evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 12 the ""evidence suggests"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument from several lines of evidence with a limit named; concession and hedging modals; common ancestry, limitation and the evidence suggests. Home language: a student may weigh the evidence in the home language first; ancestro and ancestor, limitaciA3n and limitation are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 9 claims draw on several lines of evidence; in grade 12 the ""evidence suggests"" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
ss,9,g9-ss-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Explain.Expressive,g8-ss-explain,g10-ss-explain,cause chain,cause chain,contact; contributed to; geography; context; may; revolution,social-studies:wh1-geography-shapes-contact (host unit); social-studies:wh1-scientific-revolution (host unit),"Students explain how geography shaped contact between societies and what changed in the Scientific Revolution, in World History I. Entry points (all explain how geography shaped contact between societies and what changed in the Scientific Revolution, weighing factors in context): levels 1 and 2 with the factors on the cause chain, a word bank and the cloze ""Geography contributed to ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""In the context of ___, ___ contributed to ___. This may ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, weighing several factors, citing sources and naming a limit of the evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 12, the owner year, the ""contributed to"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation that weighs factors in context; dense noun groups and cause verbs (contributed to) with hedging; context, contact and may. Home language: a student may sort the factors in the home language first; contexto and context, geografA-a and geography are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 9 explanations use context and several factors; in grade 12 the ""contributed to"" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: the host is about migration and conquest; the family note previews it and offers the Scientific Revolution host, and no student is asked about their own family's movement."
ss,9,g9-ss-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Argue.Expressive,g8-ss-argue,g10-ss-argue,claim chart,claim chart,thesis; although; corroborate; suggests; historian; interpretation,social-studies:wh1-historical-argument (host unit); social-studies:wh1-sources-and-interpretations (host unit),"Students write and defend a historical argument and compare interpretations of sources, in World History I. Entry points (all write and defend a historical argument and compare interpretations of sources, in World History I): levels 1 and 2 with the sources on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""My thesis is ___. The source suggests ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Although ___, the evidence suggests ___. Historian ___ argues ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, corroborating sources and naming a limit of the evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 12 the thesis cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a historical argument with a thesis and sourced evidence; concession and hedging modals; thesis, corroborate and historian. Home language: a student may plan the thesis in the home language first; tesis and thesis, historiador and historian are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 9 a thesis with sourced evidence is the target; in grade 12 the thesis cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
si,10,g10-si-narrate,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Narrate,g9-si-narrate,g11-si-narrate,story map,story map,over the past term; the highlight was; on behalf of; looking back; I would like to introduce,ela:e10-sl-seminar (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e10-sl-presentation (presenting in order to an audience),"Students recount an experience in school life for a formal audience (a club report, an introduction to a visitor) and an informal one. Entry points (all recount an experience in school life for a formal audience, a club report or an introduction to a visitor, and for an informal one): levels 1 and 2 with the story map and two audience cards, a word bank and the cloze ""This year our club ___. One event was ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Over the past term, the club ___. The highlight was ___."" beside the informal version, off before the end-of-unit recount; 5 and 6 with the story map only, choosing tone words for each audience, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a formal recount (opening, events, highlight, close) beside an informal one; tense shifts on purpose (present for the club now, past for the events) and reflective language; over the past term, the highlight was and on behalf of. Home language: a student may plan both versions in the home language first; the visitor hears English. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 10 the formal audience is a real visitor or club, so the opening and close carry the register; the grade 9 two-telling cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."
si,10,g10-si-inform,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Inform,g9-si-inform,g11-si-inform,text map,text map,the update covers; regarding; to answer your question; the change in; three points,ela:e10-sl-seminar (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e10-sl-presentation (telling and describing aloud),"Students give a short informational briefing (a club update, a school event) and answer audience questions. Entry points (all give a short informational briefing, a club update or a school event, and answer audience questions): levels 1 and 2 with the briefing on a text map, a word bank and the cloze ""The event is on ___. Students can ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""The update covers three points: ___. Regarding ___, ___."" and a question card, off before the end-of-unit briefing; 5 and 6 with the text map only, answering an unplanned question, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a briefing with a preview of its points and a close; nominalization (""the change in schedule"") and quotations worked into a sentence (""the coach said that""); regarding, covers and to answer your question. Home language: a student may rehearse the briefing in the home language first; the teacher names the cognate informaciA3n and information. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 10 the audience asks questions the speaker did not plan for; the grade 9 seminar frame is gone for levels 3 and 4."
si,10,g10-si-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Explain,g9-si-explain,g11-si-explain,cause chain,cause chain,I decided on; it results in; contributes to; I went with; since,ela:e10-sl-seminar (class discussion rules and turns),"Students explain a choice they made on a project to a teacher and to peers, choosing words for each listener. Entry points (all explain a choice they made on a project to a teacher and to peers, choosing words for each listener): levels 1 and 2 with the choice on a cause chain and two audience cards, a word bank and the cloze ""I chose ___ because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""I decided on ___ because it results in ___."" for the teacher and ""I went with ___ since ___."" for peers, off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, naming what they changed between listeners, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: one explanation told for two listeners; cause verbs (results in, contributes to) and hedging; decided on, went with and since. Home language: a student may talk the choice through in the home language first; the teacher names the cognates decisiA3n and decision, contribuir and contribute. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 10 the same explanation is shaped for two listeners, so word choice is the target; the grade 9 seminar cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
si,10,g10-si-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Argue,g9-si-argue,g11-si-argue,claim chart,claim chart,the source suggests; most of us agree; still open; clearly; although,ela:e10-sl-seminar (class discussion rules and turns),"Students hold a seminar on a text with several sources, citing reasons and summing up points of agreement. Entry points (all hold a seminar on a text with several sources, citing reasons and summing up points of agreement): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of the sources, a word bank and the cloze ""Source ___ says ___. I agree because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Although source A ___, source B ___. Most of us agree that ___."", off before the end-of-unit seminar; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, summing up points of agreement and the point still open, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a seminar turn that cites a source and sums up agreement; concession and boosting modals (clearly, must); most of us agree, the source suggests and still open. Home language: a student may read a source's summary in the home language first if the school has one; fuente and source are not cognates, so the word card carries it. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 10 the seminar draws on several sources, so citing and summing up are new; the grade 9 ""That changes my view"" frame is gone for levels 3 and 4."
la,10,g10-la-narrate,~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Narrate.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Narrate.Expressive,g9-la-narrate,g11-la-narrate,story map,story map,in contrast; creates; effect; tone; voice,"ela:e10-w-narrative (narrative writing); ela:e10-rl-structure-time (reading literature, same text)","Students take apart one passage from each of two ELA unit narratives: how structure and voice differ, and the tone words that mark the voice. They then build one scene that uses structure for an effect, with the teacher first and then alone. The full comparison and narrative stay in ELA. Entry points (all compare structure and voice in two ELA narratives and write a scene that uses structure for an effect): levels 1 and 2 with both passages on a story map, a word bank and the cloze ""Text 1 starts ___. Text 2 starts ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""In contrast to ___, ___ begins with ___, which creates ___."", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, naming the effect they chose, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 9 tense-shift frame is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a scene whose structure creates an effect; tense shifts on purpose and tone words (bitter, hopeful); in contrast, creates and effect. Home language: a student may draft the scene in the home language first; efecto and effect, contraste and contrast are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 10 students compare texts and name an effect; in grade 12 the contrast cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."
la,10,g10-la-inform,~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Inform.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Inform.Expressive,g9-la-inform,g11-la-inform,text map,text map,both sources; synthesize; nonetheless; whereas; quotation,"ela:e10-w-informative (informative writing); ela:e10-ri-unfolding-ideas (reading informational, same text); ela:e10-l-syntax-style (forms of English)","Students take apart one passage from each of two ELA unit sources: how the writers order ideas, and the synthesis words that join them (""both"", ""whereas""). They then build one informative paragraph that synthesizes the two sources, with the teacher first and then alone. The full piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all trace how writers order ideas in two ELA sources and write a paragraph that synthesizes them): levels 1 and 2 with both sources on a text map, a word bank and the cloze ""Both sources say ___. Source 1 ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with synthesis frames (""Whereas ___, ___. Both ___."") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, working a short quotation into a sentence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 9 clause frame is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a paragraph that synthesizes two sources; synthesis (""both sources"", ""whereas"") and quotations worked into a sentence; synthesize, both sources and nonetheless. Home language: a student may compare the sources in the home language first; sintetizar and synthesize are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 10 synthesis of two sources is added; in grade 12 the synthesis cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
la,10,g10-la-argue,~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Argue.Expressive,g9-la-argue,g11-la-argue,claim chart,claim chart,rhetoric; matters more; while it is true; rebuttal; weigh,ela:e10-w-argument (opinion or argument writing); ela:e10-ri-argument-evaluate (reading reasons and evidence),"Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit text and mark its rhetoric: the evaluative words and the rebuttal moves. They then build one argument paragraph that weighs two counterclaims, with the teacher first and then alone. The full argument stays in ELA. Entry points (all evaluate rhetoric in an ELA text and write a paragraph that weighs two counterclaims): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of both counterclaims, a word bank and the cloze ""One view is ___. Another view is ___. I think ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with rebuttal frames (""While ___ is true, ___ matters more because ___."") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, choosing tone for the audience, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 9 concession cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument paragraph that weighs two counterclaims; concession and rebuttal moves with evaluative words; matters more, rhetoric and while it is true. Home language: a student may weigh the counterclaims in the home language first; retA3rica and rhetoric are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 10 rhetoric and weighed counterclaims are added; in grade 12 the rebuttal cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
ma,10,g10-ma-explain,~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Explain.Expressive,g9-ma-explain,g11-ma-explain,cause chain,cause chain,right triangle; sine; is modeled by; assuming; density; results in,math:geo-solving-right-triangles (right triangles); math:geo-modeling-objects-density (modeling objects),"Students explain how they solved right-triangle problems and modeled real objects in Geometry. Entry points (all explain how they solved a right-triangle problem and modeled a real object, in Geometry): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled diagram, a word bank and the cloze ""I used ___ because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Assuming ___, the object is modeled by ___, which results in ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the model only, explaining a modeling choice so a partner could use it, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 9 ""this step"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of a modeling choice and a solution; dense noun groups and cause verbs with ""assuming""; is modeled by, sine and density. Home language: a student may talk through the model in the home language first; densidad and density, triA�ngulo rectA�ngulo and right triangle are close. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 10 explanations of modeling choices are added; in grade 12 the ""I used"" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
ma,10,g10-ma-argue,~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Argue.Expressive,g9-ma-argue,g11-ma-argue,claim chart,claim chart,given; prove; it follows that; definition; although; rebuttal,math:geo-triangle-congruence-criteria (congruence proofs); math:geo-parallelogram-proofs (parallelogram proofs),"Students write and present triangle congruence and parallelogram proofs, critiquing a partner's proof. Entry points (all write and present a triangle congruence or parallelogram proof, critiquing a partner's proof): levels 1 and 2 with the proof diagram and a two-column outline, a word bank and the cloze ""Given ___, so ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Since ___, it follows that ___. Although ___, ___."" for the critique, off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, critiquing a partner's proof with definitions, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 9 ""does not prove"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a formal proof with given, statements, reasons and conclusion; concession and rebuttal for the critique; given, it follows that and definition. Home language: a student may plan the proof in the home language first; definiciA3n and definition are cognates, while demostrar and prove are not, so the word card carries prove. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 10 formal proof is the target; in grade 12 the ""given, so"" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
sc,10,g10-sc-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Explain.Expressive,g9-sc-explain,g11-sc-explain,cause chain,cause chain,contributes to; periodic; reaction rate; collisions; results in; suggests,science:chem-atomic-structure-periodic-patterns (host investigation); science:chem-reaction-rates (host investigation),"Students explain periodic patterns from atomic structure and what changes reaction rates, in Chemistry. Entry points (all explain periodic patterns from atomic structure and what changes reaction rates, in Chemistry): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled particle model, a word bank and the cloze ""When ___ goes up, the rate ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""More frequent collisions contribute to ___, which results in ___."", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining at the particle level, citing sources and naming a limit, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 9 ""codes for"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a particle-level explanation of a pattern; dense noun groups (""the rate of the reaction"") and cause verbs (contributes to, results in); periodic, collisions and suggests. Home language: a student may explain the model in the home language first; periA3dico and periodic, colisiA3n and collision are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 10 particle-level explanations are the target; in grade 12 the rate cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
sc,10,g10-sc-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Argue.Expressive,g9-sc-argue,g11-sc-argue,claim chart,claim chart,feedback; equilibrium; clearly; is limited because; although; rebuttal,science:chem-earth-feedbacks (host investigation); science:chem-thermal-equilibrium (host investigation),"Students argue about Earth's feedbacks and thermal equilibrium from data, in Chemistry. Entry points (all argue about Earth's feedbacks and thermal equilibrium from data, in Chemistry): levels 1 and 2 with the data on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""The data clearly shows ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Although ___, the data shows ___. However, it is limited because ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, weighing the limits of the data and answering a rebuttal, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 9 ""evidence suggests"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument that weighs the limits of its data; concession and boosting modals (clearly, must); feedback, equilibrium and is limited because. Home language: a student may weigh the data in the home language first; equilibrio and equilibrium are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 10 claims weigh limits of data; in grade 12 the ""clearly shows"" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
ss,10,g10-ss-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Explain.Expressive,g9-ss-explain,g11-ss-explain,cause chain,cause chain,industrialization; resulted in; imperialism; over time; contributed to; may,social-studies:wh2-industrial-revolution (host unit); social-studies:wh2-imperialism-causes (host unit),"Students explain the causes and effects of the Industrial Revolution and of imperialism, in World History II. Entry points (all explain the causes and effects of the Industrial Revolution and of imperialism, weighing causes over time): levels 1 and 2 with the causes on the cause chain, a word bank and the cloze ""Industrialization resulted in ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Over time, ___ contributed to ___, which resulted in ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, weighing causes over time, citing sources and naming a limit of the evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 9 ""contributed to"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation that weighs causes over time; nominalization (industrialization, imperialism) and cause verbs (resulted in); over time, contributed to and may. Home language: a student may sort the causes in the home language first; industrializaciA3n and industrialization, imperialismo and imperialism are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 10 explanations weigh causes over time; in grade 12 the ""resulted in"" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: the imperialism host is about colonial rule, which some families lived under; the family note previews it and offers the Industrial Revolution host, and no student is asked to speak for a country or a people."
ss,10,g10-ss-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Argue.Expressive,g9-ss-argue,g11-ss-argue,claim chart,claim chart,historians disagree; weigh; the evidence suggests; although; thesis; persuasive,social-studies:wh2-research-argument (host unit); social-studies:wh2-historians-interpretations (host unit),"Students argue a research claim and weigh historians' interpretations, in World History II. Entry points (all argue a research claim and weigh historians' interpretations, in World History II): levels 1 and 2 with the interpretations on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""Historians disagree about ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Although ___ argues ___, the evidence suggests ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, weighing interpretations and naming a limit of the evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 9 thesis cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a research argument that weighs interpretations; concession, rebuttal moves and evaluative words; historians disagree, weigh and the evidence suggests. Home language: a student may read a source's summary in the home language first if the school has one; the teacher names the false friend that can trip Spanish speakers (eventualmente means possibly, not eventually). Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 10 weighing interpretations is added; in grade 12 the ""historians disagree"" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
si,11,g11-si-narrate,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Narrate,g10-si-narrate,g12-si-narrate,story map,story map,the situation was; my role was to; as a result; I learned that; one time,ela:e11-seminar (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e11-presentation (presenting in order to an audience),"Students recount an experience for an interview-style question (a time you worked on a team), shaping the order for the listener. Entry points (all recount an experience for an interview-style question, a time you worked on a team, shaping the order for the listener): levels 1 and 2 with the story map in three parts (situation, action, result), a word bank and the cloze ""One time, our team ___. I ___. In the end ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""The situation was ___. My role was to ___. As a result, ___."", off before the end-of-unit recount; 5 and 6 with the story map only, choosing which detail to lead with for this listener, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an interview recount in three parts (situation, action, result); tense shifts on purpose and reflective language (""I learned that""); my role was, as a result and one time. Home language: a student may plan the recount in the home language first; the teacher names the cognates situaciA3n and situation, resultado and result. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 11 students switch on purpose between formal and informal registers, and the interview question sets the order; the grade 10 club frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed. Teacher note: informal English and the home language are not wrong; the task is choosing for the listener. Teacher note: a student may speak as an invented applicant; no task asks about documents, aid eligibility or a family's situation."
si,11,g11-si-inform,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Inform,g10-si-inform,g12-si-inform,text map,text map,I would like to ask about; Could you tell me; appointment; I am calling about; thank you for,ela:e11-seminar (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e11-presentation (telling and describing aloud),"Students request and give information in formal settings (a counselor meeting, a phone call to an office), practicing both registers. Entry points (all request and give information in a formal setting, a counselor meeting or a phone call to an office, and then in an informal one): levels 1 and 2 with a model call to copy and change, a word bank and the cloze ""Hello, I am calling about ___. Could you tell me ___?"", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""I would like to ask about ___. Could you tell me ___? Thank you for ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with no frame, making the same request once to an office and once to a friend, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a formal request with greeting, reason, question and thanks; polite modals (could, would) and ""I am calling about""; appointment, I would like to ask and thank you for. Home language: a student may script the call in the home language first; the teacher names the false friend that can trip Spanish speakers (realizar means carry out, not realize). Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 11 the information is asked of adults outside class, so polite modals and a set call order are new; the grade 10 briefing frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: informal English and the home language are not wrong; the task is choosing for the listener. Teacher note: a student may speak as an invented applicant; no task asks about documents, aid eligibility or a family's situation."
si,11,g11-si-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Explain,g10-si-explain,g12-si-explain,cause chain,cause chain,the first step is; which allows; depends on; deadline; for example,ela:e11-seminar (class discussion rules and turns),"Students explain a process (applying for a program, a school procedure) to a younger student and to an adult. Entry points (all explain a process, applying for a program or a school procedure, to a younger student and to an adult): levels 1 and 2 with the process on a cause chain, a word bank and the cloze ""First you ___. Then ___, so ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""The first step is to ___, which allows ___."" for the adult and ""First you ___"" for the younger student, off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining one step that depends on another, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a process explained for two listeners; dense noun groups for the adult (""the application deadline for the summer program"") and cause verbs (allows, depends on); the first step is, which allows and depends on. Home language: a student may outline the process in the home language first; the teacher names that a form one applies with is usually solicitud in Spanish, while aplicaciA3n often means an app. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 11 the process is explained up and down in age, so register moves both ways; the grade 10 project frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: informal English and the home language are not wrong; the task is choosing for the listener. Teacher note: a student may speak as an invented applicant; no task asks about documents, aid eligibility or a family's situation."
si,11,g11-si-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Argue,g10-si-argue,g12-si-argue,claim chart,claim chart,we are asking for; we understand that; in return; however; objection,ela:e11-seminar (class discussion rules and turns),"Students make a case to an adult (for a class change, a club request) and answer objections, in both formal and informal registers. Entry points (all make a case to an adult, for a class change or a club request, and answer objections, in a formal and an informal register): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of reasons and likely objections, a word bank and the cloze ""We are asking for ___ because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""We understand that ___. However, ___."" and an objection card, off before the end-of-unit case; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering an objection they did not plan for, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a request case with claim, reasons, an answered objection and an ask; concession and rebuttal moves with hedging and boosting modals; we are asking for, we understand that and in return. Home language: a student may plan the case in the home language first; the teacher names the cognate objeciA3n and objection. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 11 the listener is an adult who can say no, so answering objections in a formal register is new; the grade 10 seminar frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: informal English and the home language are not wrong; the task is choosing for the listener."
la,11,g11-la-narrate,~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Narrate.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Narrate.Expressive,g10-la-narrate,g12-la-narrate,story map,story map,looking back; I realize; at first I thought; reflection; structure,"ela:e11-narrative (narrative writing); ela:e11-lit-structure (reading literature, same text)","Students take apart one passage of the ELA unit's novel or story where the author makes a structural choice, and the reflective language that carries it. They then build a reflective narrative scene, a personal or invented account, with a chosen structure, with the teacher first and then alone. The full narrative stays in ELA. Entry points (all analyze an author's structural choice and write a reflective scene with a chosen structure): levels 1 and 2 with the story map of the passage, a word bank and the cloze ""At first I thought ___. Now I see ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with reflective frames (""Looking back, I realize ___."") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, naming why they chose that structure, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 contrast cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a reflective scene with a chosen structure; tense shifts on purpose and reflective language (""looking back, I realize""); I realize, at first I thought and reflection. Home language: a student may draft the reflection in the home language first; reflexiA3n and reflection are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 reflective narrative and an author's choices are added; in grade 12 the reflective cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed."
la,11,g11-la-inform,~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Inform.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Inform.Expressive,g10-la-inform,g12-la-inform,text map,text map,most sources agree; one source adds; for a reader; reader; dense,"ela:e11-informative (informative writing); ela:e11-info-structure (reading informational, same text); ela:e11-syntax-effect (forms of English)","Students take apart one passage of the ELA unit text: how its structure makes a point clear, and how dense noun groups pack information. They then build an informative paragraph that synthesizes several sources for a named reader, with the teacher first and then alone. The full piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all analyze how an ELA text's structure makes points clear and write a paragraph that synthesizes several sources for a named reader): levels 1 and 2 with the sources on a text map, a word bank and the cloze ""Most sources agree that ___. One source adds ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with synthesis and reader frames (""For a reader new to ___, ___."") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, choosing the structure for the named reader, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 synthesis cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a synthesis paragraph for a named reader; nominalization and quotations worked into a sentence; most sources agree, for a reader and in addition. Home language: a student may group the sources in the home language first; fuente and source are not cognates, so the word card carries it. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 several sources and a named reader are added; in grade 12 the reader frame is gone for levels 3 and 4."
la,11,g11-la-argue,~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Argue.Expressive,g10-la-argue,g12-la-argue,claim chart,claim chart,it is clear that; audience; tone; clearly; must,ela:e11-argument (opinion or argument writing); ela:e11-info-rhetoric (reading reasons and evidence),"Students take apart one paragraph of a speech or essay from the ELA unit: how its rhetoric reaches its audience, and how hedging and boosting modals set the tone. They then build one argument paragraph for a chosen audience, adjusting tone, with the teacher first and then alone. The full argument stays in ELA. Entry points (all analyze rhetoric in an ELA speech or essay and write a paragraph for a chosen audience, adjusting tone): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart and an audience card, a word bank and the cloze ""For ___, I would say ___ because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with hedging and boosting frames (""It is clear that ___. This may ___."") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, writing the same claim for two audiences, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 rebuttal cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument paragraph for a chosen audience; hedging and boosting modals (may, clearly, must) and evaluative words; it is clear that, audience and tone. Home language: a student may plan the audience shift in the home language first; audiencia and audience are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 audience and tone are added; in grade 12 the hedging cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
ma,11,g11-ma-explain,~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Explain.Expressive,g10-ma-explain,g12-ma-explain,cause chain,cause chain,function model; periodic; amplitude; the model suggests; may; results in,math:alg2-combining-functions-models (function models); math:alg2-periodic-modeling (periodic models),"Students explain how they built a function model and a periodic model from data in Algebra II. Entry points (all explain how they built a function model and a periodic model from data, in Algebra II): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled graph and data, a word bank and the cloze ""The amplitude shows ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""The model may ___, which suggests ___. The period results in ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the model only, explaining the choice of model and its limits, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 ""I used"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of a model choice and its limits; dense noun groups and hedging (may, suggests); amplitude, period and the model suggests. Home language: a student may talk through the model in the home language first; amplitud and amplitude, periA3dico and periodic are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 students explain model choices and their limits; in grade 12 the amplitude cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
ma,11,g11-ma-argue,~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Argue.Expressive,g10-ma-argue,g12-ma-argue,claim chart,claim chart,significant; margin of error; might; the evidence suggests; limitation; although,math:alg2-experiments-significance (significance claims); math:alg2-sample-estimates-margin (margin of error),"Students argue whether a result is significant and what a margin of error allows, from study data. Entry points (all argue whether a result is significant and what a margin of error allows, from study data): levels 1 and 2 with the study data on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""The result is significant because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""The evidence suggests ___, although the margin of error ___. This might ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, critiquing another claim about the same study, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 ""given, so"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument about uncertainty with its limits; hedging modals (might, may) and concession; significant, margin of error and limitation. Home language: a student may weigh the study in the home language first; significativo and significant, margen de error and margin of error are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 claims about uncertainty are added; in grade 12 the significance cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
sc,11,g11-sc-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Explain.Expressive,g10-sc-explain,g12-sc-explain,cause chain,cause chain,orbit; is proportional to; signal; contributes to; may; model,science:phys-orbits (host investigation); science:phys-wave-technologies (host investigation),"Students explain orbits and how wave technologies carry information, in Physics. Entry points (all explain orbits and how wave technologies carry information, in Physics): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled model, a word bank and the cloze ""The force of gravity ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""The force is proportional to ___, which contributes to ___."", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, using a mathematical model and naming a limit of it, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 rate cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation with a mathematical model; dense noun groups and cause verbs with hedging; orbit, signal and is proportional to. Home language: a student may explain the model in the home language first; A3rbita and orbit, seA�al and signal share a root but look different, so the card carries both. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 explanations use mathematical models; in grade 12 the gravity cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
sc,11,g11-sc-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Argue.Expressive,g10-sc-argue,g12-sc-argue,claim chart,claim chart,reliable; published claim; rebuttal; might; although; limitation,science:phys-wave-particle-light (host investigation); science:phys-em-radiation-effects (judging published claims),"Students argue whether light is a wave or a particle and whether a published claim about radiation's effects holds up, in Physics, answering a rebuttal. Entry points (all argue whether light is a wave or a particle, or whether a published claim about radiation's effects holds up, answering a rebuttal): levels 1 and 2 with the evidence on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""The claim is (not) reliable because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Although ___ might ___, the evidence shows ___."" and a rebuttal frame, off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering a rebuttal and naming a limit of the evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 ""clearly shows"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument that judges a claim and answers a rebuttal; hedging and boosting modals with rebuttal moves; reliable, published claim and rebuttal. Home language: a student may weigh the claim in the home language first; the teacher names the false friend that can trip Spanish speakers (actual means current, not actual). Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 rebuttal is added; in grade 12 the ""reliable"" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4."
ss,11,g11-ss-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Explain.Expressive,g10-ss-explain,g12-ss-explain,cause chain,cause chain,sectional; tension; continuity; resulted in; over time; while,social-studies:us1-market-revolution (host unit); social-studies:us1-sectional-crisis (host unit),"Students explain the market revolution and the causes of the sectional crisis, in US History I. Entry points (all explain the market revolution and the causes of the sectional crisis, tracing continuity and change): levels 1 and 2 with the causes on the cause chain, a word bank and the cloze ""Tension grew because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""While ___ stayed the same, ___ changed, which resulted in ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, weighing causes, citing sources and naming a limit of the evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 ""resulted in"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of continuity and change; dense noun groups and cause verbs with hedging; continuity, tension and resulted in. Home language: a student may sort the causes in the home language first; continuidad and continuity, tensiA3n and tension are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 explanations trace continuity and change; in grade 12 the tension cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: the sectional crisis host is about slavery and the coming war; the family note previews it and offers the market revolution host, slavery is never role-played, and no student is asked to speak for a group."
ss,11,g11-ss-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Argue.Expressive,g10-ss-argue,g12-ss-argue,claim chart,claim chart,reliable; news source; bias; claims; corroborate; although,social-studies:us1-argument-writing (host unit); social-studies:us1-news-reading (host unit),"Students write a historical argument and judge a news source's claims, in US History I. Entry points (all write a historical argument and judge a news source's claims, in US History I): levels 1 and 2 with the source on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""The source claims ___. It is (not) reliable because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Although ___ claims ___, other sources show ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, corroborating sources and naming a limit of the evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 ""historians disagree"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument that judges a source's claims; concession, reporting verbs and hedging; reliable, bias and corroborate. Home language: a student may read news in the home language too and compare how outlets report a story, by choice; fiable and reliable are not cognates, so the word card carries it. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 judging claims in the news is added; in grade 12 the ""reliable"" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: the news host may touch immigration enforcement; pick pieces on other topics, or offer one of several."
si,12,g12-si-narrate,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Narrate,g11-si-narrate,,story map,story map,at the time; I was responsible for; as a result; I learned; looking back,ela:e12-seminar (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e12-presentation (presenting in order to an audience),"Students recount an experience for a college, job or program interview, then retell it in an email, choosing register for each. Entry points (all recount an experience for a college, job or program interview, then retell it in an email, choosing the register for each): levels 1 and 2 with the story map in three parts, a model answer to change, a word bank and the cloze ""At the time, I ___. I was responsible for ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""At the time, ___. I was responsible for ___. As a result, ___. Looking back, ___."" for the interview and a model email to change, off before the end-of-unit recount; 5 and 6 with the story map only, writing the email from their spoken answer, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an interview answer and an email of the same experience; tense shifts on purpose and reflective language (""looking back""); at the time, I was responsible for and I learned. Home language: a student may plan the answer in the home language first; the interviewer and the email reader get English. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 12 the audiences are adults outside class, so the same recount must work aloud and in writing; the grade 11 situation-action-result cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed. Teacher note: a student may speak as an invented applicant; no task asks about documents, aid eligibility or a family's situation."
si,12,g12-si-inform,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Inform,g11-si-inform,,text map,text map,I am writing to ask; regarding; would it be possible; I would appreciate; please let me know,ela:e12-seminar (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e12-presentation (telling and describing aloud),"Students write and say information for adult settings (an email to a teacher, a request to an office, an orientation for younger students). Entry points (all write a short request to a school office and then say it aloud): levels 1 and 2 with a model message to copy and change, a bank of request phrases and a rehearsal with the teacher, the copying off before the check and the phrase bank kept; 3 and 4 with frames for greeting, request and thanks (""I am writing to ask about ___. Could you ___?""), off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with no frame, writing the same request once for a classmate and once for an office. Forms: a short formal message with greeting, request, detail and thanks; polite modals and nominalization (""regarding the change in schedule""); regarding, would it be possible and please let me know. Home language: a student may draft the request in the home language first and then write it in English; no student writes or translates a message for a family. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 12 the audiences are adults outside class, so a written request joins the spoken one; the grade 11 call script is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: a student may speak as an invented applicant; no task asks about documents, aid eligibility or a family's situation."
si,12,g12-si-explain,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Explain,g11-si-explain,,cause chain,cause chain,my plan is to; the main reason; in order to; one option is; so that,ela:e12-seminar (class discussion rules and turns),"Students explain a plan, their own or an invented student's, for a project, a program or the year after high school, and the reasons for it, in talk and in a short written statement. Entry points (all explain a plan and the reasons for it, aloud and then in a short written statement): levels 1 and 2 with the plan on a cause chain, a word bank and the cloze ""My plan is to ___ so that ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""My plan is to ___. The main reason is ___. In order to ___, I will ___."", off before the end-of-unit statement; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, weighing one option against another in writing, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a plan statement with the goal, the steps and the reasons; purpose clauses (""in order to"", ""so that"") and hedging (may, might); my plan is to, one option is and the main reason. Home language: a student may plan in the home language first; the teacher names the cognates opciA3n and option, razA3n and reason. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 12 the audiences are adults outside class, so the plan is spoken and then written; the grade 11 two-listener frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: a student may speak as an invented applicant; no task asks about documents, aid eligibility or a family's situation."
si,12,g12-si-argue,~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Argue,g11-si-argue,,claim chart,claim chart,"I propose; I understand that; however; that is a fair point, but; I still believe",ela:e12-seminar (class discussion rules and turns),"Students lead a discussion and argue for a proposal in a school or community setting, conceding a point and holding their main claim. Entry points (all lead a discussion and argue for a proposal in a school or community setting, conceding a point and holding their main claim): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of the proposal, a model opening to change, a word bank and the cloze ""I propose ___ because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""I propose ___. I understand that ___; however, ___. I still believe ___."", off before the end-of-unit discussion; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, conceding a fair point aloud and holding the claim, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a proposal with claim, reasons, a conceded point and a close; concession and rebuttal (""that is a fair point, but"") with evaluative words; I propose, I understand that and I still believe. Home language: a student may plan the proposal in the home language first; the teacher names the cognate proponer and propose. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 12 students lead in a school or community setting, so conceding without giving up the claim is the target; the grade 11 objection card is gone for levels 3 and 4."
la,12,g12-la-narrate,WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Narrate.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Narrate.Expressive,g11-la-narrate,,story map,story map,turning point; I came to see; reader; revise; resolution,"ela:e12-narrative (narrative writing); ela:e12-lit-structure (reading literature, same text)","Students take apart one passage of a text from the ELA unit where structure and tone build meaning. They then write a polished narrative for a real reader, a personal statement or a story on a topic the student chooses, real or invented, revising voice for that reader, with the teacher first and then alone. The full piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all analyze how structure and tone build meaning in an ELA text and write a polished narrative for a real reader): levels 1 and 2 with the story map, a model passage to change, a word bank and the cloze ""It began when ___. That was the turning point."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with turning-point and reflective frames off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, revising voice for the named reader, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 11 reflective cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a polished narrative with a structure and tone chosen for a reader; tense shifts on purpose, reflective language and tone words; turning point, I came to see and resolution. Model: story map at the 9 to 12 stage, structure across time. Home language: a student may draft in the home language first, and a story may carry words from it on purpose, as published writers do. Owner year for LA 9-12. Both modes for 9-12: analyzing complex narrative structure and voice, and writing narrative with structure and tone chosen for a reader. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed. Teacher note: no student is asked or nudged to write about migration, loss or hardship."
la,12,g12-la-inform,WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Inform.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Inform.Expressive,g11-la-inform,,text map,text map,refers to; defined as; formal register; synthesize; whereas,"ela:e12-informative (informative writing); ela:e12-info-structure (reading informational, same text); ela:e12-syntax-effect (forms of English)","Students take apart one section of a complex ELA unit text: how it defines a term and how its formal register works. They then build one informative section that defines terms, synthesizes sources and keeps a formal register, with the teacher first and then alone. The full report stays in ELA. Entry points (all evaluate a complex ELA text's structure and write a formal section that defines terms and synthesizes sources): levels 1 and 2 with the text map of the sources, a word bank and the cloze ""___ refers to ___. Both sources ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with definition and synthesis frames (""___, defined here as ___, ___."") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, controlling a complex structure for a named reader, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 11 reader frame is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a formal section that defines, synthesizes and concludes; synthesis, nominalization and quotations worked into a sentence; refers to, defined as and formal register. Model: text map at the 9 to 12 stage, two or more sources synthesized. Home language: a student may outline in the home language first; the teacher names Latin-root cognates common in formal register (definir and define, sintetizar and synthesize). Owner year for LA 9-12. Both modes for 9-12: analyzing complex informational structure and writing synthesis in a formal register."
la,12,g12-la-argue,WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Argue.Expressive,g11-la-argue,,claim chart,claim chart,granted; nevertheless; admittedly; rebuttal; concede,ela:e12-argument (opinion or argument writing); ela:e12-info-rhetoric (reading reasons and evidence),"Students take apart one paragraph of a seminal speech or essay from the ELA unit: how it rebuts and concedes. They then build one argument paragraph with a rebuttal and a concession for a real audience, with the teacher first and then alone. The full argument stays in ELA. Entry points (all analyze how a seminal ELA speech persuades and write a paragraph with a rebuttal and a concession for a real audience): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""Granted, ___. Still, ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with concession and rebuttal frames (""Admittedly, ___; nevertheless, ___."") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, naming the limits of their own evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 11 hedging cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument paragraph with a concession and a rebuttal; concession (""Although ___, ___""), hedging and boosting modals and rebuttal moves; granted, nevertheless and admittedly. Model: claim chart at the 9 to 12 stage, rebuttal and the limits of the evidence. Home language: a student may plan the rebuttal in the home language first; the teacher names the cognate concesiA3n and concession. Owner year for LA 9-12. Both modes for 9-12: analyzing rhetoric and writing argument with rebuttal for an audience."
ma,12,g12-ma-explain,WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Explain.Expressive,g11-ma-explain,,cause chain,cause chain,method; in order to; rate of change; may suggest; the first step is; results in,"math:stat-investigation-project (investigation explained); math:calc-related-rates (related rates explained, Calculus); math:pc-trig-equations (solutions explained, Precalculus); math:alg2-combining-functions-models (if no grade 12 math course)","Students explain a method in the grade 12 math course they take: a statistical investigation (question, data, method, conclusion) in Statistics, a related rates solution in Calculus, or the solutions of a trigonometric equation in Precalculus; a student in no grade 12 math course uses the Algebra II modeling row. Entry points (all explain a method from their own grade 12 math course, step by step, so another student could follow it): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled work and a model explanation to change, a word bank and the cloze ""In order to ___, I ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""In order to ___, the first step is ___, which results in ___. This may ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with no frame, explaining the method so a partner could use it on a new problem, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 11 amplitude cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a method explanation from goal to conclusion; dense noun groups, cause verbs (results in) and hedging (may suggest); in order to, rate of change and method. Model: cause chain at the 9 to 12 stage, weighted causes (which step matters most to the result). Home language: a student may talk through the method in the home language first; mActodo and method, tasa de cambio and rate of change are close. Owner year for MA 9-12. Both modes for 9-12: following and giving explanations of methods and models."
ma,12,g12-ma-argue,WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Argue.Expressive,g11-ma-argue,,claim chart,claim chart,conclude; assumption; strong evidence; although; might; justified,"math:stat-test-proportion (test conclusions); math:calc-mean-value-extrema (justified extrema, Calculus); math:pc-exp-log-models (which model fits, Precalculus); math:alg2-experiments-significance (if no grade 12 math course)","Students argue a conclusion in the grade 12 math course they take: from a statistical test or an expected-value decision in Statistics, a justified extreme value in Calculus, or which model fits data and how far it can be trusted in Precalculus; a student in no grade 12 math course uses the Algebra II significance row. Entry points (all argue a conclusion from their own grade 12 math course and critique another conclusion): levels 1 and 2 with the work on the claim chart and a model conclusion to change, a word bank and the cloze ""We conclude ___ because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Although ___, the evidence is strong that ___. One limitation is ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, critiquing another conclusion and naming the assumptions behind it, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 11 significance cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argued conclusion with its assumptions and limits; concession, hedging and boosting modals; conclude, assumption and strong evidence. Model: claim chart at the 9 to 12 stage, rebuttal and the limits of the evidence. Home language: a student may weigh the conclusion in the home language first; concluir and conclude, suposiciA3n and assumption are close. Owner year for MA 9-12. Both modes for 9-12: making and critiquing arguments with assumptions and evidence."
sc,12,g12-sc-explain,WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Explain.Expressive,g11-sc-explain,,cause chain,cause chain,circulation; is driven by; contributes to; system; may; water quality,science:ess-ocean-circulation (host investigation); science:env-water-quality (host investigation); science:phys-orbits (if no grade 12 science course),"Students explain ocean circulation or water quality in the grade 12 science course; a student in no grade 12 science course uses the grade 11 Physics row. Entry points (all explain ocean circulation or water quality in the grade 12 science course, or orbits in the grade 11 Physics row for a student in no grade 12 science course): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled system model, a word bank and the cloze ""The current is driven by ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""___ is driven by ___, which contributes to ___. This may ___."", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, weighing several causes, citing sources and naming a limit, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 11 gravity cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of a complex system with weighted causes; dense noun groups and cause verbs (is driven by, contributes to) with hedging; circulation, system and may. Model: cause chain at the 9 to 12 stage, weighted, multi-factor causes. Home language: a student may explain the model in the home language first; circulaciA3n and circulation, sistema and system are cognates. Owner year for SC 9-12. Both modes for 9-12: following and giving explanations of complex systems."
sc,12,g12-sc-argue,WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Argue.Expressive,g11-sc-argue,,claim chart,claim chart,trade-off; uncertainty; clearly; although; limitation; might,science:env-energy-transition (host investigation); science:ess-climate-model-test (host investigation); science:phys-wave-particle-light (if no grade 12 science course),"Students argue about energy choices or a climate model's test in the grade 12 science course; a student in no grade 12 science course uses the grade 11 Physics row. Entry points (all argue about an energy choice or a climate model's test, or about light in the grade 11 Physics row for a student in no grade 12 science course): levels 1 and 2 with the evidence on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze ""The best choice is ___ because ___."", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with ""Although ___, the trade-off favors ___. One uncertainty is ___."", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering a rebuttal and naming the limits of the evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 11 ""reliable"" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument with evidence, a trade-off, limits and a rebuttal; concession, hedging and boosting modals and evaluative words; trade-off, uncertainty and clearly. Model: claim chart at the 9 to 12 stage, rebuttal and the limits of the evidence. Home language: a student may weigh the choice in the home language first; incertidumbre is not a cognate of uncertainty, so the word card carries it. Owner year for SC 9-12. Both modes for 9-12: argument with evidence, limits and rebuttal."
