9: ## The course in brief
10: 
11: - **Units.** 36: 9 in grade 9 (units 1 to 9), 9 in grade 10 (units 10 to 18), 9 in grade 11 (units 19 to 27), 9 in
12:   grade 12 (units 28 to 36). One unit per content row of the progression: each year has nine (LA Narrate, Inform
13:   and Argue; MA, SC and SS Explain and Argue), since cluster 9-12 gives ELD-MA, ELD-SC and ELD-SS no Narrate or
14:   Inform id. The four SI rows of each year ride inside the content unit whose key language use they share. Each unit
15:   is built around one key language use, in both modes. SI in every unit (bar item 1; STANDARD section 3): the unit's
16:   own SI row, or one SI use named as review in What students learn (units 3, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 16, 18, 21,
17:   24, 25, 26, 27, 29, 30, 34, 35 and 36), each with its talk move on the host content and its look-for.
18: - **Days.** 640: 160 a year for four years. Each year: 154 days in units and 6 for two course-level checks (two
19:   check days and one respond day each). One lesson of 45 minutes a day, five a week, 32 weeks, the protected daily
20:   ELD period settled in STANDARD.md section 2 and APPROACH.md "Pacing" (120 hours a year). It is not ELA time and
21:   does not stand in for ELA (bar item 8). The 45 minutes hold the talk set-up, handing out and collecting cards,
22:   frames and drafts, and the exit ticket; there is no lab or field clean-up, since ELD runs no investigation. Each
23:   unit's days include its end-of-unit assessment (and, in units over 25 days, its mid-unit assessments): no unit is
24:   over 25 days, so none holds a mid-unit assessment. Every unit's end-of-unit assessment is two days (a sitting with
25:   unseen items: an interpretive part on a new grade-level text or task of the same kind, then a talk task heard by
26:   the teacher in a small seminar and a writing task, at each entry point), followed by one respond day inside the
27:   unit. Days follow the host windows first: each unit runs in the weeks its host is taught, and the two checks sit
28:   in fixed weeks, so a unit's length is mostly the gap between hosts. Within those windows the load sets the rest:
