165:   eyes. Where a host unit's data came from a lab, the host frame's safety sheet and safety read cover it.
166: - **Grade 12 calendar.** The last weeks hold teaching: unit 36 runs days 137 to 157 and the year-end check sits in
167:   week 32 (days 158 to 160), the finals week. If seniors leave early, the year-end check and its respond day move to
168:   the seniors' last three days, and unit 36 folds up to 5 days, in this order, never its end-of-unit assessment or
169:   respond day: its Fluency days on review forms (2), the second change-of-audience day (1), and the second
170:   independent application day (2). A school whose seniors leave more than 5 days early places the 160 course days
171:   so they end on the seniors' last day (CALENDAR.md), rather than cutting the unit (For the lead, item 5).
172: - **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md,
173:   kind `sensitivity`, community: multilingual learners and immigrant families), and every Spanish note and family
174:   note gets a `spanish` read (APPROACH, "Sensitive ground", Cory 2026-11-21). Before reading, the agent studies the
175:   APPROACH page's Sensitive ground rules, the federal English learner toolkit (US Department of Education, Office of
176:   English Language Acquisition, free online, https://www.ncela.ed.gov/educator-support/toolkits/english-learner-toolkit),
177:   the National Academies 2017 report on children and youth learning English (free to read online), and, for units
178:   whose host is about war, conquest or loss, the National Child Traumatic Stress Network's free guide for educators
179:   (https://www.nctsn.org/resources/child-trauma-toolkit-educators). Flagged units name more below: unit 1 (death and
180:   loss in suspense stories), units 8 and 11 (conquest, migration and colonial rule), unit 13 (stories of colonial
181:   rule), unit 18 (war and its peace, retrieved), unit 23 (slavery and the coming of the Civil War), unit 24
182:   (Reconstruction and its overthrow), units 1, 13, 19, 28 and 36 (own-story and own-plan prompts), units 19 to 23
183:   and 28 to 31 (applicant and plan prompts: invented applicants allowed), unit 32 (policy debate). The newcomer strand
184:   and all family notes get the read before use. No outside human reader is planned (Cory, 2026-11-14); the course's
185:   checks get the answer-key agent's read.
