g9-si-narrate
strand: si
grade: 9
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Narrate
assumes: g8-si-narrate
hands_on: g10-si-narrate
model_in: story map
model_out: story map
words: during the meeting; the group discussed; looking back; at the meeting; I changed
cross_subject: ela:e9-sl-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e9-sl-presentation (presenting in order to an audience)
notes: Students recount an event (a team meeting, an orientation day) for a peer and then for an adult, noticing what they change between the two tellings. Entry points (all recount one event, a team meeting or an orientation day, for a peer and then for an adult, and say what they changed): levels 1 and 2 with the story map and two audience cards, a word bank and the cloze "Yesterday we had ___. First ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "At the meeting, we ___." for the peer and "During the meeting, the group ___." for the adult, off before the end-of-unit recount; 5 and 6 with the story map only, naming two changes they made for the adult, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: one recount told twice, for a peer and for an adult; tense shifts on purpose and reflective language ("looking back, I"); during, at the meeting and the group discussed. Model: story map at the 9 to 12 stage, structure across time. Home language: a student may notice how the home language changes for an elder or a teacher and use that as a way into English register; this is offered, never asked. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 9 seminar talk and a register for adults join peer talk, so the same event is told two ways; the grade 8 newsletter frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed. Teacher note: informal English and the home language are not wrong; the task is choosing for the listener.

g9-si-inform
strand: si
grade: 9
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Inform
assumes: g8-si-inform
hands_on: g10-si-inform
model_in: text map
model_out: text map
words: the seminar focused on; both sides agreed; whereas; I was wondering whether; main point
cross_subject: ela:e9-sl-discussion (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e9-sl-presentation (telling and describing aloud)
notes: Students summarize a seminar's main points for a classmate who missed it and ask a teacher for information in a formal register. Entry points (all sum up a seminar's main points for a classmate who missed it and ask a teacher for information in a formal register): levels 1 and 2 with a text map of the seminar's points, a word bank and the cloze "The main point was ___. Could you tell me ___?", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "The seminar focused on ___. Both sides agreed that ___. I was wondering whether ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, writing the summary once for the classmate and once as a note to the teacher, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a summary of a discussion by main point; synthesis ("both sides agreed", "whereas") and the polite question "I was wondering whether"; focused on, main point and I was wondering. Model: text map at the 9 to 12 stage, two or more sources (speakers) synthesized. Home language: a student may take seminar notes in any language; the teacher names the cognate seminario and seminar. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 9 the information comes from a seminar of several speakers, and a formal question to a teacher joins; the grade 8 briefing frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: informal English and the home language are not wrong; the task is choosing for the listener.

g9-si-explain
strand: si
grade: 9
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Explain
assumes: g8-si-explain
hands_on: g10-si-explain
model_in: cause chain
model_out: cause chain
words: my reasoning was; which suggests; What led you to; it seems; may
cross_subject: ela:e9-sl-discussion (class discussion rules and turns)
notes: Students explain their reasoning aloud in seminar ("I said that because...") and ask others to explain theirs. Entry points (all explain their reasoning aloud in seminar and ask others to explain theirs): levels 1 and 2 with their reasoning on a cause chain, a word bank and the cloze "I said that because ___. Why do you think ___?", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "My reasoning was that ___, which suggests ___. What led you to ___?", off before the end-of-unit seminar; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, hedging a point they are not sure of, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a reasoning turn that gives the point and then the chain behind it; hedging (may, suggests, it seems) and cause verbs (led to, results in); my reasoning, suggests and what led you. Model: cause chain at the 9 to 12 stage, weighted causes (which reason counts most). Home language: a student may think the reasoning through in the home language before speaking; the teacher names the cognate razonamiento and reasoning. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 9 the explanation is of one's own thinking in a seminar, so hedging is new; the grade 8 conflict frame is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g9-si-argue
strand: si
grade: 9
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Argue
assumes: g8-si-argue
hands_on: g10-si-argue
model_in: claim chart
model_out: claim chart
words: although; I still think; That changes my view; convinced me; building on
cross_subject: ela:e9-sl-discussion (class discussion rules and turns)
notes: Students take part in a seminar: state a view, build on or question others and change their view when given a good reason. Entry points (all take part in a seminar: state a view, build on or question others and change their view when given a good reason): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of the seminar question, a word bank and the cloze "I think ___. I agree with ___ because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Although ___, I still think ___." and "That changes my view because ___.", off before the end-of-unit seminar; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, saying what reason changed their view, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a seminar turn that states, builds on, questions or revises a view; concession ("Although ___, ___") and "That changes my view"; although, I still think and convinced. Model: claim chart at the 9 to 12 stage, rebuttal and the limits of the evidence. Home language: a student may prepare a view in the home language first; the teacher names the cognate convencer and convince. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 9 changing one's view for a good reason is taught as a strength; the grade 8 leader's script is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: informal English and the home language are not wrong; the task is choosing for the listener.

g9-la-narrate
strand: la
grade: 9
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Narrate.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Narrate.Expressive
assumes: g8-la-narrate
hands_on: g10-la-narrate
model_in: story map
model_out: story map
words: time shift; back then; even now; parallel; voice
cross_subject: ela:e9-w-narrative (narrative writing); ela:e9-rl-structure-time (reading literature, same text)
notes: Students take apart one passage of the ELA unit's story where time shifts, flashback or a parallel plot, and the tense changes that signal it. They then build a narrative scene with one time shift, with the teacher first and then alone. The full narrative stays in ELA. Entry points (all study how time is ordered in an ELA story and write a scene with one time shift): levels 1 and 2 with the story map of the two time lines, audio of the passage, a word bank and the cloze "Now ___. Years earlier, ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with tense-shift frames ("Even now, ___. Back then, ___.") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, shaping the shift on purpose and naming its effect, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a scene with one time shift; tense shifts on purpose and reflective language ("looking back"); back then, even now and time shift. Home language: a student may draft the scene in the home language first; the teacher names how Spanish and English both shift tense to show memory. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 9 structure across time is the target, with a grade 9 text and audio support; in grade 12, the owner year, the tense-shift frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed.

g9-la-inform
strand: la
grade: 9
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Inform.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Inform.Expressive
assumes: g8-la-inform
hands_on: g10-la-inform
model_in: text map
model_out: text map
words: develops; whereas; which; the development of; analyze
cross_subject: ela:e9-w-informative (informative writing); ela:e9-ri-unfolding-ideas (reading informational, same text); ela:e9-l-phrases-clauses (forms of English)
notes: Students take apart one passage of the ELA unit text where an idea unfolds, and the clauses and phrases that join and define it ("which", "whereas"). They trace how the ideas unfold, then build one analytical paragraph that links ideas with clause and phrase choices, with the teacher first and then alone. The full analysis stays in ELA. Entry points (all trace how ideas unfold in an ELA text and write an analytical paragraph that links them): levels 1 and 2 with the text map of the passage, a word bank and the cloze "At first the writer ___. Later ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with clause frames ("___, which ___, ___." "Whereas ___, ___.") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, keeping a formal tone for a named reader, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an analytical paragraph that traces an idea; nominalization ("the development of") and clauses that join and define (which, whereas); whereas, develops and analyze. Home language: a student may outline the analysis in the home language first; analizar and analyze are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 9 the work is analysis of a whole text's development; in grade 12 the clause frame is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g9-la-argue
strand: la
grade: 9
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Argue.Expressive
assumes: g8-la-argue
hands_on: g10-la-argue
model_in: claim chart
model_out: claim chart
words: although; reasoning; precise; might; concede
cross_subject: ela:e9-w-argument (opinion or argument writing); ela:e9-ri-argument-evaluate (reading reasons and evidence)
notes: Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit text and evaluate its reasoning, marking concession ("Although ___") and its modals. They then build one argument paragraph with a precise claim and an answered counterclaim, with the teacher first and then alone. The full argument stays in ELA. Entry points (all evaluate an ELA argument's reasoning and write a paragraph with a precise claim and an answered counterclaim): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart built with a partner, a word bank and the cloze "The claim is ___. Although ___, ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with concession and reasoning frames off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, weighing the counterclaim and choosing tone for the audience, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an argument paragraph with a precise claim and an answered counterclaim; concession ("Although ___, ___") and hedging modals (may, might); although, reasoning and precise. Home language: a student may plan the argument in the home language first; aunque and although are not cognates, so the word card carries it. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 9 evaluating reasoning is the target; in grade 12 the concession cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g9-ma-explain
strand: ma
grade: 9
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Explain.Expressive
assumes: g8-ma-explain
