model_out: cause chain
words: is equivalent to; justify each step; intercept; inverse operation; therefore; slope
cross_subject: math:alg1-equation-reasoning (reasoning with equations); math:alg1-linear-equation-graphs (graphs explained)
notes: Students explain each step of solving an equation and what a graph shows, in Algebra I. Entry points (all explain each step of solving an equation and what a graph shows, in Algebra I): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled equation and graph, a word bank and the cloze "This step is true because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Since ___, it follows that ___. The intercept means ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the model only, justifying each step with precise terms, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 12, the owner year, the "this step" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a step-by-step justification of a solution; dense noun groups ("the y-intercept of the line") and cause verbs; is equivalent to, intercept and inverse operation. Home language: a student may talk through the steps in the home language first; equivalente and equivalent, operación inversa and inverse operation are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 9 explanations justify each step of an equation; in grade 12 the "this step" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g9-ma-argue
strand: ma
grade: 9
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Argue.Expressive
assumes: g8-ma-argue
hands_on: g10-ma-argue
model_in: claim chart
model_out: claim chart
words: correlation; causation; does not prove; although; may; strong evidence
cross_subject: math:alg1-correlation-causation (correlation claims); math:alg1-linear-vs-exponential (growth claims)
notes: Students argue whether a correlation shows causation and whether data are linear or exponential, citing the data. Entry points (all argue whether a correlation shows causation and whether data are linear or exponential, citing the data): levels 1 and 2 with the graphs on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "The data shows ___, but it does not prove ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Although ___, this may ___. The data is strong evidence that ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, critiquing another claim using definitions, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 12 the "does not prove" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument from data that marks what it can and cannot show; concession and hedging modals (may, might); correlation, causation and does not prove. Home language: a student may weigh the data in the home language first; correlación and correlation, causalidad and causation are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 9 claims rest on data and definitions; in grade 12 the "does not prove" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.

