notes: Students explain how geography shaped contact between societies and what changed in the Scientific Revolution, in World History I. Entry points (all explain how geography shaped contact between societies and what changed in the Scientific Revolution, weighing factors in context): levels 1 and 2 with the factors on the cause chain, a word bank and the cloze "Geography contributed to ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "In the context of ___, ___ contributed to ___. This may ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, weighing several factors, citing sources and naming a limit of the evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; in grade 12, the owner year, the "contributed to" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation that weighs factors in context; dense noun groups and cause verbs (contributed to) with hedging; context, contact and may. Home language: a student may sort the factors in the home language first; contexto and context, geografía and geography are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 9 explanations use context and several factors; in grade 12 the "contributed to" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: the host is about migration and conquest; the family note previews it and offers the Scientific Revolution host, and no student is asked about their own family's movement.

