words: the update covers; regarding; to answer your question; the change in; three points
cross_subject: ela:e10-sl-seminar (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e10-sl-presentation (telling and describing aloud)
notes: Students give a short informational briefing (a club update, a school event) and answer audience questions. Entry points (all give a short informational briefing, a club update or a school event, and answer audience questions): levels 1 and 2 with the briefing on a text map, a word bank and the cloze "The event is on ___. Students can ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "The update covers three points: ___. Regarding ___, ___." and a question card, off before the end-of-unit briefing; 5 and 6 with the text map only, answering an unplanned question, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a briefing with a preview of its points and a close; nominalization ("the change in schedule") and quotations worked into a sentence ("the coach said that"); regarding, covers and to answer your question. Home language: a student may rehearse the briefing in the home language first; the teacher names the cognate información and information. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 10 the audience asks questions the speaker did not plan for; the grade 9 seminar frame is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g10-si-explain
strand: si
grade: 10
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Explain
assumes: g9-si-explain
hands_on: g11-si-explain
model_in: cause chain
model_out: cause chain
words: I decided on; it results in; contributes to; I went with; since
cross_subject: ela:e10-sl-seminar (class discussion rules and turns)
notes: Students explain a choice they made on a project to a teacher and to peers, choosing words for each listener. Entry points (all explain a choice they made on a project to a teacher and to peers, choosing words for each listener): levels 1 and 2 with the choice on a cause chain and two audience cards, a word bank and the cloze "I chose ___ because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "I decided on ___ because it results in ___." for the teacher and "I went with ___ since ___." for peers, off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, naming what they changed between listeners, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: one explanation told for two listeners; cause verbs (results in, contributes to) and hedging; decided on, went with and since. Home language: a student may talk the choice through in the home language first; the teacher names the cognates decisión and decision, contribuir and contribute. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 10 the same explanation is shaped for two listeners, so word choice is the target; the grade 9 seminar cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g10-si-argue
strand: si
grade: 10
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Argue
assumes: g9-si-argue
hands_on: g11-si-argue
model_in: claim chart
model_out: claim chart
words: the source suggests; most of us agree; still open; clearly; although
cross_subject: ela:e10-sl-seminar (class discussion rules and turns)
notes: Students hold a seminar on a text with several sources, citing reasons and summing up points of agreement. Entry points (all hold a seminar on a text with several sources, citing reasons and summing up points of agreement): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of the sources, a word bank and the cloze "Source ___ says ___. I agree because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Although source A ___, source B ___. Most of us agree that ___.", off before the end-of-unit seminar; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, summing up points of agreement and the point still open, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a seminar turn that cites a source and sums up agreement; concession and boosting modals (clearly, must); most of us agree, the source suggests and still open. Home language: a student may read a source's summary in the home language first if the school has one; fuente and source are not cognates, so the word card carries it. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 10 the seminar draws on several sources, so citing and summing up are new; the grade 9 "That changes my view" frame is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g10-la-narrate
strand: la
grade: 10
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Narrate.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Narrate.Expressive
assumes: g9-la-narrate
hands_on: g11-la-narrate
model_in: story map
model_out: story map
words: in contrast; creates; effect; tone; voice
cross_subject: ela:e10-w-narrative (narrative writing); ela:e10-rl-structure-time (reading literature, same text)
notes: Students take apart one passage from each of two ELA unit narratives: how structure and voice differ, and the tone words that mark the voice. They then build one scene that uses structure for an effect, with the teacher first and then alone. The full comparison and narrative stay in ELA. Entry points (all compare structure and voice in two ELA narratives and write a scene that uses structure for an effect): levels 1 and 2 with both passages on a story map, a word bank and the cloze "Text 1 starts ___. Text 2 starts ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "In contrast to ___, ___ begins with ___, which creates ___.", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, naming the effect they chose, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 9 tense-shift frame is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a scene whose structure creates an effect; tense shifts on purpose and tone words (bitter, hopeful); in contrast, creates and effect. Home language: a student may draft the scene in the home language first; efecto and effect, contraste and contrast are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 10 students compare texts and name an effect; in grade 12 the contrast cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed.

g10-la-inform
strand: la
grade: 10
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Inform.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Inform.Expressive
assumes: g9-la-inform
hands_on: g11-la-inform
model_in: text map
model_out: text map
words: both sources; synthesize; nonetheless; whereas; quotation
cross_subject: ela:e10-w-informative (informative writing); ela:e10-ri-unfolding-ideas (reading informational, same text); ela:e10-l-syntax-style (forms of English)
notes: Students take apart one passage from each of two ELA unit sources: how the writers order ideas, and the synthesis words that join them ("both", "whereas"). They then build one informative paragraph that synthesizes the two sources, with the teacher first and then alone. The full piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all trace how writers order ideas in two ELA sources and write a paragraph that synthesizes them): levels 1 and 2 with both sources on a text map, a word bank and the cloze "Both sources say ___. Source 1 ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with synthesis frames ("Whereas ___, ___. Both ___.") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, working a short quotation into a sentence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 9 clause frame is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a paragraph that synthesizes two sources; synthesis ("both sources", "whereas") and quotations worked into a sentence; synthesize, both sources and nonetheless. Home language: a student may compare the sources in the home language first; sintetizar and synthesize are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 10 synthesis of two sources is added; in grade 12 the synthesis cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g10-la-argue
strand: la
grade: 10
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Argue.Expressive
assumes: g9-la-argue
hands_on: g11-la-argue
model_in: claim chart
model_out: claim chart
words: rhetoric; matters more; while it is true; rebuttal; weigh
cross_subject: ela:e10-w-argument (opinion or argument writing); ela:e10-ri-argument-evaluate (reading reasons and evidence)
notes: Students take apart one paragraph of the ELA unit text and mark its rhetoric: the evaluative words and the rebuttal moves. They then build one argument paragraph that weighs two counterclaims, with the teacher first and then alone. The full argument stays in ELA. Entry points (all evaluate rhetoric in an ELA text and write a paragraph that weighs two counterclaims): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of both counterclaims, a word bank and the cloze "One view is ___. Another view is ___. I think ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with rebuttal frames ("While ___ is true, ___ matters more because ___.") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, choosing tone for the audience, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 9 concession cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument paragraph that weighs two counterclaims; concession and rebuttal moves with evaluative words; matters more, rhetoric and while it is true. Home language: a student may weigh the counterclaims in the home language first; retórica and rhetoric are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 10 rhetoric and weighed counterclaims are added; in grade 12 the rebuttal cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g10-ma-explain
strand: ma
grade: 10
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Explain.Expressive
assumes: g9-ma-explain
hands_on: g11-ma-explain
model_in: cause chain
model_out: cause chain
words: right triangle; sine; is modeled by; assuming; density; results in
cross_subject: math:geo-solving-right-triangles (right triangles); math:geo-modeling-objects-density (modeling objects)
notes: Students explain how they solved right-triangle problems and modeled real objects in Geometry. Entry points (all explain how they solved a right-triangle problem and modeled a real object, in Geometry): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled diagram, a word bank and the cloze "I used ___ because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Assuming ___, the object is modeled by ___, which results in ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the model only, explaining a modeling choice so a partner could use it, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 9 "this step" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of a modeling choice and a solution; dense noun groups and cause verbs with "assuming"; is modeled by, sine and density. Home language: a student may talk through the model in the home language first; densidad and density, triángulo rectángulo and right triangle are close. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 10 explanations of modeling choices are added; in grade 12 the "I used" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g10-ma-argue
strand: ma
grade: 10
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Argue.Expressive
assumes: g9-ma-argue
hands_on: g11-ma-argue
model_in: claim chart
model_out: claim chart
words: given; prove; it follows that; definition; although; rebuttal
cross_subject: math:geo-triangle-congruence-criteria (congruence proofs); math:geo-parallelogram-proofs (parallelogram proofs)
notes: Students write and present triangle congruence and parallelogram proofs, critiquing a partner's proof. Entry points (all write and present a triangle congruence or parallelogram proof, critiquing a partner's proof): levels 1 and 2 with the proof diagram and a two-column outline, a word bank and the cloze "Given ___, so ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Since ___, it follows that ___. Although ___, ___." for the critique, off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, critiquing a partner's proof with definitions, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 9 "does not prove" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a formal proof with given, statements, reasons and conclusion; concession and rebuttal for the critique; given, it follows that and definition. Home language: a student may plan the proof in the home language first; definición and definition are cognates, while demostrar and prove are not, so the word card carries prove. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 10 formal proof is the target; in grade 12 the "given, so" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g10-sc-explain
strand: sc
grade: 10
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Explain.Expressive
assumes: g9-sc-explain
hands_on: g11-sc-explain
model_in: cause chain
model_out: cause chain
words: contributes to; periodic; reaction rate; collisions; results in; suggests
cross_subject: science:chem-atomic-structure-periodic-patterns (host investigation); science:chem-reaction-rates (host investigation)
notes: Students explain periodic patterns from atomic structure and what changes reaction rates, in Chemistry. Entry points (all explain periodic patterns from atomic structure and what changes reaction rates, in Chemistry): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled particle model, a word bank and the cloze "When ___ goes up, the rate ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "More frequent collisions contribute to ___, which results in ___.", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining at the particle level, citing sources and naming a limit, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 9 "codes for" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a particle-level explanation of a pattern; dense noun groups ("the rate of the reaction") and cause verbs (contributes to, results in); periodic, collisions and suggests. Home language: a student may explain the model in the home language first; periódico and periodic, colisión and collision are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 10 particle-level explanations are the target; in grade 12 the rate cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g10-sc-argue
strand: sc
grade: 10
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Argue.Expressive
assumes: g9-sc-argue
hands_on: g11-sc-argue
model_in: claim chart
model_out: claim chart
words: feedback; equilibrium; clearly; is limited because; although; rebuttal
cross_subject: science:chem-earth-feedbacks (host investigation); science:chem-thermal-equilibrium (host investigation)
notes: Students argue about Earth's feedbacks and thermal equilibrium from data, in Chemistry. Entry points (all argue about Earth's feedbacks and thermal equilibrium from data, in Chemistry): levels 1 and 2 with the data on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "The data clearly shows ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Although ___, the data shows ___. However, it is limited because ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, weighing the limits of the data and answering a rebuttal, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 9 "evidence suggests" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument that weighs the limits of its data; concession and boosting modals (clearly, must); feedback, equilibrium and is limited because. Home language: a student may weigh the data in the home language first; equilibrio and equilibrium are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 10 claims weigh limits of data; in grade 12 the "clearly shows" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g10-ss-explain
strand: ss
grade: 10
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Explain.Expressive
assumes: g9-ss-explain
hands_on: g11-ss-explain
model_in: cause chain
model_out: cause chain
words: industrialization; resulted in; imperialism; over time; contributed to; may
cross_subject: social-studies:wh2-industrial-revolution (host unit); social-studies:wh2-imperialism-causes (host unit)
notes: Students explain the causes and effects of the Industrial Revolution and of imperialism, in World History II. Entry points (all explain the causes and effects of the Industrial Revolution and of imperialism, weighing causes over time): levels 1 and 2 with the causes on the cause chain, a word bank and the cloze "Industrialization resulted in ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Over time, ___ contributed to ___, which resulted in ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, weighing causes over time, citing sources and naming a limit of the evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 9 "contributed to" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation that weighs causes over time; nominalization (industrialization, imperialism) and cause verbs (resulted in); over time, contributed to and may. Home language: a student may sort the causes in the home language first; industrialización and industrialization, imperialismo and imperialism are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 10 explanations weigh causes over time; in grade 12 the "resulted in" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: the imperialism host is about colonial rule, which some families lived under; the family note previews it and offers the Industrial Revolution host, and no student is asked to speak for a country or a people.

g10-ss-argue
strand: ss
grade: 10
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Argue.Expressive
assumes: g9-ss-argue
hands_on: g11-ss-argue
model_in: claim chart
model_out: claim chart
words: historians disagree; weigh; the evidence suggests; although; thesis; persuasive
cross_subject: social-studies:wh2-research-argument (host unit); social-studies:wh2-historians-interpretations (host unit)
notes: Students argue a research claim and weigh historians' interpretations, in World History II. Entry points (all argue a research claim and weigh historians' interpretations, in World History II): levels 1 and 2 with the interpretations on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "Historians disagree about ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Although ___ argues ___, the evidence suggests ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, weighing interpretations and naming a limit of the evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 9 thesis cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a research argument that weighs interpretations; concession, rebuttal moves and evaluative words; historians disagree, weigh and the evidence suggests. Home language: a student may read a source's summary in the home language first if the school has one; the teacher names the false friend that can trip Spanish speakers (eventualmente means possibly, not eventually). Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 10 weighing interpretations is added; in grade 12 the "historians disagree" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g11-si-narrate
strand: si
grade: 11
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Narrate
assumes: g10-si-narrate
hands_on: g12-si-narrate
model_in: story map
model_out: story map
words: the situation was; my role was to; as a result; I learned that; one time
cross_subject: ela:e11-seminar (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e11-presentation (presenting in order to an audience)
notes: Students recount an experience for an interview-style question (a time you worked on a team), shaping the order for the listener. Entry points (all recount an experience for an interview-style question, a time you worked on a team, shaping the order for the listener): levels 1 and 2 with the story map in three parts (situation, action, result), a word bank and the cloze "One time, our team ___. I ___. In the end ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "The situation was ___. My role was to ___. As a result, ___.", off before the end-of-unit recount; 5 and 6 with the story map only, choosing which detail to lead with for this listener, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an interview recount in three parts (situation, action, result); tense shifts on purpose and reflective language ("I learned that"); my role was, as a result and one time. Home language: a student may plan the recount in the home language first; the teacher names the cognates situación and situation, resultado and result. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 11 students switch on purpose between formal and informal registers, and the interview question sets the order; the grade 10 club frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed. Teacher note: informal English and the home language are not wrong; the task is choosing for the listener. Teacher note: a student may speak as an invented applicant; no task asks about documents, aid eligibility or a family's situation.

g11-si-inform
strand: si
grade: 11
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Inform
assumes: g10-si-inform
hands_on: g12-si-inform
model_in: text map
model_out: text map
words: I would like to ask about; Could you tell me; appointment; I am calling about; thank you for
cross_subject: ela:e11-seminar (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e11-presentation (telling and describing aloud)
notes: Students request and give information in formal settings (a counselor meeting, a phone call to an office), practicing both registers. Entry points (all request and give information in a formal setting, a counselor meeting or a phone call to an office, and then in an informal one): levels 1 and 2 with a model call to copy and change, a word bank and the cloze "Hello, I am calling about ___. Could you tell me ___?", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "I would like to ask about ___. Could you tell me ___? Thank you for ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with no frame, making the same request once to an office and once to a friend, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a formal request with greeting, reason, question and thanks; polite modals (could, would) and "I am calling about"; appointment, I would like to ask and thank you for. Home language: a student may script the call in the home language first; the teacher names the false friend that can trip Spanish speakers (realizar means carry out, not realize). Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 11 the information is asked of adults outside class, so polite modals and a set call order are new; the grade 10 briefing frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: informal English and the home language are not wrong; the task is choosing for the listener. Teacher note: a student may speak as an invented applicant; no task asks about documents, aid eligibility or a family's situation.

g11-si-explain
strand: si
grade: 11
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Explain
assumes: g10-si-explain
hands_on: g12-si-explain
model_in: cause chain
model_out: cause chain
words: the first step is; which allows; depends on; deadline; for example
cross_subject: ela:e11-seminar (class discussion rules and turns)
notes: Students explain a process (applying for a program, a school procedure) to a younger student and to an adult. Entry points (all explain a process, applying for a program or a school procedure, to a younger student and to an adult): levels 1 and 2 with the process on a cause chain, a word bank and the cloze "First you ___. Then ___, so ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "The first step is to ___, which allows ___." for the adult and "First you ___" for the younger student, off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, explaining one step that depends on another, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a process explained for two listeners; dense noun groups for the adult ("the application deadline for the summer program") and cause verbs (allows, depends on); the first step is, which allows and depends on. Home language: a student may outline the process in the home language first; the teacher names that a form one applies with is usually solicitud in Spanish, while aplicación often means an app. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 11 the process is explained up and down in age, so register moves both ways; the grade 10 project frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: informal English and the home language are not wrong; the task is choosing for the listener. Teacher note: a student may speak as an invented applicant; no task asks about documents, aid eligibility or a family's situation.

g11-si-argue
strand: si
grade: 11
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Argue
assumes: g10-si-argue
hands_on: g12-si-argue
model_in: claim chart
model_out: claim chart
words: we are asking for; we understand that; in return; however; objection
cross_subject: ela:e11-seminar (class discussion rules and turns)
notes: Students make a case to an adult (for a class change, a club request) and answer objections, in both formal and informal registers. Entry points (all make a case to an adult, for a class change or a club request, and answer objections, in a formal and an informal register): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of reasons and likely objections, a word bank and the cloze "We are asking for ___ because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "We understand that ___. However, ___." and an objection card, off before the end-of-unit case; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering an objection they did not plan for, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a request case with claim, reasons, an answered objection and an ask; concession and rebuttal moves with hedging and boosting modals; we are asking for, we understand that and in return. Home language: a student may plan the case in the home language first; the teacher names the cognate objeción and objection. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 11 the listener is an adult who can say no, so answering objections in a formal register is new; the grade 10 seminar frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: informal English and the home language are not wrong; the task is choosing for the listener.

g11-la-narrate
strand: la
grade: 11
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Narrate.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Narrate.Expressive
assumes: g10-la-narrate
hands_on: g12-la-narrate
model_in: story map
model_out: story map
words: looking back; I realize; at first I thought; reflection; structure
cross_subject: ela:e11-narrative (narrative writing); ela:e11-lit-structure (reading literature, same text)
notes: Students take apart one passage of the ELA unit's novel or story where the author makes a structural choice, and the reflective language that carries it. They then build a reflective narrative scene, a personal or invented account, with a chosen structure, with the teacher first and then alone. The full narrative stays in ELA. Entry points (all analyze an author's structural choice and write a reflective scene with a chosen structure): levels 1 and 2 with the story map of the passage, a word bank and the cloze "At first I thought ___. Now I see ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with reflective frames ("Looking back, I realize ___.") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, naming why they chose that structure, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 contrast cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a reflective scene with a chosen structure; tense shifts on purpose and reflective language ("looking back, I realize"); I realize, at first I thought and reflection. Home language: a student may draft the reflection in the home language first; reflexión and reflection are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 reflective narrative and an author's choices are added; in grade 12 the reflective cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed.

g11-la-inform
strand: la
grade: 11
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Inform.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Inform.Expressive
assumes: g10-la-inform
hands_on: g12-la-inform
model_in: text map
model_out: text map
words: most sources agree; one source adds; for a reader; reader; dense
cross_subject: ela:e11-informative (informative writing); ela:e11-info-structure (reading informational, same text); ela:e11-syntax-effect (forms of English)
notes: Students take apart one passage of the ELA unit text: how its structure makes a point clear, and how dense noun groups pack information. They then build an informative paragraph that synthesizes several sources for a named reader, with the teacher first and then alone. The full piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all analyze how an ELA text's structure makes points clear and write a paragraph that synthesizes several sources for a named reader): levels 1 and 2 with the sources on a text map, a word bank and the cloze "Most sources agree that ___. One source adds ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with synthesis and reader frames ("For a reader new to ___, ___.") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, choosing the structure for the named reader, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 synthesis cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a synthesis paragraph for a named reader; nominalization and quotations worked into a sentence; most sources agree, for a reader and in addition. Home language: a student may group the sources in the home language first; fuente and source are not cognates, so the word card carries it. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 several sources and a named reader are added; in grade 12 the reader frame is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g11-la-argue
strand: la
grade: 11
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Argue.Expressive
assumes: g10-la-argue
hands_on: g12-la-argue
model_in: claim chart
model_out: claim chart
words: it is clear that; audience; tone; clearly; must
cross_subject: ela:e11-argument (opinion or argument writing); ela:e11-info-rhetoric (reading reasons and evidence)
notes: Students take apart one paragraph of a speech or essay from the ELA unit: how its rhetoric reaches its audience, and how hedging and boosting modals set the tone. They then build one argument paragraph for a chosen audience, adjusting tone, with the teacher first and then alone. The full argument stays in ELA. Entry points (all analyze rhetoric in an ELA speech or essay and write a paragraph for a chosen audience, adjusting tone): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart and an audience card, a word bank and the cloze "For ___, I would say ___ because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with hedging and boosting frames ("It is clear that ___. This may ___.") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, writing the same claim for two audiences, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 rebuttal cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument paragraph for a chosen audience; hedging and boosting modals (may, clearly, must) and evaluative words; it is clear that, audience and tone. Home language: a student may plan the audience shift in the home language first; audiencia and audience are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 audience and tone are added; in grade 12 the hedging cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g11-ma-explain
strand: ma
grade: 11
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Explain.Expressive
assumes: g10-ma-explain
hands_on: g12-ma-explain
model_in: cause chain
model_out: cause chain
words: function model; periodic; amplitude; the model suggests; may; results in
cross_subject: math:alg2-combining-functions-models (function models); math:alg2-periodic-modeling (periodic models)
notes: Students explain how they built a function model and a periodic model from data in Algebra II. Entry points (all explain how they built a function model and a periodic model from data, in Algebra II): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled graph and data, a word bank and the cloze "The amplitude shows ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "The model may ___, which suggests ___. The period results in ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the model only, explaining the choice of model and its limits, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 "I used" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of a model choice and its limits; dense noun groups and hedging (may, suggests); amplitude, period and the model suggests. Home language: a student may talk through the model in the home language first; amplitud and amplitude, periódico and periodic are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 students explain model choices and their limits; in grade 12 the amplitude cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g11-ma-argue
strand: ma
grade: 11
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Argue.Expressive
assumes: g10-ma-argue
hands_on: g12-ma-argue
model_in: claim chart
model_out: claim chart
words: significant; margin of error; might; the evidence suggests; limitation; although
cross_subject: math:alg2-experiments-significance (significance claims); math:alg2-sample-estimates-margin (margin of error)
notes: Students argue whether a result is significant and what a margin of error allows, from study data. Entry points (all argue whether a result is significant and what a margin of error allows, from study data): levels 1 and 2 with the study data on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "The result is significant because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "The evidence suggests ___, although the margin of error ___. This might ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, critiquing another claim about the same study, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 "given, so" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument about uncertainty with its limits; hedging modals (might, may) and concession; significant, margin of error and limitation. Home language: a student may weigh the study in the home language first; significativo and significant, margen de error and margin of error are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 claims about uncertainty are added; in grade 12 the significance cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g11-sc-explain
strand: sc
grade: 11
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Explain.Expressive
assumes: g10-sc-explain
hands_on: g12-sc-explain
model_in: cause chain
model_out: cause chain
words: orbit; is proportional to; signal; contributes to; may; model
cross_subject: science:phys-orbits (host investigation); science:phys-wave-technologies (host investigation)
notes: Students explain orbits and how wave technologies carry information, in Physics. Entry points (all explain orbits and how wave technologies carry information, in Physics): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled model, a word bank and the cloze "The force of gravity ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "The force is proportional to ___, which contributes to ___.", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, using a mathematical model and naming a limit of it, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 rate cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation with a mathematical model; dense noun groups and cause verbs with hedging; orbit, signal and is proportional to. Home language: a student may explain the model in the home language first; órbita and orbit, señal and signal share a root but look different, so the card carries both. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 explanations use mathematical models; in grade 12 the gravity cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g11-sc-argue
strand: sc
grade: 11
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Argue.Expressive
assumes: g10-sc-argue
hands_on: g12-sc-argue
model_in: claim chart
model_out: claim chart
words: reliable; published claim; rebuttal; might; although; limitation
cross_subject: science:phys-wave-particle-light (host investigation); science:phys-em-radiation-effects (judging published claims)
notes: Students argue whether light is a wave or a particle and whether a published claim about radiation's effects holds up, in Physics, answering a rebuttal. Entry points (all argue whether light is a wave or a particle, or whether a published claim about radiation's effects holds up, answering a rebuttal): levels 1 and 2 with the evidence on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "The claim is (not) reliable because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Although ___ might ___, the evidence shows ___." and a rebuttal frame, off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering a rebuttal and naming a limit of the evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 "clearly shows" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument that judges a claim and answers a rebuttal; hedging and boosting modals with rebuttal moves; reliable, published claim and rebuttal. Home language: a student may weigh the claim in the home language first; the teacher names the false friend that can trip Spanish speakers (actual means current, not actual). Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 rebuttal is added; in grade 12 the "reliable" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g11-ss-explain
strand: ss
grade: 11
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Explain.Expressive
assumes: g10-ss-explain
hands_on: g12-ss-explain
model_in: cause chain
model_out: cause chain
words: sectional; tension; continuity; resulted in; over time; while
cross_subject: social-studies:us1-market-revolution (host unit); social-studies:us1-sectional-crisis (host unit)
notes: Students explain the market revolution and the causes of the sectional crisis, in US History I. Entry points (all explain the market revolution and the causes of the sectional crisis, tracing continuity and change): levels 1 and 2 with the causes on the cause chain, a word bank and the cloze "Tension grew because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "While ___ stayed the same, ___ changed, which resulted in ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, weighing causes, citing sources and naming a limit of the evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 "resulted in" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of continuity and change; dense noun groups and cause verbs with hedging; continuity, tension and resulted in. Home language: a student may sort the causes in the home language first; continuidad and continuity, tensión and tension are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 explanations trace continuity and change; in grade 12 the tension cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: the sectional crisis host is about slavery and the coming war; the family note previews it and offers the market revolution host, slavery is never role-played, and no student is asked to speak for a group.

g11-ss-argue
strand: ss
grade: 11
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Argue.Expressive
assumes: g10-ss-argue
hands_on: g12-ss-argue
model_in: claim chart
model_out: claim chart
words: reliable; news source; bias; claims; corroborate; although
cross_subject: social-studies:us1-argument-writing (host unit); social-studies:us1-news-reading (host unit)
notes: Students write a historical argument and judge a news source's claims, in US History I. Entry points (all write a historical argument and judge a news source's claims, in US History I): levels 1 and 2 with the source on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "The source claims ___. It is (not) reliable because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Although ___ claims ___, other sources show ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, corroborating sources and naming a limit of the evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 "historians disagree" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument that judges a source's claims; concession, reporting verbs and hedging; reliable, bias and corroborate. Home language: a student may read news in the home language too and compare how outlets report a story, by choice; fiable and reliable are not cognates, so the word card carries it. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 judging claims in the news is added; in grade 12 the "reliable" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: the news host may touch immigration enforcement; pick pieces on other topics, or offer one of several.

g12-si-narrate
strand: si
grade: 12
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Narrate
assumes: g11-si-narrate
model_in: story map
model_out: story map
words: at the time; I was responsible for; as a result; I learned; looking back
cross_subject: ela:e12-seminar (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e12-presentation (presenting in order to an audience)
notes: Students recount an experience for a college, job or program interview, then retell it in an email, choosing register for each. Entry points (all recount an experience for a college, job or program interview, then retell it in an email, choosing the register for each): levels 1 and 2 with the story map in three parts, a model answer to change, a word bank and the cloze "At the time, I ___. I was responsible for ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "At the time, ___. I was responsible for ___. As a result, ___. Looking back, ___." for the interview and a model email to change, off before the end-of-unit recount; 5 and 6 with the story map only, writing the email from their spoken answer, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: an interview answer and an email of the same experience; tense shifts on purpose and reflective language ("looking back"); at the time, I was responsible for and I learned. Home language: a student may plan the answer in the home language first; the interviewer and the email reader get English. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 12 the audiences are adults outside class, so the same recount must work aloud and in writing; the grade 11 situation-action-result cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed. Teacher note: a student may speak as an invented applicant; no task asks about documents, aid eligibility or a family's situation.

g12-si-inform
strand: si
grade: 12
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Inform
assumes: g11-si-inform
model_in: text map
model_out: text map
words: I am writing to ask; regarding; would it be possible; I would appreciate; please let me know
cross_subject: ela:e12-seminar (class discussion rules and turns); ela:e12-presentation (telling and describing aloud)
notes: Students write and say information for adult settings (an email to a teacher, a request to an office, an orientation for younger students). Entry points (all write a short request to a school office and then say it aloud): levels 1 and 2 with a model message to copy and change, a bank of request phrases and a rehearsal with the teacher, the copying off before the check and the phrase bank kept; 3 and 4 with frames for greeting, request and thanks ("I am writing to ask about ___. Could you ___?"), off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with no frame, writing the same request once for a classmate and once for an office. Forms: a short formal message with greeting, request, detail and thanks; polite modals and nominalization ("regarding the change in schedule"); regarding, would it be possible and please let me know. Home language: a student may draft the request in the home language first and then write it in English; no student writes or translates a message for a family. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 12 the audiences are adults outside class, so a written request joins the spoken one; the grade 11 call script is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: a student may speak as an invented applicant; no task asks about documents, aid eligibility or a family's situation.

g12-si-explain
strand: si
grade: 12
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Explain
assumes: g11-si-explain
model_in: cause chain
model_out: cause chain
words: my plan is to; the main reason; in order to; one option is; so that
cross_subject: ela:e12-seminar (class discussion rules and turns)
notes: Students explain a plan, their own or an invented student's, for a project, a program or the year after high school, and the reasons for it, in talk and in a short written statement. Entry points (all explain a plan and the reasons for it, aloud and then in a short written statement): levels 1 and 2 with the plan on a cause chain, a word bank and the cloze "My plan is to ___ so that ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "My plan is to ___. The main reason is ___. In order to ___, I will ___.", off before the end-of-unit statement; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, weighing one option against another in writing, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a plan statement with the goal, the steps and the reasons; purpose clauses ("in order to", "so that") and hedging (may, might); my plan is to, one option is and the main reason. Home language: a student may plan in the home language first; the teacher names the cognates opción and option, razón and reason. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 12 the audiences are adults outside class, so the plan is spoken and then written; the grade 11 two-listener frame is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: a student may speak as an invented applicant; no task asks about documents, aid eligibility or a family's situation.

g12-si-argue
strand: si
grade: 12
standard: ~WIDA.ELD-SI.4-12.Argue
assumes: g11-si-argue
model_in: claim chart
model_out: claim chart
words: I propose; I understand that; however; that is a fair point, but; I still believe
cross_subject: ela:e12-seminar (class discussion rules and turns)
notes: Students lead a discussion and argue for a proposal in a school or community setting, conceding a point and holding their main claim. Entry points (all lead a discussion and argue for a proposal in a school or community setting, conceding a point and holding their main claim): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart of the proposal, a model opening to change, a word bank and the cloze "I propose ___ because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "I propose ___. I understand that ___; however, ___. I still believe ___.", off before the end-of-unit discussion; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, conceding a fair point aloud and holding the claim, the frame gone after the first application day. Forms: a proposal with claim, reasons, a conceded point and a close; concession and rebuttal ("that is a fair point, but") with evaluative words; I propose, I understand that and I still believe. Home language: a student may plan the proposal in the home language first; the teacher names the cognate proponer and propose. Carried, owned in grade 4. In grade 12 students lead in a school or community setting, so conceding without giving up the claim is the target; the grade 11 objection card is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g12-la-narrate
strand: la
grade: 12
standard: WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Narrate.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Narrate.Expressive
assumes: g11-la-narrate
model_in: story map
model_out: story map
words: turning point; I came to see; reader; revise; resolution
cross_subject: ela:e12-narrative (narrative writing); ela:e12-lit-structure (reading literature, same text)
notes: Students take apart one passage of a text from the ELA unit where structure and tone build meaning. They then write a polished narrative for a real reader, a personal statement or a story on a topic the student chooses, real or invented, revising voice for that reader, with the teacher first and then alone. The full piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all analyze how structure and tone build meaning in an ELA text and write a polished narrative for a real reader): levels 1 and 2 with the story map, a model passage to change, a word bank and the cloze "It began when ___. That was the turning point.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with turning-point and reflective frames off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, revising voice for the named reader, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 11 reflective cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a polished narrative with a structure and tone chosen for a reader; tense shifts on purpose, reflective language and tone words; turning point, I came to see and resolution. Model: story map at the 9 to 12 stage, structure across time. Home language: a student may draft in the home language first, and a story may carry words from it on purpose, as published writers do. Owner year for LA 9-12. Both modes for 9-12: analyzing complex narrative structure and voice, and writing narrative with structure and tone chosen for a reader. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed. Teacher note: no student is asked or nudged to write about migration, loss or hardship.

g12-la-inform
strand: la
grade: 12
standard: WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Inform.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Inform.Expressive
assumes: g11-la-inform
model_in: text map
model_out: text map
words: refers to; defined as; formal register; synthesize; whereas
cross_subject: ela:e12-informative (informative writing); ela:e12-info-structure (reading informational, same text); ela:e12-syntax-effect (forms of English)
notes: Students take apart one section of a complex ELA unit text: how it defines a term and how its formal register works. They then build one informative section that defines terms, synthesizes sources and keeps a formal register, with the teacher first and then alone. The full report stays in ELA. Entry points (all evaluate a complex ELA text's structure and write a formal section that defines terms and synthesizes sources): levels 1 and 2 with the text map of the sources, a word bank and the cloze "___ refers to ___. Both sources ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with definition and synthesis frames ("___, defined here as ___, ___.") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, controlling a complex structure for a named reader, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 11 reader frame is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a formal section that defines, synthesizes and concludes; synthesis, nominalization and quotations worked into a sentence; refers to, defined as and formal register. Model: text map at the 9 to 12 stage, two or more sources synthesized. Home language: a student may outline in the home language first; the teacher names Latin-root cognates common in formal register (definir and define, sintetizar and synthesize). Owner year for LA 9-12. Both modes for 9-12: analyzing complex informational structure and writing synthesis in a formal register.

g12-la-argue
strand: la
grade: 12
standard: WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Argue.Expressive
assumes: g11-la-argue
model_in: claim chart
model_out: claim chart
words: granted; nevertheless; admittedly; rebuttal; concede
cross_subject: ela:e12-argument (opinion or argument writing); ela:e12-info-rhetoric (reading reasons and evidence)
notes: Students take apart one paragraph of a seminal speech or essay from the ELA unit: how it rebuts and concedes. They then build one argument paragraph with a rebuttal and a concession for a real audience, with the teacher first and then alone. The full argument stays in ELA. Entry points (all analyze how a seminal ELA speech persuades and write a paragraph with a rebuttal and a concession for a real audience): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "Granted, ___. Still, ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with concession and rebuttal frames ("Admittedly, ___; nevertheless, ___.") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, naming the limits of their own evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 11 hedging cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument paragraph with a concession and a rebuttal; concession ("Although ___, ___"), hedging and boosting modals and rebuttal moves; granted, nevertheless and admittedly. Model: claim chart at the 9 to 12 stage, rebuttal and the limits of the evidence. Home language: a student may plan the rebuttal in the home language first; the teacher names the cognate concesión and concession. Owner year for LA 9-12. Both modes for 9-12: analyzing rhetoric and writing argument with rebuttal for an audience.

g12-ma-explain
strand: ma
grade: 12
standard: WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Explain.Expressive
assumes: g11-ma-explain
model_in: cause chain
model_out: cause chain
words: method; in order to; rate of change; may suggest; the first step is; results in
cross_subject: math:stat-investigation-project (investigation explained); math:calc-related-rates (related rates explained, Calculus); math:pc-trig-equations (solutions explained, Precalculus); math:alg2-combining-functions-models (if no grade 12 math course)
notes: Students explain a method in the grade 12 math course they take: a statistical investigation (question, data, method, conclusion) in Statistics, a related rates solution in Calculus, or the solutions of a trigonometric equation in Precalculus; a student in no grade 12 math course uses the Algebra II modeling row. Entry points (all explain a method from their own grade 12 math course, step by step, so another student could follow it): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled work and a model explanation to change, a word bank and the cloze "In order to ___, I ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "In order to ___, the first step is ___, which results in ___. This may ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with no frame, explaining the method so a partner could use it on a new problem, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 11 amplitude cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a method explanation from goal to conclusion; dense noun groups, cause verbs (results in) and hedging (may suggest); in order to, rate of change and method. Model: cause chain at the 9 to 12 stage, weighted causes (which step matters most to the result). Home language: a student may talk through the method in the home language first; método and method, tasa de cambio and rate of change are close. Owner year for MA 9-12. Both modes for 9-12: following and giving explanations of methods and models.

g12-ma-argue
strand: ma
grade: 12
standard: WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Argue.Expressive
assumes: g11-ma-argue
model_in: claim chart
model_out: claim chart
words: conclude; assumption; strong evidence; although; might; justified
cross_subject: math:stat-test-proportion (test conclusions); math:calc-mean-value-extrema (justified extrema, Calculus); math:pc-exp-log-models (which model fits, Precalculus); math:alg2-experiments-significance (if no grade 12 math course)
notes: Students argue a conclusion in the grade 12 math course they take: from a statistical test or an expected-value decision in Statistics, a justified extreme value in Calculus, or which model fits data and how far it can be trusted in Precalculus; a student in no grade 12 math course uses the Algebra II significance row. Entry points (all argue a conclusion from their own grade 12 math course and critique another conclusion): levels 1 and 2 with the work on the claim chart and a model conclusion to change, a word bank and the cloze "We conclude ___ because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Although ___, the evidence is strong that ___. One limitation is ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, critiquing another conclusion and naming the assumptions behind it, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 11 significance cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argued conclusion with its assumptions and limits; concession, hedging and boosting modals; conclude, assumption and strong evidence. Model: claim chart at the 9 to 12 stage, rebuttal and the limits of the evidence. Home language: a student may weigh the conclusion in the home language first; concluir and conclude, suposición and assumption are close. Owner year for MA 9-12. Both modes for 9-12: making and critiquing arguments with assumptions and evidence.

g12-sc-explain
strand: sc
grade: 12
standard: WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Explain.Expressive
assumes: g11-sc-explain
model_in: cause chain
model_out: cause chain
words: circulation; is driven by; contributes to; system; may; water quality
cross_subject: science:ess-ocean-circulation (host investigation); science:env-water-quality (host investigation); science:phys-orbits (if no grade 12 science course)
notes: Students explain ocean circulation or water quality in the grade 12 science course; a student in no grade 12 science course uses the grade 11 Physics row. Entry points (all explain ocean circulation or water quality in the grade 12 science course, or orbits in the grade 11 Physics row for a student in no grade 12 science course): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled system model, a word bank and the cloze "The current is driven by ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "___ is driven by ___, which contributes to ___. This may ___.", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, weighing several causes, citing sources and naming a limit, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 11 gravity cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of a complex system with weighted causes; dense noun groups and cause verbs (is driven by, contributes to) with hedging; circulation, system and may. Model: cause chain at the 9 to 12 stage, weighted, multi-factor causes. Home language: a student may explain the model in the home language first; circulación and circulation, sistema and system are cognates. Owner year for SC 9-12. Both modes for 9-12: following and giving explanations of complex systems.

g12-sc-argue
strand: sc
grade: 12
standard: WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Argue.Expressive
assumes: g11-sc-argue
model_in: claim chart
model_out: claim chart
words: trade-off; uncertainty; clearly; although; limitation; might
cross_subject: science:env-energy-transition (host investigation); science:ess-climate-model-test (host investigation); science:phys-wave-particle-light (if no grade 12 science course)
notes: Students argue about energy choices or a climate model's test in the grade 12 science course; a student in no grade 12 science course uses the grade 11 Physics row. Entry points (all argue about an energy choice or a climate model's test, or about light in the grade 11 Physics row for a student in no grade 12 science course): levels 1 and 2 with the evidence on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "The best choice is ___ because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Although ___, the trade-off favors ___. One uncertainty is ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering a rebuttal and naming the limits of the evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 11 "reliable" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument with evidence, a trade-off, limits and a rebuttal; concession, hedging and boosting modals and evaluative words; trade-off, uncertainty and clearly. Model: claim chart at the 9 to 12 stage, rebuttal and the limits of the evidence. Home language: a student may weigh the choice in the home language first; incertidumbre is not a cognate of uncertainty, so the word card carries it. Owner year for SC 9-12. Both modes for 9-12: argument with evidence, limits and rebuttal.

g12-ss-explain
strand: ss
grade: 12
standard: WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Explain.Expressive
assumes: g11-ss-explain
model_in: cause chain
model_out: cause chain
words: supply; demand; price; results in; is limited by; checks and balances
cross_subject: social-studies:econ-markets-prices (host unit); social-studies:gov-checks-balances (host unit)
