Students take apart one passage of the ELA unit's novel or story where the author makes a structural choice, and the reflective language that carries it. They then build a reflective narrative scene, a personal or invented account, with a chosen structure, with the teacher first and then alone. The full narrative stays in ELA. Entry points (all analyze an author's structural choice and write a reflective scene with a chosen structure): levels 1 and 2 with the story map of the passage, a word bank and the cloze "At first I thought ___. Now I see ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with reflective frames ("Looking back, I realize ___.") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the story map only, naming why they chose that structure, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 contrast cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a reflective scene with a chosen structure; tense shifts on purpose and reflective language ("looking back, I realize"); I realize, at first I thought and reflection. Home language: a student may draft the reflection in the home language first; reflexión and reflection are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 reflective narrative and an author's choices are added; in grade 12 the reflective cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: prompts follow APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground; a home, family or where-you-are-from prompt is one of several, and an invented character or a chosen place is always allowed.

g11-la-inform | ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Inform.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Inform.Expressive | g10-la-inform -> g12-la-inform
text map -> text map | most sources agree; one source adds; for a reader; reader; dense | ela:e11-informative (informative writing); ela:e11-info-structure (reading informational, same text); ela:e11-syntax-effect (forms of English)
Students take apart one passage of the ELA unit text: how its structure makes a point clear, and how dense noun groups pack information. They then build an informative paragraph that synthesizes several sources for a named reader, with the teacher first and then alone. The full piece stays in ELA. Entry points (all analyze how an ELA text's structure makes points clear and write a paragraph that synthesizes several sources for a named reader): levels 1 and 2 with the sources on a text map, a word bank and the cloze "Most sources agree that ___. One source adds ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with synthesis and reader frames ("For a reader new to ___, ___.") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the text map only, choosing the structure for the named reader, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 synthesis cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: a synthesis paragraph for a named reader; nominalization and quotations worked into a sentence; most sources agree, for a reader and in addition. Home language: a student may group the sources in the home language first; fuente and source are not cognates, so the word card carries it. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 several sources and a named reader are added; in grade 12 the reader frame is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g11-la-argue | ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-LA.9-12.Argue.Expressive | g10-la-argue -> g12-la-argue
claim chart -> claim chart | it is clear that; audience; tone; clearly; must | ela:e11-argument (opinion or argument writing); ela:e11-info-rhetoric (reading reasons and evidence)
Students take apart one paragraph of a speech or essay from the ELA unit: how its rhetoric reaches its audience, and how hedging and boosting modals set the tone. They then build one argument paragraph for a chosen audience, adjusting tone, with the teacher first and then alone. The full argument stays in ELA. Entry points (all analyze rhetoric in an ELA speech or essay and write a paragraph for a chosen audience, adjusting tone): levels 1 and 2 with the claim chart and an audience card, a word bank and the cloze "For ___, I would say ___ because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with hedging and boosting frames ("It is clear that ___. This may ___.") off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, writing the same claim for two audiences, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 rebuttal cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument paragraph for a chosen audience; hedging and boosting modals (may, clearly, must) and evaluative words; it is clear that, audience and tone. Home language: a student may plan the audience shift in the home language first; audiencia and audience are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 audience and tone are added; in grade 12 the hedging cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g11-ma-explain | ~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Explain.Expressive | g10-ma-explain -> g12-ma-explain
cause chain -> cause chain | function model; periodic; amplitude; the model suggests; may; results in | math:alg2-combining-functions-models (function models); math:alg2-periodic-modeling (periodic models)
Students explain how they built a function model and a periodic model from data in Algebra II. Entry points (all explain how they built a function model and a periodic model from data, in Algebra II): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled graph and data, a word bank and the cloze "The amplitude shows ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "The model may ___, which suggests ___. The period results in ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the model only, explaining the choice of model and its limits, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 "I used" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of a model choice and its limits; dense noun groups and hedging (may, suggests); amplitude, period and the model suggests. Home language: a student may talk through the model in the home language first; amplitud and amplitude, periódico and periodic are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 students explain model choices and their limits; in grade 12 the amplitude cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g11-ma-argue | ~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-MA.9-12.Argue.Expressive | g10-ma-argue -> g12-ma-argue
claim chart -> claim chart | significant; margin of error; might; the evidence suggests; limitation; although | math:alg2-experiments-significance (significance claims); math:alg2-sample-estimates-margin (margin of error)
Students argue whether a result is significant and what a margin of error allows, from study data. Entry points (all argue whether a result is significant and what a margin of error allows, from study data): levels 1 and 2 with the study data on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "The result is significant because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "The evidence suggests ___, although the margin of error ___. This might ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, critiquing another claim about the same study, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 "given, so" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument about uncertainty with its limits; hedging modals (might, may) and concession; significant, margin of error and limitation. Home language: a student may weigh the study in the home language first; significativo and significant, margen de error and margin of error are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 claims about uncertainty are added; in grade 12 the significance cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g11-sc-explain | ~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Explain.Expressive | g10-sc-explain -> g12-sc-explain
cause chain -> cause chain | orbit; is proportional to; signal; contributes to; may; model | science:phys-orbits (host investigation); science:phys-wave-technologies (host investigation)
Students explain orbits and how wave technologies carry information, in Physics. Entry points (all explain orbits and how wave technologies carry information, in Physics): levels 1 and 2 with the labeled model, a word bank and the cloze "The force of gravity ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "The force is proportional to ___, which contributes to ___.", off before the end-of-unit writing task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, using a mathematical model and naming a limit of it, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 rate cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation with a mathematical model; dense noun groups and cause verbs with hedging; orbit, signal and is proportional to. Home language: a student may explain the model in the home language first; órbita and orbit, señal and signal share a root but look different, so the card carries both. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 explanations use mathematical models; in grade 12 the gravity cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g11-sc-argue | ~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SC.9-12.Argue.Expressive | g10-sc-argue -> g12-sc-argue
claim chart -> claim chart | reliable; published claim; rebuttal; might; although; limitation | science:phys-wave-particle-light (host investigation); science:phys-em-radiation-effects (judging published claims)
Students argue whether light is a wave or a particle and whether a published claim about radiation's effects holds up, in Physics, answering a rebuttal. Entry points (all argue whether light is a wave or a particle, or whether a published claim about radiation's effects holds up, answering a rebuttal): levels 1 and 2 with the evidence on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "The claim is (not) reliable because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Although ___ might ___, the evidence shows ___." and a rebuttal frame, off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, answering a rebuttal and naming a limit of the evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 "clearly shows" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument that judges a claim and answers a rebuttal; hedging and boosting modals with rebuttal moves; reliable, published claim and rebuttal. Home language: a student may weigh the claim in the home language first; the teacher names the false friend that can trip Spanish speakers (actual means current, not actual). Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 rebuttal is added; in grade 12 the "reliable" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4.

g11-ss-explain | ~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Explain.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Explain.Expressive | g10-ss-explain -> g12-ss-explain
cause chain -> cause chain | sectional; tension; continuity; resulted in; over time; while | social-studies:us1-market-revolution (host unit); social-studies:us1-sectional-crisis (host unit)
Students explain the market revolution and the causes of the sectional crisis, in US History I. Entry points (all explain the market revolution and the causes of the sectional crisis, tracing continuity and change): levels 1 and 2 with the causes on the cause chain, a word bank and the cloze "Tension grew because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "While ___ stayed the same, ___ changed, which resulted in ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the cause chain only, weighing causes, citing sources and naming a limit of the evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 "resulted in" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an explanation of continuity and change; dense noun groups and cause verbs with hedging; continuity, tension and resulted in. Home language: a student may sort the causes in the home language first; continuidad and continuity, tensión and tension are cognates. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 explanations trace continuity and change; in grade 12 the tension cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: the sectional crisis host is about slavery and the coming war; the family note previews it and offers the market revolution host, slavery is never role-played, and no student is asked to speak for a group.

g11-ss-argue | ~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Argue.Interpretive; ~WIDA.ELD-SS.9-12.Argue.Expressive | g10-ss-argue -> g12-ss-argue
claim chart -> claim chart | reliable; news source; bias; claims; corroborate; although | social-studies:us1-argument-writing (host unit); social-studies:us1-news-reading (host unit)
Students write a historical argument and judge a news source's claims, in US History I. Entry points (all write a historical argument and judge a news source's claims, in US History I): levels 1 and 2 with the source on the claim chart, a word bank and the cloze "The source claims ___. It is (not) reliable because ___.", the cloze off before the check and the word bank kept; 3 and 4 with "Although ___ claims ___, other sources show ___.", off before the end-of-unit task; 5 and 6 with the claim chart only, corroborating sources and naming a limit of the evidence, the frame gone after the first application day; the grade 10 "historians disagree" cloze is gone for 3 and 4. Forms: an argument that judges a source's claims; concession, reporting verbs and hedging; reliable, bias and corroborate. Home language: a student may read news in the home language too and compare how outlets report a story, by choice; fiable and reliable are not cognates, so the word card carries it. Carried, owned in grade 12. In grade 11 judging claims in the news is added; in grade 12 the "reliable" cloze is gone for levels 3 and 4. Teacher note: the news host may touch immigration enforcement; pick pieces on other topics, or offer one of several.

