1: # Course frame: English Language Development, grades 6 to 8
2: 
3: Course `english-language-development-grades-6-to-8` (catalogue id `eld-6-8`), grades 6, 7 and 8, a band course of
4: three grade years. Draft frame, written 2026-11-25 on the shape of the pilot frame (English Language Development,
5: grades K to 2) and its fixes; the scope and sequence sets the days later, one year at a time. Each year's part of
6: this file (its units, its end-of-unit assessments, its two course-level checks) is that grade year's frame, which
7: meets STANDARD.md section 2 ("one frame per grade year").
8: 
9: ## The course in brief
10: 
11: - **Units.** 26: 8 in grade 6 (units 1 to 8), 9 in grade 7 (units 9 to 17), 9 in grade 8 (units 18 to 26). One unit
12:   per content row of the progression (9 a year: LA Narrate, Inform and Argue; MA, SC and SS Explain and Argue),
13:   except that grade 6 pairs its two MA rows on one host (unit 8), because math grade 6 teaches equations and
14:   statistical questions in the year's last two months. The four SI rows of each year ride inside the content unit
15:   whose key language use they share. Each unit is built around one or two key language uses, in both modes. SI in
16:   every unit (bar item 1; STANDARD section 3): the unit's own SI row, or one SI use named as review in What students
17:   learn (units 4 to 8, 11, 14 to 17, 19, 22 and 24 to 26), each with its talk move on the host content and its
18:   look-for, chosen so that every key language use returns inside the year.
19: - **Days.** 480: 160 a year for three years. Each year: 154 days in units and 6 for two course-level checks (two
20:   check days and one respond day each). One lesson of 45 minutes a day, five a week, 32 weeks, the protected daily
21:   ELD period settled in STANDARD.md section 2 and APPROACH.md "Pacing" (120 hours a year). It is not ELA time and
22:   does not stand in for ELA (bar item 8). The 45 minutes hold the talk set-up, the roles of the year's talk routine,
23:   handing out and collecting cards and the exit ticket; there is no lab or field clean-up, since ELD runs no
24:   investigation. Each unit's days include its end-of-unit assessment (and, in units over 25 days, a mid-unit
25:   assessment): units 1 and 8 are over 25 days and each hold one mid-unit assessment day. Every unit's end-of-unit
26:   assessment is two days (a sitting with unseen items: an interpretive part on new content of the same kind, then a
27:   talk task heard by the teacher and a writing task, at each entry point), followed by one respond day inside the
28:   unit. Days follow the host windows first: each unit runs in the weeks its host is taught, and the two checks sit in
29:   fixed weeks, so a unit's length is mostly the gap between hosts. Within those windows the load sets the rest: an
30:   LA row (a host passage taken apart and an own piece built, in both modes) weighs most, a content row next, an SI
31:   row least, since it rides on the same unit's talk. Five units are tight for what they hold and say what they leave
32:   to the respond day: units 15, 16 and 17 (13 days each, grade 7's last three hosts all run in its last seven weeks)
33:   and units 25 and 26 (13 days each, grade 8's editorial and natural selection hosts both run in its last eight
34:   weeks). Each keeps its Fluency days to the forms it owns and leaves review forms to the respond day's groups; the
35:   scope and sequence may move days within a year, never across a check. Years run in school order: grade 6 is band
36:   days 1 to 160, grade 7 band days 161 to 320, grade 8 band days 321 to 480; days below are given within the year
37:   (grade 7 day 88 is band day 248). These are course days: the school calendar (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md) places
38:   them on school days and weeks, and at five a week a year's course day d falls on school day d, in week d divided
39:   by 5, rounded up.
40: - **Standard.** English language development standards (WIDA 2020, data/standards/wida-2020.txt), ids and our own
41:   words only: no Language Expectation, proficiency level descriptor or other framework wording appears in any page
42:   (STANDARD.md section 1); public pages say "English language development standards" with the id. The band's 39
43:   rows own 18 ids, all in grade 8, the owner year of cluster 6-8: LA Narrate, Inform and Argue and MA, SC and SS
44:   Explain and Argue, each in both modes (cluster 6-8 has no MA, SC or SS Narrate or Inform id). Grades 6 and 7 carry
45:   the same 18 ids, and all three years carry the SI 4-12 ids (owner year grade 4, in the ELD grades 3 to 5 course;
46:   the owner rule of STANDARD section 2): each unit that carries one names it in What students learn with its owner.
47:   Ids in full: every part of an owned id's expectations is taught in its unit, checked against the framework's own
48:   lists for the id (not the code alone) and said in our words (each grade 8 unit names its parts; For the lead,
49:   item 2). Check set: bar part 10 items 1 to 14 (ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS), the PRIME 2020 criteria in our words
50:   (docs/RUBRIC-ELD.md, a planning reference only; Curio Class claims no alignment) and the California criteria map
51:   rows of STANDARD section 4. Values rule (DESK-ANSWERS 2026-11-12) holds.
52: - **Language through content.** Every unit names its host: the core unit of the same grade that the class studies
53:   that month (STANDARD section 3), cited with the host frame's own course days. The host column of the Units table
54:   (headed Host unit and key language uses, in place of C3) gives it. Where a row names two hosts, the unit uses the
55:   one that runs alongside it and retrieves the other where it was taught earlier; a host taught later in the year
56:   is not used, and the unit says so (units 1, 10, 11, 13 and 18). Some units run just after a host (unit 7 after
57:   science grade 6 unit 4, unit 8 after math grade 6 unit 6, unit 19 after math grade 8 unit 1) or a few days ahead
58:   (units 3, 5, 10 and 12, each with an "If later" line): these are the nearest fits that keep every row in host
59:   order with two checks a year, and the scope and sequence may move days within a year without changing units. Each
60:   host is checked against the host row's standard, not its title. Content is the grade's own, made reachable with
61:   help; it is never swapped for an easier text. For an LA unit the host is the ELA unit it pairs with (STANDARD
62:   section 5): ELD takes one passage of the ELA text apart and names its language, and the reading and writing
63:   lessons stay in ELA.
64: - **Key language uses as the spine.** Each year builds all four uses, and each returns with a harder text or task
65:   inside the year (bar item 13), through the unit's own row or its SI review use; what gets harder is named in that
66:   unit. Grade 6: narrate 1 to 4 (a first-week recount by class period grows to the week's weather told from the
67:   class's own data log, keeping one point of view), inform 1 to 5 and 7 (a routine in sections grows to a section
68:   with a heading and a definition, then to a hazard briefing), explain 3 to 4 and 8 (a cause chain of the Nile flood
69:   grows to a mechanism with an unseen step, then to a solving method a partner can use), argue 2 to 6, 7 and 8 (one
70:   claim with cited evidence grows to two interpretations weighed, then to a counterclaim from data and a
71:   counterexample). Grade 7: narrate 9 to 11 and 17 (a lab recount for a teammate grows to how the group solved a
72:   percent problem, then to a sequence of events at Rome's end), inform 10 to 15 (a classifying paragraph grows to a
73:   group's plan summed up for another group), explain 11 to 12, 16 and 17 (what each part of an equation means grows
74:   to a procedure with a reason for each step, then to two causes weighed), argue 13 to 14, 15 and 16 (a counterclaim
75:   answered grows to a research claim, then to claims from data and conditions). Grade 8: inform 18 to 19 and 26 (a
76:   formal section with varied transitions grows to a briefing on a proof's steps, then to a mechanism summary),
77:   argue 19 to 20, 21, 22 and 25 (a congruence claim grows to a counterclaim from a credible source, then to an
78:   editorial answered), explain 21 to 22, 24 and 26 (a group conflict and its solution grows to why a check on power
79:   works, then to a mechanism tied to evidence), narrate 23 to 25 (a scene with a flashback grows to a newsletter
80:   recount for readers who were not there).
81: - **Every unit, the same moves** (bar items 9 to 12; APPROACH, "Across the unit"): the key language use taught
82:   openly from a model taken apart, built with the teacher, then made alone; forms taught directly at the text, the
83:   sentence and the word (each form named with its job); structured talk every day with frames, before any writing;
84:   both modes (interpretive: listening, reading and viewing; expressive: speaking and writing, with drawing and
85:   labeled models as a step); one change of audience or purpose; one moment where students name a form and its job
86:   and judge their own language on a self-check page they keep; the host-teacher note (bar item 14: the content used
87:   and the language taught, two lines).
88: - **The talk routine.** One named routine a year, the same every week, each growing from the one before: grade 6
89:   "role circle" (chair, timekeeper, note taker and reporter, the roles turning each week), grade 7 "build or
90:   question" (each turn builds on or questions a named speaker's reason), grade 8 "student-led round" (a student
91:   opens, invites each view, challenges one reason respectfully and sums up). A grade 7 or 8 class that never had
92:   the earlier years learns the roles in the first week of its first unit (9 or 18).
93: - **Entry points.** Three, by proficiency (STANDARD section 2): levels 1 and 2, 3 and 4, 5 and 6. Every entry point
94:   does the same task on the same content at the same thinking demand; levels change the help, never the task (bar
95:   item 2). Help is withdrawn on the progression's default days: for 5 and 6 the frame comes off after the first
96:   application day; for 3 and 4 before the end-of-unit writing or talk task; 1 and 2 keep the word bank and lose the
97:   cloze before the check. Each year also drops one frame the year before kept for 3 and 4 (the rows' "is gone for 3
98:   and 4" lines). A student moves to the next entry point when the teacher's paper sheet shows the unit's look-fors
99:   "seen" in the end-of-unit assessment and in one course-level check, or on the school's own progress check (bar
100:   item 7). Placement and exit use the school's proficiency score; Curio Class gives no placement test and keeps no
101:   scores. Every unit has notes for newcomers, long-term English learners and English learners with disabilities,
102:   and a line on telling a language need from a learning need (APPROACH, "Sensitive ground": a gap in English is
103:   never read as a disability). Long-term English learners are common in middle school: many speak English easily
104:   and need the academic forms, so their note in each unit points to the written forms and the change of audience,
105:   never to beginner talk tasks, and treats them as the capable speakers they are.
106: - **Newcomer strand.** A 20-day on-ramp beside each year, outside its 160 days (STANDARD section 2; Desk
107:   q-fix-eld-1): every year works the SI 4-12 ids and WIDA.ELD-LA.6-8.Inform only, since cluster 6-8 has no MA, SC
108:   or SS Inform id. Its content is the school day itself (a schedule with several teachers, lockers and passing time,
109:   asking for help) and the current host unit's pictures and labeled models. A newcomer who needs early reading in
110:   English works the ELA K to 2 foundational rows beside it, at an age-fit pace and with age-fit texts. A newcomer
111:   arriving at any point takes it, then joins the class's current unit at entry points 1 and 2. The strand is written
112:   with the year's scope and sequence and gets its own sensitivity read before use.
113: - **Day kinds.** As docs/GLOSSARY.md (row "teaching day") and APPROACH.md "Kinds of day in ELD": Understanding is
114:   the first teaching of a form, a text's structure or a model (the form shown in the host text, named, explained);
115:   Fluency practices forms already taught (talk rehearsal with the frame, sentence combining, word work and cognate
116:   sorts) and drafts nothing; Application uses the forms in talk and then in writing on the content (every day that
117:   drafts or revises is Application); Check is an end-of-unit assessment, mid-unit assessment or course-level check
118:   day; Respond reteaches the forms the sheet shows "not yet". Fluency is a smaller share than in K to 2 (about a
119:   fifth of teaching days), since grades 6 to 8 move more of the practice into writing.
120: - **Readiness and retrieval.** Grade 6 picks up the grade 5 rows of the ELD grades 3 to 5 course. Every row grades
121:   7 and 8 assume was taught the year before in this band (grade 7 assumes grade 6, grade 8 assumes grade 7), and
122:   grade 6's assumes were taught in grade 5, so each opens as a retrieval task inside a day of the unit that first
123:   needs it (day 2, after day 1's hook), in the same words as sequence-write rule 2; no unit needs a readiness check.
124:   The count starts from the student's own grade year. A student who joins without the earlier year meets the
125:   retrieval task at entry points 1 and 2 with the earlier year's model on the card, and a newcomer takes the strand
126:   first. A host's earlier unit is retrieved the same way (named in each unit). A mixed-grade group uses the year of
127:   its youngest grade, and the teacher page says so (STANDARD section 2).
128: - **Base text.** None of its own (docs/reviews/eld-open-material-2026-11-16.md: no open ELD course exists). Texts are
129:   the host units' own: the ELA grades 6 to 8 courses' texts, credited through those courses, and the science,
130:   social studies and math units' Curio Class pages, data tables, records and labeled models. ELD adapts EL
131:   Education's Language Dive routine (a sentence from the text taken apart and rebuilt; CC BY 4.0), credited once a
132:   day where used. Curio Class writes the scaffolds (word banks, cause and claim cards, the frames per level, the
133:   model page each unit builds with the teacher) and each end-of-unit assessment's new content of the same kind,
134:   Curio Class's own and invented for practice, named per unit. No source map: ELD places no base units.
135: - **Paper path.** Every lesson runs on paper, cards and talk. Any tool a unit names (a model-sentence replay tool:
136:   hear and replay the day's model sentence; a word deck) has a paper path beside it (the teacher reads the model
137:   aloud, the cards on the table) and needs no student account. No tool records a student's voice or writing, scores
138:   pronunciation or places a student at a level (APPROACH, "What a tool may and may not do").
139: - **Devices.** None per student. One shared display is optional for the model page, the host's data tables and the
140:   replay tool. No owned code needs technology: models are drawn and labeled on paper, so a paper-path class meets
141:   every id in full.
142: - **Costs.** Printing only: word banks, role and question cards and the four model sheets, reused across a year,
143:   about $4 a unit for a class of 30 (a planning figure, not a quote); no supplies are bought.
144: - **Home languages.** A help, never a ranking (bar item 6): planning and talk in the home language before English,
145:   cognates named (including hidden cognates, which share a root but look different), false friends flagged
146:   (asistir means attend, molestar means bother), and teacher notes on how Spanish does the same thing (other
147:   languages when a reader exists, APPROACH "Home languages"). No student translates for another, for the class or
148:   for a family. Family notes are in English and Spanish.
149: - **Safety.** ELD runs no investigation and no field work. Students talk and write from the host's own data,
150:   records and models; where a teacher repeats a moment from the host (a cart rolled down a ramp, a magnet brought
151:   near a clip), it is the host's own step under the host unit's hazards and controls (the host frame's safety sheet
152:   and its safety read), never a new test, and nothing is heated, tasted or mixed.
153: - **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md,
154:   kind `sensitivity`, community: multilingual learners and immigrant families), and every Spanish note and family
155:   note gets a `spanish` read (APPROACH, "Sensitive ground", Cory 2026-11-21). Before reading, the agent studies the
156:   APPROACH page's Sensitive ground rules, the federal English learner toolkit (US Department of Education, Office of
157:   English Language Acquisition, free online, https://www.ncela.ed.gov/educator-support/toolkits/english-learner-toolkit),
158:   the National Academies 2017 report on children learning English (free to read online) and, for the units on
159:   floods, hazards, war and leaving home, the National Child Traumatic Stress Network guide for educators of children
160:   of war (free online, https://www.nctsn.org/sites/default/files/resources//children_of_war_guide_for_educators.pdf).
161:   Flagged units name more below: units 1 and 7 (a flood and a choice about moving; natural hazards), unit 2
162:   (punishments and slavery in an old law code), unit 9 (Karuk and Yurok river life), unit 12 (food and the body),
163:   unit 17 (invasion and the end of a state), unit 18 (loss in wartime and a parent living apart), units 20 and 24
164:   (slavery at the founding; Native nations and state power), unit 23 (a myth of seizure and a child shared between
165:   two homes), units 24 and 25 (civics and voting, never a question about status or papers), unit 26 (natural
166:   selection, beliefs respected). The newcomer strand and all family notes get the read before use. No outside human
167:   reader is planned (Cory, 2026-11-14); the course's checks get the answer-key agent's read.
168: 
